window.CV_DATA={"questions":[{"id":"cv001","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An image was loaded with default cv2.imread. Which conversion matches its channel order?","options":["cv2.COLOR_RGB2GRAY","cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY","cv2.COLOR_GRAY2BGR","Channel order has no effect on grayscale conversion"],"correct":1,"answer":"cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY","explanation":"Default color imread returns BGR. RGB2GRAY applies the red and blue coefficients to the wrong input channels."},{"id":"cv002","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The sample uses cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (4, 4), 0). What correction matches this call?","options":["Use (5, 5), keeping sigmaX=0","Use (4, 4) and sigmaX=-1","Change gray to RGB and keep (4, 4)","Swap width and height"],"correct":0,"answer":"Use (5, 5), keeping sigmaX=0","explanation":"The explicitly supplied positive Gaussian kernel dimensions must be odd. With a valid odd size, sigmaX=0 lets OpenCV infer sigma from the kernel size."},{"id":"cv003","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A color image has NumPy shape (480, 640, 3). After cv2.resize(img, (320, 240)), what is its shape?","options":["(320, 240, 3)","(240, 320, 3)","(240, 320)","(480, 640, 1)"],"correct":1,"answer":"(240, 320, 3)","explanation":"OpenCV dsize uses (width,height), whereas a NumPy image uses (height,width,channels). Resize retains three channels here."},{"id":"cv004","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"After BGR2GRAY on a three-channel image, which statement about the output is precise?","options":["Its usual NumPy shape is (H,W), representing one grayscale channel","It has three identical channels","It has zero channels","Its width becomes one third of the input width"],"correct":0,"answer":"Its usual NumPy shape is (H,W), representing one grayscale channel","explanation":"The usual OpenCV grayscale array is two-dimensional (H,W). This represents one intensity channel even though shape has no explicit final channel axis."},{"id":"cv005","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can Gaussian smoothing before Canny reduce spurious edges?","options":["It increases every gradient magnitude","It reduces rapid noise fluctuations that derivative filters amplify","It learns class labels","It makes all true boundaries stronger"],"correct":1,"answer":"It reduces rapid noise fluctuations that derivative filters amplify","explanation":"Noise can create abrupt pixel changes. Smoothing reduces these fluctuations, but also weakens small real details, so blur strength is a tradeoff."},{"id":"cv006","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A disease label depends on yellow discoloration. What is the strongest objection to feeding only a Canny edge map to the classifier?","options":["The image is always too large","Edges discard color information that may distinguish the classes","Canny produces learned features","Edge maps cannot ever be classified"],"correct":1,"answer":"Edges discard color information that may distinguish the classes","explanation":"An edge map mainly captures intensity boundaries. It can remove discriminative color and texture. Compare alternatives using validation data."},{"id":"cv007","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A leaf dataset contains narrow disease streaks. Stronger blur raises training accuracy but lowers validation accuracy. What is a justified conclusion?","options":["More preprocessing always helps","Strong blur may remove useful streak detail; choose strength using validation","Keep increasing blur until the images look clean","Select blur using the test set repeatedly"],"correct":1,"answer":"Strong blur may remove useful streak detail; choose strength using validation","explanation":"Visually clean images need not preserve the evidence a classifier needs. The validation result supports testing weaker smoothing or a different denoiser."},{"id":"cv008","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"cv2.imread returns None for a missing image. What should the pipeline do before color conversion?","options":["Treat None as a black image silently","Check img is None and raise or handle the loading failure","Normalize None by dividing by 255","Run cv2.Canny(None,100,200)"],"correct":1,"answer":"Check img is None and raise or handle the loading failure","explanation":"Failing at image loading gives a useful diagnosis. Otherwise later OpenCV assertions obscure the original problem."},{"id":"cv009","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You need all 640×480 images in a 256×256 tensor while retaining the whole image and its aspect ratio. Which operation is appropriate?","options":["Stretch to 256×256","Resize to 256×192 and pad vertically","Crop the middle 256×256 region without resizing","Keep the original dimensions"],"correct":1,"answer":"Resize to 256×192 and pad vertically","explanation":"Scaling by 256/640=0.4 gives height192. Add64 pixels of vertical padding. Stretching distorts geometry; cropping can remove relevant content."},{"id":"cv010","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample Q1a/Q5c, derived aspect-preserving preprocessing exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 640×480 image is aspect-preservingly resized to width 256, then centred in a 256×256 canvas. How many padding pixels go on EACH of the top and bottom? Assume exact equal padding.","value":32,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"32 pixels per side","explanation":"The resized height is 480×256/640=192. The total vertical padding is 256−192=64, split equally into 32 and 32."},{"id":"cv011","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Szeliski §3.5.1; application to categorical image masks","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You resize a binary segmentation mask whose values are class IDs. Which interpolation best avoids invented fractional labels?","options":["Bilinear interpolation","Bicubic interpolation","Nearest-neighbor interpolation","Any interpolation gives the same labels"],"correct":2,"answer":"Nearest-neighbor interpolation","explanation":"Nearest neighbor selects an existing label rather than averaging IDs. RGB images and categorical masks need different interpolation choices."},{"id":"cv012","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which statement correctly describes the two Canny thresholds?","options":["They are the minimum and maximum pixel brightness allowed","The upper threshold establishes strong gradient edges; connected intermediate edges can survive hysteresis","They are class scores","The lower threshold must be twice the upper threshold"],"correct":1,"answer":"The upper threshold establishes strong gradient edges; connected intermediate edges can survive hysteresis","explanation":"Canny applies hysteresis to edge-gradient evidence. An intermediate candidate needs a connection to a strong edge, rather than merely falling within a brightness interval."},{"id":"cv013","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why should training and inference use a compatible preprocessing pipeline?","options":["Because every camera generates identical pixels","A mismatch can change the feature distribution seen by the model","Because test images must be included in training","Because augmentation must be random during every inference"],"correct":1,"answer":"A mismatch can change the feature distribution seen by the model","explanation":"For example, a model trained on normalized RGB may fail when inference sends unnormalized BGR. Reuse required deterministic transforms; training augmentation need not be applied at inference."},{"id":"cv014","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"What does dividing a float image by 255 accomplish when the original pixel values are in [0,255]?","options":["It learns the dataset mean","It rescales values to [0,1]","It removes all lighting variation","It equalizes the histogram"],"correct":1,"answer":"It rescales values to [0,1]","explanation":"This is a fixed rescaling, with no statistics fitted from data. It changes numeric scale but does not by itself remove illumination differences."},{"id":"cv015","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 5","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Correct the two errors in the sample snippet and add a clear image-loading check: imread(\"leaf.jpg\"); cvtColor(img,COLOR_RGB2GRAY); GaussianBlur(gray,(4,4),0).","answer":"img = cv2.imread(\"leaf.jpg\")\nif img is None:\n    raise FileNotFoundError(\"leaf.jpg\")\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)","explanation":"The requested errors are channel order and the even Gaussian kernel. A loading check is an additional robustness improvement. Other valid positive odd kernels can earn credit if justified."},{"id":"cv016","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 5","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Propose three preprocessing choices for inconsistent resolution, lighting, and noise. Explain the benefit and one risk of each.","answer":"Resize with a consistent aspect-ratio policy to standardize input geometry; check that small details survive. Normalize or adjust illumination where appropriate; preserve diagnostic color. Use modest denoising suited to the noise; avoid erasing fine structures.","explanation":"A strong answer matches each operation to a specific problem. It also acknowledges preprocessing tradeoffs and evaluates choices with validation data."},{"id":"cv017","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5; derived evaluation safeguard","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Near-duplicate images of the same object appear in both training and test sets. What is the main risk?","options":["The test score can be optimistic because the model has effectively seen the object","The model must underfit","Accuracy becomes mathematically undefined","Only the file extensions matter"],"correct":0,"answer":"The test score can be optimistic because the model has effectively seen the object","explanation":"A test set should represent new examples at the intended deployment level. Split by object, person, capture session or other dependency group when needed."},{"id":"cv018","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Lecture 2, validation and cross-validation; sample Q5","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You tune the blur size and learning rate. Which set should guide those choices?","options":["Training accuracy only","A validation split or training-set cross-validation","The final test set after every trial","All images before the split"],"correct":1,"answer":"A validation split or training-set cross-validation","explanation":"Use validation for choices. Keep the final test set for estimating the performance of the chosen pipeline, without repeatedly adapting to its labels."},{"id":"cv019","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample, Question 5","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For 2,000 images, an 80% training / 20% test split gives how many test images?","value":400,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"400 test images","explanation":"0.20×2,000=400; the remaining 1,600 are training. If hyperparameters are tuned, also reserve validation data or use cross-validation."},{"id":"cv020","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample, Question 5","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A stratified 70% training / 15% validation / 15% test split of 2,000 images gives how many training images?","value":1400,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"1,400 training images","explanation":"0.70×2,000=1,400; validation and test each contain 300. Preserve class balance approximately and keep dependency groups together."},{"id":"cv021","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5d, extended imbalanced-evaluation exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An inspection dataset contains 950 normal and 50 defective images. Predicting normal for every image gives 95% accuracy. What does this hide?","options":["The classifier has 100% defect recall","The classifier finds none of the defective items","The labels are necessarily wrong","The model is well calibrated"],"correct":1,"answer":"The classifier finds none of the defective items","explanation":"All50 defects become false negatives. Overall accuracy is dominated by the normal class, so report class-sensitive metrics and the confusion matrix."},{"id":"cv022","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5d, extended metric calculation","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For the defective class, TP=30 and FN=20. What is recall as a percentage? Enter a value on a 0–100 scale.","value":60,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"60% recall","explanation":"Recall=TP/(TP+FN)=30/(30+20)=0.6=60%. Recall asks how many actual defects were detected."},{"id":"cv023","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5d, extended metric calculation","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For the defective class, TP=30 and FP=10. What is precision as a percentage? Enter a value on a 0–100 scale.","value":75,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"75% precision","explanation":"Precision=TP/(TP+FP)=30/40=0.75=75%. Precision asks how many predicted defects were genuine."},{"id":"cv024","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q2e","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A model scores 95% training accuracy and 62% test accuracy. Which interpretation is appropriately cautious?","options":["It proves the test labels are wrong","It suggests overfitting; also check split validity and distribution differences","It guarantees that doubling depth will fix the problem","It implies perfect calibration"],"correct":1,"answer":"It suggests overfitting; also check split validity and distribution differences","explanation":"A large gap commonly suggests overfitting, but test difficulty, domain shift and evaluation mistakes can also contribute. Investigate before selecting a remedy."},{"id":"cv025","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5e","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Phone A images classify well, but phone B images classify poorly. What is a plausible explanation?","options":["BGR and RGB are guaranteed identical on all phones","Different color response, lighting or capture conditions cause domain shift","The model cannot classify images with pixels","The test size alone proves overfitting"],"correct":1,"answer":"Different color response, lighting or capture conditions cause domain shift","explanation":"The relationship between training images and deployment images matters. Camera processing, background, resolution and lighting can change the distribution."},{"id":"cv026","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5; Lecture 5, training and augmentation","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You add augmented copies before splitting, and copies of one original land in both training and test. What should change?","options":["Create the split first, then augment only training examples","Augment the test set repeatedly until accuracy improves","Duplicate every validation image","Remove all training data"],"correct":0,"answer":"Create the split first, then augment only training examples","explanation":"Related copies leak information across the split. Group the originals first, split them, and generate training augmentation afterward."},{"id":"cv027","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Lecture 2, split principles; sample Q5","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which normalization procedure avoids using validation and test statistics during training?","options":["Compute mean/std on all images, then split","Fit mean/std on training images and apply those fixed values elsewhere","Fit a separate model using test labels","Change normalization after each test result"],"correct":1,"answer":"Fit mean/std on training images and apply those fixed values elsewhere","explanation":"Learned statistics belong to the training process. Use training-fitted values consistently for validation, test, and inference."},{"id":"cv028","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample Q5d, extended metric exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Class F1 scores for Plastic, Paper, Glass, Other are 0.8, 0.6, 0.4, 0.2. What is macro-F1?","options":["0.8","0.5","0.2","It equals accuracy for every dataset"],"correct":1,"answer":"0.5","explanation":"Macro-F1 averages the four class F1 values: (0.8+0.6+0.4+0.2)/4=0.5. It does not weight classes by their frequency."},{"id":"cv029","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 5","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Design a defensible waste-classification dataset and evaluation procedure for 2,000 images across four classes.","answer":"Keep labels consistent; document sources and class counts; deduplicate and split related captures together. Use stratified 70/15/15 if groups allow:1,400 train,300 validation,300 test. Apply fixed resizing/normalization and label-preserving training augmentation. Tune on validation, evaluate the final model once on test, and inspect class-wise metrics/confusion matrix.","explanation":"An 80/20 split with validation inside training can also be valid. A useful answer gives counts and explains how the split measures generalization rather than just naming percentages."},{"id":"cv030","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Original sample, Questions 1 and 5","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A dataset associates every glass item with a blue background and every plastic item with a red background. Explain why a high random-test accuracy may fail in deployment.","answer":"The classifier may learn background shortcuts instead of material cues. A random split preserves the same association, so the test set may not expose the problem. Capture varied backgrounds for each class and evaluate on a separate capture environment.","explanation":"The goal is to test the intended signal under deployment conditions. More data from the same confounded setup may reinforce the shortcut."},{"id":"nn001","topic":"Learning concepts","source":"Trask Ch. 2, printed pp. 14–19","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You double the number of training images while keeping input resolution, layer widths and output classes fixed. How do the stored model sizes of a fixed MLP and a kNN classifier normally change?","answer":"MLP parameter count stays fixed; kNN stores more training examples.","explanation":"A fixed architecture has a fixed number of learned weights. A basic kNN system retains the examples it compares against, so its storage grows with the dataset.","options":["Both double because both see twice as many examples.","MLP parameter count stays fixed; kNN stores more training examples.","MLP parameter count doubles; kNN storage stays fixed.","Neither can store additional information."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn002","topic":"Learning concepts","source":"Trask Ch. 2, printed pp. 12–17; Lecture 4 slide 17","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A system groups unlabelled images by their visual features. A second system predicts object categories from images paired with known categories. Which description correctly separates their learning signals?","answer":"Grouping seeks structure without category labels; category prediction compares outputs with supplied labels.","explanation":"Supervision concerns the presence of target information, while parametric versus nonparametric concerns how the model is represented. These are different axes.","options":["Both are supervised because images are inputs.","Grouping uses category targets; category prediction uses no targets.","Grouping seeks structure without category labels; category prediction compares outputs with supplied labels.","The first must be nonparametric and the second must be parametric."],"correct":2},{"id":"nn003","topic":"Learning concepts","source":"Trask Ch. 2, printed pp. 15–16; Ch. 4 printed pp. 48–49","kind":"short","difficulty":"core","prompt":"A program repeatedly runs prediction = input * weight on labelled examples, but never compares predictions with targets or modifies weight. Explain precisely why this is inference rather than learning.","answer":"The program evaluates a fixed function. Learning requires an error or other learning signal and a rule that changes the model parameters in response.","explanation":"Repeating a forward pass does not itself update the stored knowledge. The predict–compare–learn sequence separates computation of a prediction from adaptation of the weights."},{"id":"nn004","topic":"Learning concepts","source":"Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 26–27; Lecture 4 slides 18–19","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A feedforward model receives only the current image. The developer expects its next prediction to depend on the previous image because the model was called previously. What is missing?","answer":"An explicit history input or persistent state mechanism.","explanation":"An ordinary feedforward invocation uses its current input and fixed parameters. Learned parameters persist, but a sequence of previous inference inputs is not automatically stored as recurrent state.","options":["A larger learning rate.","An explicit history input or persistent state mechanism.","A second output class.","A positive value for every weight."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn005","topic":"Learning concepts","source":"Trask Ch. 2, printed pp. 15–19; Lecture 4 slide 21","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A network achieves zero training error on a small dataset. Give two reasons that this alone does not establish useful generalization.","answer":"It may fit noise or memorise training examples, and the evaluation distribution may differ from the training distribution. Performance needs to be measured on held-out data that was not used for fitting or selecting the model.","explanation":"Optimising the empirical training objective measures fit to observed examples. It does not prove performance on unseen inputs or robustness to a changed distribution."},{"id":"nn006","topic":"Forward prediction","source":"Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 28–32","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For inputs x = [8.5, 0.65, 1.2] and weights w = [0.1, 0.2, 0], compute the scalar prediction w·x. No bias or activation is used.","answer":"0.98","explanation":"The contributions are 0.85, 0.13 and 0. The final prediction is 0.98; input magnitude and weight both determine a contribution.","value":0.98,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn007","topic":"Forward prediction","source":"Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 38–46; Lecture 3 slide 11","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A batch X uses rows for examples and has shape 7×4. Each of three output units has four weights stored as rows of W, so W is 3×4. Which forward expression and output shape are correct?","answer":"XWᵀ, shape 7×3","explanation":"The contracted feature dimensions must match: (7×4)(4×3) gives 7×3. The column-vector convention for one example is Wx; row-major batches use XWᵀ.","options":["XW, shape 7×3","WX, shape 3×7","XWᵀ, shape 7×3","XᵀW, shape 4×3"],"correct":2},{"id":"nn008","topic":"Forward prediction","source":"Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 27–34; Lecture 3 slides 70–74","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A weighted sum produces 1.4 for one class. Which interpretation is mathematically justified before any probability transformation?","answer":"A raw score whose scale is not constrained to [0,1].","explanation":"A linear weighted sum can be any real number. Sigmoid or softmax constrains output ranges for probabilistic modelling, and calibration still needs separate evidence.","options":["A calibrated probability of 140%.","A raw score whose scale is not constrained to [0,1].","An impossible neural-network output.","A certainty that the class occurs."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn009","topic":"Forward prediction","source":"Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 31–34","kind":"short","difficulty":"core","prompt":"You permute the feature order in every input vector. What must happen to the weight vector to preserve exactly the same prediction, and why?","answer":"Apply the same permutation to the weights. Each feature must remain paired with its corresponding coefficient; the sum of the same products is unchanged.","explanation":"The positions have semantic meaning. Shuffling only the inputs or only the weights changes which products are computed, even though their individual values stay the same."},{"id":"nn010","topic":"Forward prediction","source":"Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 42–46; Lecture 4 slide 12","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use column vectors and no activation or biases: x = [2,−1]ᵀ, W₁ = [[1,2],[0,3]], W₂ = [[2,1]]. Compute W₂(W₁x).","answer":"-3","explanation":"The hidden vector is [0,−3]ᵀ, and the output is −3. The composed weight row W₂W₁ is [2,7], giving the same result.","steps":["W₁x = [0, −3]ᵀ.","W₂[0, −3]ᵀ = 2·0 + 1·(−3) = −3."],"value":-3,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn011","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 8–12; Lecture 5 slide 13","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"A CIFAR image has 32×32×3 values. A fully connected linear classifier predicts 10 scores and includes one bias per score. How many trainable parameters does it have?","answer":"30730","explanation":"There are 3072 inputs, 10 rows of 3072 weights, and 10 biases: 30,720 + 10 = 30,730.","value":30730,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn012","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slide 16","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For a 10-class classifier with 3072 input features, the bias trick appends a constant 1 to x. What shape must the augmented weight matrix have?","answer":"10×3073, with b in the last column","explanation":"The augmented input has 3073 entries. [W b][x;1] = Wx+b, so each class row receives one additional coefficient.","options":["10×3073, with b in the last column","3073×10, with b in the last row under the column-vector convention","11×3072, with b as another class","10×3072, because the constant adds no column"],"correct":0},{"id":"nn013","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 13–15","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Using the lecture example x = [56,231,24,2]ᵀ, the dog row w = [1.5,1.3,2.1,0] and bias b = 3.2, compute the dog score.","answer":"437.9","explanation":"Compute 84 + 300.3 + 50.4 + 0 + 3.2 = 437.9. A score is a weighted template response, not a probability.","value":437.9,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn014","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 25–29","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A binary boundary is 2x₁ − x₂ + 1 = 0. Which direction is normal to the boundary, pointing toward increasing score 2x₁ − x₂ + 1?","answer":"(2,−1)","explanation":"The score gradient with respect to x is (2,−1). The bias moves the hyperplane but does not change its normal direction.","options":["(1,2)","(2,−1)","(−1,−2)","(1,1)"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn015","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 17–18, which explicitly ignore bias","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Let f(x)=Wx+b with b≠0. Which identity holds when the input is multiplied by scalar c?","answer":"f(cx)=cf(x)+(1−c)b","explanation":"W(cx)+b = cWx+b = c(Wx+b)+(1−c)b. The simple scaling identity in the lecture assumes the bias is absent.","options":["f(cx)=cf(x) for every c","f(cx)=cf(x)+(1−c)b","f(cx)=f(x)+c","f(cx)=cW(x+b)"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn016","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 17–18","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For a bias-free classifier with a unique highest score, multiplying an input by c>0 has what effect on predicted class and score magnitudes?","answer":"Class is unchanged and all scores are multiplied by c.","explanation":"Positive scaling preserves the ordering of the scores. Negative scaling reverses their order; nonzero biases can also invalidate this invariance.","options":["Class is unchanged and all scores are multiplied by c.","Class is unchanged and all scores remain identical.","Class must change because the image values change.","Scores are multiplied by c²."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn017","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 30–32; Lecture 4 slides 4,12","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can a single linear binary classifier not represent XOR on points (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1)? State the geometric obstruction.","answer":"The two positive points occupy opposite corners of the square, and the two negative points occupy the other opposite corners. No single line can put both positives on one side and both negatives on the other.","explanation":"A weighted sum with a threshold creates one half-space. Nonlinear features or a hidden layer with a nonlinear activation can create several regions."},{"id":"nn018","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slide 30","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A class contains points in an annulus 1≤√(x₁²+x₂²)≤2 and the other class contains all remaining points. Why is one affine binary score insufficient?","answer":"Its decision region is a half-space with a straight boundary, while the annulus needs inner and outer curved boundaries.","explanation":"Adding a bias only translates a hyperplane. Features such as squared radius can expose the radial structure, but the band still requires two thresholds or a suitable nonlinear classifier."},{"id":"nn019","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 13–14; Lecture 5 slides 4,14 (wording clarification)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"What is the accurate limitation of flattening an image before a fully connected layer?","answer":"A known reshape preserves all pixel values, but the FC layer does not enforce local connectivity or shared weights.","explanation":"A fixed flattening order is invertible. The modelling issue is the lack of an architectural preference for neighbouring pixels and repeated patterns at different positions.","options":["Flattening irreversibly deletes the original pixel indices.","Flattening makes RGB values impossible to reconstruct.","A known reshape preserves all pixel values, but the FC layer does not enforce local connectivity or shared weights.","Flattening alone makes the classifier nonlinear."],"correct":2},{"id":"nn020","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 9–12,70–74","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A classifier outputs scores [−4,2,1]. Which class is predicted using argmax, and what would applying softmax do to this class choice?","answer":"Second class; softmax preserves the highest-scoring class.","explanation":"Exponentiation is strictly increasing and all probabilities share one denominator, so softmax preserves score ordering. It does not turn the model into a nonlinear decision rule in the original input space.","options":["First class; softmax may switch it.","Second class; softmax preserves the highest-scoring class.","Third class; softmax always chooses the last class.","No class, because a score is negative."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn021","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 25–29","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Classes A and B have affine scores w_A·x+b_A and w_B·x+b_B. Write the exact equation of their equal-score boundary.","answer":"(w_A−w_B)·x + (b_A−b_B) = 0.","explanation":"The classification boundary compares the two scores, so it uses their difference. The zero-score plane for A alone need not be a decision boundary."},{"id":"nn022","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3 slides 11–12; Trask Ch. 3 printed pp. 45–46","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For nine images stored as rows in X∈ℝ^(9×3072), W∈ℝ^(10×3072), and b∈ℝ^10, which batch computation produces a 9×10 score matrix?","answer":"XWᵀ plus b broadcast across examples","explanation":"The feature dimensions contract and one vector of class biases is added to every example. Writing shapes before code exposes incorrect transposes.","options":["WX+b","XWᵀ plus b broadcast across examples","XᵀW plus one bias per pixel","WᵀXᵀ plus b broadcast across pixels"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn023","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 44–45","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Scores are [3.2,5.1,−1.7], the correct class is the first, and Δ=1. Compute summed multiclass hinge loss Σ_(j≠y) max(0,s_j−s_y+Δ).","answer":"2.9","explanation":"Only the second class violates the margin: max(0,2.9)+max(0,−3.9)=2.9.","value":2.9,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn024","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 40–43","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Scores are [2.5,2,1], the correct class is the first, and Δ=1. Compute the summed multiclass hinge loss.","answer":"0.5","explanation":"The prediction is correct but its gap over the second class is only 0.5, which fails the required margin of 1. Correct classification does not imply zero hinge loss.","value":0.5,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn025","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slide 52","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"What happens if the correct class term is incorrectly included in Σ max(0,s_j−s_y+Δ), with Δ>0?","answer":"The term adds the constant Δ to every example, without changing gradients away from kinks.","explanation":"At j=y the score difference is zero and the hinge evaluates to Δ. This prevents zero reported loss but adds only a constant to the objective.","options":["The loss is reduced by Δ.","The term adds the constant Δ to every example, without changing gradients away from kinks.","The correct-class term becomes zero.","Every gradient becomes Δ."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn026","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 55–58","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A bias-free linear model has zero summed hinge loss on every training example with Δ=1. What follows if all its weights are multiplied by 2?","answer":"Its hinge data loss remains zero; an L2 penalty would increase.","explanation":"Every already sufficient correct-versus-incorrect gap doubles, still satisfying the margin. The squared weight norm becomes four times larger, showing why regularization can select among zero-data-loss solutions.","options":["Its hinge data loss remains zero; an L2 penalty would increase.","Its hinge data loss becomes exactly 2.","It changes every predicted class.","It must improve held-out accuracy."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn027","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slide 51","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"With C=5 classes, all class scores equal 0, and margin Δ=1, what is the summed multiclass hinge loss for one example?","answer":"4","explanation":"There are C−1 incorrect classes, each contributing 1. For nearly equal small initial scores the expected loss is approximately C−1, not C.","value":4,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn028","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slide 48","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Three examples have summed hinge losses 2.9,0,12.9. What is their mean data loss? Give at least four decimal places.","answer":"5.266666667","explanation":"The dataset objective averages per-example losses: 15.8/3 = 5.266666… . The lecture rounds this to 5.27.","value":5.266666667,"tolerance":0.0001},{"id":"nn029","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 53,60–63","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"You replace a per-example sum of the C−1 hinge terms with their mean, while retaining the same regularization coefficient λ. Why is this more than a harmless renaming?","answer":"The data loss is divided by C−1 while the penalty is unchanged, so regularization becomes stronger relative to data fitting. To preserve the same optimum up to a global objective scale, also divide λ by C−1.","explanation":"Overall scaling of every objective term can be absorbed into a learning-rate change, but scaling only one term changes the tradeoff and generally changes the solution."},{"id":"nn030","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 70–75","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Logits are [0,ln 2,ln 3]. Compute the softmax probability of the third class.","answer":"0.5","explanation":"Exponentials are [1,2,3], so the third probability is 3/(1+2+3)=0.5.","value":0.5,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn031","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 75,80","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"The model assigns probability 0.5 to the correct class. Compute one-example cross-entropy L=−ln p_y using natural logarithms. Round the final answer to three decimal places or give more precision.","answer":"0.693147181","explanation":"For a one-hot target, cross-entropy selects −ln of the correct-class probability. The other classes contribute through normalization.","value":0.693147181,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"nn032","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 71–75 (algebraic consequence)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can max(s) be subtracted from every logit before evaluating softmax?","answer":"Adding the same constant to all logits leaves every softmax probability unchanged and reduces overflow risk.","explanation":"The common exponential factor cancels between numerator and denominator. After subtracting the maximum, the largest exponential is 1.","options":["It changes the predicted class but improves speed.","Adding the same constant to all logits leaves every softmax probability unchanged and reduces overflow risk.","It forces the correct-class probability to 1.","Softmax uses only the sign of each logit."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn033","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 85–90","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An input is already classified correctly. Multiplying all logits by a positive factor larger than 1 will generally do what?","answer":"Keep argmax fixed and make softmax more concentrated around the largest score.","explanation":"Scaling differences changes the softmax distribution, unlike adding a common constant. Larger confidence does not itself establish better calibration.","options":["Keep argmax fixed and make softmax more concentrated around the largest score.","Change argmax to the smallest score.","Leave all softmax probabilities unchanged.","Guarantee better probability calibration."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn034","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 83–84 (sign typo corrected)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 10-class softmax classifier starts with identical logits. Compute its cross-entropy loss for a one-hot target, using natural logarithms. Round the final answer to three decimal places or give more precision.","answer":"2.302585093","explanation":"The correct-class probability is 1/10, giving −ln(1/10)=ln 10. Slide 84 contains a sign typo in −log(C); its positive numerical example is correct.","value":2.302585093,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"nn035","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 61–65; Lecture 5 slide 23","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"R(W)=λΣ_k W_k² with λ=0.1, no factor 1/2, and W=[1,−2]. Compute ∂R/∂W₂ (the component at −2).","answer":"-0.4","explanation":"The derivative of λW₂² is 2λW₂. A convention using (λ/2)‖W‖² would instead produce λW₂; the stated convention matters.","value":-0.4,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn036","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 64–65","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"On input x=[1,1], w_A=[1,0] and w_B=[0.5,0.5] produce the same score. How do their L1 norms and squared L2 norms compare?","answer":"L1 is the same; squared L2 prefers w_B.","explanation":"Both L1 norms are 1. Squared L2 is 1 for w_A and 0.5 for w_B, illustrating its preference for spreading a fixed total contribution over weights.","options":["L1 is the same; squared L2 prefers w_B.","Squared L2 is the same; L1 prefers w_A.","Both penalties prefer w_A.","Both penalties have identical values."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn037","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3 slides 50,81–82,85–90","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Compare when summed hinge loss and softmax cross-entropy can equal zero, assuming Δ>0 and finite logits for more than one class.","answer":"Hinge loss is exactly zero once every incorrect score is at least Δ below the correct score. Softmax cross-entropy is strictly positive for finite logits and tends toward zero as the correct-class probability tends to 1; both are unbounded above.","explanation":"Finite exponentials give every class positive softmax probability, so p_y<1. Hinge instead has a flat zero-loss region once all margins are satisfied."},{"id":"nn038","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 50–57","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For x=0.5, w=0.5 and target y=0.8, prediction is p=xw. Compute the squared error E=(p−y)².","answer":"0.3025","explanation":"p=0.25, residual δ=−0.55, and E=0.3025. The residual retains direction; the squared error does not.","value":0.3025,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn039","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 56–59","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Use the book update quantity weight_delta=(p−y)x. For x=0.5, w=0.5 and y=0.8, compute weight_delta.","answer":"-0.275","explanation":"The residual is −0.55 and multiplying by x gives −0.275. This is the derivative of half-squared loss, not the full squared error.","value":-0.275,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn040","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 50,56–59 (loss convention clarified)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For p=xw and δ=p−y, which derivative pair is correct?","answer":"d(δ²)/dw=2δx and d(δ²/2)/dw=δx","explanation":"Differentiating the square supplies a factor 2, and differentiating p=xw supplies x. The book displays squared error but uses δx in its update; the factor can be absorbed into the learning rate.","options":["d(δ²)/dw=δx and d(δ²/2)/dw=2δx","Both derivatives equal δx.","d(δ²)/dw=2δx and d(δ²/2)/dw=δx","Both derivatives equal δ, regardless of x."],"correct":2},{"id":"nn041","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 75–76","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"With x=0.5, w=0.5, y=0.8 and α=0.1, perform one book-style update w←w−α(p−y)x. What is the new w?","answer":"0.5275","explanation":"The update quantity is −0.275, so subtraction increases the weight by 0.0275. Increasing w increases p for this positive input.","value":0.5275,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn042","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed p. 57 (negative reversal)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For x=−2, w=0.1, y=1 and half-squared loss L=(xw−y)²/2, take one step with η=0.1. What is the new weight?","answer":"-0.14","explanation":"p=−0.2, δ=−1.2 and ∂L/∂w=2.4. The weight decreases, because a more negative weight raises the prediction when multiplied by negative x.","value":-0.14,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn043","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed p. 57 (stopping)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A scalar model p=wx with no bias sees x=0 and target y=1. The error is nonzero. Why is its weight gradient zero, and would adding a bias change this?","answer":"The prediction is zero for every w, so changing w cannot affect the error and ∂L/∂w=δx=0 for half-squared loss. With p=wx+b, the bias gradient is δ and can be nonzero.","explanation":"A nonzero error does not imply every parameter influences that error. The input gates the effect of the weight."},{"id":"nn044","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use x=[8.5,0.65,1.2], w=[0.1,0.2,−0.1], y=1 and half-squared loss. Compute the gradient for the first weight.","answer":"-1.19","explanation":"p=0.86 and δ=−0.14. The first gradient is δ·8.5=−1.19; all weights share this residual but scale it by different inputs.","value":-1.19,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn045","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For x=[8.5,0.65,1.2], w=[0.1,0.2,−0.1], y=1, use w_j←w_j−0.01(p−y)x_j. What is the new third weight?","answer":"-0.09832","explanation":"The third gradient is −0.14·1.2=−0.168, so −0.1−0.01(−0.168)=−0.09832.","value":-0.09832,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn046","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 82–85","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In a linear unit with half-squared loss, w_j currently equals zero, x_j≠0 and the residual δ≠0. Which statement is correct?","answer":"Its gradient can be nonzero because ∂L/∂w_j=δx_j does not require w_j≠0.","explanation":"Zero input blocks a particular gradient; zero coefficient does not. The derivative of w_jx_j with respect to w_j is x_j.","options":["The weight is permanently frozen at zero.","Its gradient can be nonzero because ∂L/∂w_j=δx_j does not require w_j≠0.","The model must add random noise before this weight can learn.","Only negative initial weights can update."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn047","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 86–89","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"The book freezes one coefficient whose input is much larger than the others. Explain why a bigger learning rate may become usable and why freezing is still a modelling restriction.","answer":"The largest input can dominate gradient magnitude and curvature, causing large steps or divergence. Freezing its coefficient removes its update from the dynamics, but prevents the model from adapting that contribution and forces other coefficients to compensate.","explanation":"Freezing is useful for understanding sensitivity. Feature scaling or an appropriate learning rate is normally preferable when every coefficient should be learned."},{"id":"nn048","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 90–91 (update typo corrected)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"One input x=2 feeds outputs p₁=0.5x and p₂=−1x, with targets [0,1]. For L=Σ_j(p_j−y_j)²/2, give the two weight gradients in output order.","answer":"[2,−6].","explanation":"The predictions are [1,−2], residuals [1,−3], and each residual is multiplied by the shared input 2. The correct update uses residuals, not current weights."},{"id":"nn049","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 92–93 (orientation clarified); Lecture 4 slide 30","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For column-vector p=Wx, W∈ℝ^(3×4), x∈ℝ^4, and half-squared loss with δ=p−y∈ℝ^3, what is the weight-gradient matrix?","answer":"δxᵀ, shape 3×4","explanation":"Entry (j,k) is δ_jx_k: each output residual multiplies each input. The gradient must have the same shape as W.","options":["xδᵀ, shape 4×3","δxᵀ, shape 3×4","Wᵀδ, shape 4","δᵀx, a scalar"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn050","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 91–93 (code issues clarified)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A multi-output training snippet sets weight_deltas = input * weights instead of input * delta. What essential information is missing from its update?","answer":"The discrepancy between prediction and target. The correct per-output update quantity is input times that output’s residual; using the current weight ignores the desired target.","explanation":"For a squared-error objective this incorrect expression resembles shrinkage rather than the data-loss gradient. Some printed snippets in pp. 91–93 also overwrite delta rather than delta[i]; use the equations and shape checks to repair them."},{"id":"nn051","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 72–76; Ch. 5 printed pp. 86–87 (derived extension)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"For one scalar example with L=(xw−y)²/2, curvature is d²L/dw²=x². By what factor does curvature grow if x is doubled?","answer":"4","explanation":"The feature magnitude enters curvature quadratically. This explains why merely keeping a learning rate below 1 is not a universal stability guarantee.","value":4,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn052","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 72–76 (derived scalar stability)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"For one repeated example x=2 with half-squared loss, weight error evolves as e_(t+1)=(1−4η)e_t. Which learning rate converges, rather than maintaining or increasing the error magnitude?","answer":"η=0.4","explanation":"Convergence needs |1−4η|<1, or 0<η<0.5. At 0.4, the factor is −0.6, so the error alternates sides while shrinking. At 0.5 it alternates without shrinking.","options":["η=0.4","η=0.5","η=0.6","η=1"],"correct":0},{"id":"nn053","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 54–55,72–76","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Compare a fixed hot-and-cold step with a gradient-descent step near the minimum. Why can either still overshoot?","answer":"Hot-and-cold uses a fixed displacement unrelated to local error, so it can oscillate around a minimum. Gradient descent scales its step by the gradient, which shrinks near a smooth minimum, but an excessive learning rate can still jump across the minimum and diverge.","explanation":"The derivative gives local sensitivity and direction; it does not promise that an arbitrary finite step lowers the objective."},{"id":"nn054","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 11","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"An MLP maps 3072 inputs to 100 hidden units to 10 outputs. Both affine layers have biases. How many trainable parameters are present?","answer":"308310","explanation":"The first layer has 307,300 parameters; the second has 1,010. ReLU adds no trainable parameters.","value":308310,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn055","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 11","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"With column vectors in f(x)=W₂ReLU(W₁x+b₁)+b₂, mapping 3072→100→10, which pair of weight shapes is correct?","answer":"W₁:100×3072, W₂:10×100","explanation":"Each row defines one output unit of that layer. W₁ converts 3072 values to 100, and W₂ converts those 100 activations to 10 scores.","options":["W₁:3072×100, W₂:100×10","W₁:100×3072, W₂:10×100","W₁:10×3072, W₂:100×10","W₁:100×100, W₂:10×10"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn056","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 12","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Collapse f(x)=W₂(W₁x+b₁)+b₂ into a single affine map. Does having two affine layers alone create nonlinear decision boundaries?","answer":"W_eff=W₂W₁ and b_eff=W₂b₁+b₂. The result is W_eff x+b_eff, so it remains affine and does not create nonlinear boundaries.","explanation":"Biases shift boundaries but do not supply the nonlinearity that prevents a layer stack from collapsing."},{"id":"nn057","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 15","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The lecture spam network has hidden ReLU outputs [0.8,0], output weights [0.7,0.2], zero output bias and sigmoid output. Compute its spam probability. Round the final answer to three decimal places or give more precision.","answer":"0.63645254","explanation":"The output logit is 0.56 and sigmoid(0.56)=0.6364525… . This is a model probability; the threshold 0.5 yields spam.","value":0.63645254,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"nn058","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slides 13–15","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Hidden unit H₂ in the spam example has weighted input −0.1 before ReLU. Which fact explains both its zero output and its blocked gradient for this example?","answer":"Its preactivation is negative, so it lies on ReLU’s flat branch.","explanation":"ReLU depends on the total preactivation, not the sign of any one weight. A negative preactivation gives output 0 and derivative 0.","options":["One of its weights is negative.","Its preactivation is negative, so it lies on ReLU’s flat branch.","Its output connection is positive.","The target label is 1."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn059","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slides 13–14,28; Lecture 5 slide 20","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"A ReLU receives preactivation z=−3 and upstream gradient ∂L/∂a=−0.7. What is ∂L/∂z? This point is away from the kink.","answer":"0","explanation":"The local derivative is 0 for a strictly negative input, so the upstream signal is blocked regardless of its sign.","value":0,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn060","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A sigmoid node has output p=0.8. Compute its local derivative dp/dz.","answer":"0.16","explanation":"Sigmoid derivative is p(1−p). It approaches zero near p=0 or p=1, which explains saturation.","value":0.16,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn061","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 13 (activation derivative derived)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A tanh node has activation a=0.6. Compute its local derivative da/dz=1−a².","answer":"0.64","explanation":"tanh is zero-centred but still saturates near ±1; its derivative is 1−tanh²(z).","value":0.64,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn062","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slides 13,25,28 (chain-rule illustration)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Along a scalar path, five successive local derivatives are each 0.1 and the final upstream gradient is 1. What gradient reaches the first input?","answer":"1e-05","explanation":"The chain rule multiplies derivatives along the path, giving 10^−5. This illustrates why repeated saturated activations can attenuate gradients.","value":1e-05,"tolerance":1e-09},{"id":"nn063","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slides 13–14; Lecture 5 slide 20","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Distinguish a dead ReLU from sigmoid saturation. Why does the remedy depend on the cause?","answer":"A ReLU on the negative branch has exactly zero derivative; a unit that stays there across its data can stop learning through that path. A saturated sigmoid has a very small derivative near its extremes. Leaky ReLU supplies a negative-side slope, while avoiding extreme preactivations helps saturation.","explanation":"ReLU is non-saturating on its positive branch, not everywhere. Alternative activations and initialization or learning-rate choices address different failure modes."},{"id":"nn064","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 13","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Leaky ReLU is g(z)=max(0.01z,z). Compute g(−3).","answer":"-0.03","explanation":"For negative z, 0.01z is larger than z. The output stays negative and the local slope is 0.01 rather than zero.","value":-0.03,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn065","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slides 4,11–12","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"How can hidden nonlinear units overcome the single-template limitation of a linear classifier? Explain without claiming that every hidden unit is a literal object part.","answer":"The hidden layer computes several learned responses, nonlinear activations select or transform them, and the output layer combines them into class scores. Different hidden responses can support different appearances of the same class.","explanation":"This creates a nonlinear feature map and can form multiple decision regions. The lecture’s left-facing and right-facing templates are an intuition, not a requirement that representations be human-interpretable."},{"id":"nn066","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 16; Lecture 5 slide 24 (mini-batch calculation)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"There are 1000 training examples and batch size 64. Every example is used once per epoch, including the final incomplete batch. How many optimizer updates occur in one epoch?","answer":"16","explanation":"Fifteen full batches use 960 examples; one final batch uses 40. An epoch and an optimizer step are different units of training progress.","value":16,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn067","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slide 22","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The lecture PyTorch model ends in nn.Linear(100,10) and uses nn.CrossEntropyLoss. What should normally be passed to that loss?","answer":"Raw logits and integer class labels, with no extra softmax in the model.","explanation":"The cross-entropy implementation shown combines normalization with the negative log-likelihood. Feeding argmax predictions removes the differentiable score information; an extra softmax changes the intended computation.","options":["Raw logits and integer class labels, with no extra softmax in the model.","Argmax class numbers as predictions.","Already rounded probabilities.","A sigmoid value for only the largest class."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn068","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4 slides 18–20","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A model is trained to compress each image into a small code and reconstruct that same image. Another model compares two face images using shared feature extractors. Which architecture choices match these objectives?","answer":"Autoencoder and Siamese network","explanation":"Reconstruction motivates an encoder–decoder autoencoder. Paired similarity or verification motivates a Siamese architecture with shared representations. Architecture should follow the task and learning objective.","options":["GAN and RNN","Autoencoder and Siamese network","CNN classifier and SOM","RNN and DBN"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn069","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 26–27","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For f=(x+y)z at x=−2,y=5,z=−4, compute the forward value f.","answer":"-12","explanation":"Cache q=x+y=3, then f=qz=−12. The intermediate q is also needed by the multiplication node during the backward pass.","value":-12,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn070","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 26–27","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For f=(x+y)z at x=−2,y=5,z=−4, compute ∂f/∂x.","answer":"-4","explanation":"The multiplication node sends z=−4 to q, and the add node passes that gradient unchanged to x. Increasing x by 0.01 changes f by approximately −0.04.","value":-4,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn071","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28 (copy/branch rule)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Let f(x)=x²+3x. A computational graph copies x into the two factors of x² and the branch 3x. Compute df/dx at x=2.","answer":"7","explanation":"The squared branch supplies 2x=4 and the linear branch supplies 3. Their contributions must be added at the shared input, giving 7.","value":7,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn072","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 28,31","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"At a multiply gate z=ab, why can a small forward input receive a large gradient? State both backward expressions for upstream gradient g.","answer":"∂L/∂a=gb and ∂L/∂b=ga. The gradient for one input is scaled by the other input, so a small a can receive a large gradient if b is large.","explanation":"Forward magnitude and gradient magnitude measure different things. The multiply gate uses cached input values and swaps the other input into each derivative."},{"id":"nn073","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A max gate outputs max(a,b) with a=2,b=−1 and upstream gradient g=−3. What gradients reach (a,b)?","answer":"(−3,0)","explanation":"Only a is the unique winner, so the gate routes the full signed upstream gradient to a. The loser gets zero.","options":["(−3,−3)","(0,−3)","(−3,0)","(3,0)"],"correct":2},{"id":"nn074","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28; Lecture 5 slide 18 (nondifferentiable cases clarified)","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"What must be specified when a max gate has tied inputs or a ReLU input equals zero? Why can a central numerical gradient check disagree there?","answer":"These are nondifferentiable points, so an implementation chooses a valid subgradient or tie-routing convention. A central difference may sample different branches and need not match that chosen derivative.","explanation":"Use points away from kinks when checking analytic derivatives, or interpret the discrepancy using the active branches rather than declaring every mismatch a bug."},{"id":"nn075","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 29","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The spam network’s sigmoid output has logit z=0.56 and target y=1. For binary cross-entropy, compute ∂L/∂z. Use unrounded sigmoid output. Round the final answer to three decimal places or give more precision.","answer":"-0.36354746","explanation":"The combined sigmoid–BCE derivative is p−y. It is not (p−y)p(1−p); the sigmoid factor is already included in the simplified expression.","value":-0.36354746,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"nn076","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 29","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In the spam network, h₁=0.8, z=0.56 and y=1. Compute the BCE gradient for the output weight from h₁, using unrounded p=sigmoid(z). Round the final answer to three decimal places or give more precision.","answer":"-0.29083797","explanation":"Multiply the output-logit gradient p−1 by the cached input activation 0.8. The inactive hidden unit gives zero gradient for its output connection.","value":-0.29083797,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"nn077","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 29","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In the spam example, h₁’s preactivation is 0.8, its output weight is 0.7, z=0.56 and y=1. What gradient reaches h₁’s preactivation under BCE? Round the final answer to three decimal places or give more precision.","answer":"-0.25448322","explanation":"The output gradient is multiplied by 0.7 and passes through the active ReLU with local derivative 1. This value is then multiplied by each input feature for the first-layer weight gradients.","value":-0.25448322,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"nn078","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 29","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In the spam example, the feature “win” has input value 0. Its connection to active H₁ receives which gradient, and what does this establish?","answer":"Zero for this example; other examples with nonzero input can update it.","explanation":"The weight gradient is the hidden preactivation gradient times its input. A zero input blocks that connection’s update for this example, without freezing it across the dataset.","options":["Zero; the feature can never be useful.","Zero for this example; other examples with nonzero input can update it.","The same gradient as “free” because H₁ is active.","One, because the target is spam."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn079","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 30","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For y=Wx, x=[1,−2]ᵀ and upstream g=[0.5,−1]ᵀ, which matrix is ∂L/∂W?","answer":"[[0.5,−1],[−1,2]]","explanation":"Use the outer product gxᵀ. Each row is one output’s upstream gradient multiplied by the full input row.","options":["[[0.5,−1],[−1,2]]","[[0.5,−1],[−1,−2]]","[[1,−2],[0.5,−1]]","[[0.5,−1],[2,−2]]"],"correct":0},{"id":"nn080","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 30","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For W=[[3,4],[−1,2]] and upstream g=[0.5,−1]ᵀ in y=Wx, compute the gradient with respect to x.","answer":"[2.5,0]ᵀ.","explanation":"Wᵀg gives [3·0.5+(−1)(−1),4·0.5+2(−1)]ᵀ=[2.5,0]ᵀ. Contributions from every output must reach each input."},{"id":"nn081","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 28,30 (shared-bias batch rule)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A shared two-class bias is added to each of two examples. Their upstream score gradients are [1,−2] and [3,1]. Assuming the upstream values already include any averaging factor, compute the second bias gradient.","answer":"-1","explanation":"A bias is reused across examples, so its gradients add. The full bias gradient is [4,−1]; divide only if the loss convention has not already averaged the upstream gradients.","value":-1,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn082","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 31,33","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A multiply layer caches a reference to its input array. That array is overwritten before backward. What can go wrong, and what must backward use?","answer":"It may use values that differ from the forward computation and return an incorrect derivative. Backward must use the relevant input values from that forward pass, saved safely or protected from mutation.","explanation":"The chain rule differentiates the actual evaluated graph at its forward values. Correct formulas cannot compensate for an incorrect cache."},{"id":"nn083","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slide 32","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For f(w)=w³+2w at w=2, use the central difference [f(w+h)−f(w−h)]/(2h) with h=0.001. What derivative estimate results? Give six digits after the decimal point.","answer":"14.000001","explanation":"The exact derivative is 3w²+2=14. Central difference has the h² correction for this cubic, giving 14.000001 up to rounding.","value":14.000001,"tolerance":1e-07},{"id":"nn084","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 22,33","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Two training batches call backward() consecutively without clearing gradients, then step() is called once. What does the optimizer see under the usual accumulation behavior described in the lecture?","answer":"The sum of both batches’ gradients, unless the code explicitly rescales or resets them.","explanation":"Accumulation can be intentional, but an ordinary per-batch training loop clears previous gradients before computing the next update. Forgetting this changes the effective update.","options":["Only the gradient from the second batch.","The sum of both batches’ gradients, unless the code explicitly rescales or resets them.","Their elementwise maximum.","No gradient because backward erased it."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn085","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4 slides 9,25,28 (combined chain-rule exercise)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Let a=ReLU(wx), p=va and L=(p−1)²/2. At x=2,w=0.5,v=3, all quantities are scalar. Compute ∂L/∂w.","answer":"12","explanation":"The active preactivation is 1, p=3 and the residual is 2. The gradient is residual·v·ReLU′(wx)·x = 2·3·1·2 = 12.","value":12,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn086","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 6–7","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Use CNN cross-correlation without a filter flip or bias. Patch [[3,1,0],[2,1,0],[4,2,1]] meets filter [[1,0,−1],[1,0,−1],[1,0,−1]]. Compute one output.","answer":"8","explanation":"The three row contributions are 3,2,3. A strong positive response detects this filter’s bright-left/dark-right vertical contrast.","value":8,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn087","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 6","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"What changes if the same image patch and kernel are evaluated with mathematical convolution rather than CNN cross-correlation?","answer":"Convolution flips the kernel in both spatial directions before applying it; cross-correlation uses the displayed kernel directly. For the lecture’s antisymmetric vertical-edge kernel, the flip reverses the response sign.","explanation":"Libraries commonly call cross-correlation “convolution.” Both learn useful filters; the distinction is essential when a numerical question specifies a displayed fixed kernel."},{"id":"nn088","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 9–11","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 32×32×3 input is processed with six 5×5×3 filters, stride 1 and no padding. What is the output tensor shape using H×W×C notation?","answer":"28×28×6","explanation":"The spatial size is 32−5+1=28. Each filter spans all three input channels and produces one output channel, so output depth is six.","options":["28×28×3","32×32×6","28×28×6","5×5×6"],"correct":2},{"id":"nn089","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 11 (floor convention used; slide table clarified)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Under the standard floor convention O=floor((W−K+2P)/S)+1, compute O for W=7,K=3,P=0,S=3. Count only fully fitting windows.","answer":"2","explanation":"Windows start at 0 and 3. A third start at 6 would not fit. The slide’s table calls this “does not fit,” but its floor formula gives two outputs and leaves unused border positions.","value":2,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn090","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 12","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For an odd kernel K=5 and stride 1, how much symmetric zero padding P is needed on each side to preserve spatial width?","answer":"2","explanation":"P=(K−1)/2=2 for odd K and stride 1. “Same” does not imply this simple formula for arbitrary stride or even kernels.","value":2,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn091","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 11","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Input H×W×C is 28×30×8. A layer uses 3×3 kernels, padding 1, stride 2 and 12 output filters. With floor output sizing, which shape results?","answer":"14×15×12","explanation":"Apply the size formula separately to height and width: floor(27/2)+1=14 and floor(29/2)+1=15. Output depth is the number of filters.","options":["14×15×12","15×16×12","14×15×8","28×30×12"],"correct":0},{"id":"nn092","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 9–13","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Six ordinary dense-channel 5×5 filters process a three-channel image. Include one bias per filter. How many trainable parameters are in this layer?","answer":"456","explanation":"Every filter has 75 kernel weights and one bias. The same 456 parameters are reused at every spatial location.","value":456,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn093","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 13","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Count parameters for a standard 3×3 convolution with 64 input channels and 128 output channels, including one bias per output channel.","answer":"73856","explanation":"128·(64·9+1)=73,856. Standard filters span all input channels; depthwise convolution has a different count.","value":73856,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn094","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 5,13–14","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An image’s height and width are doubled, while kernel size and input/output channels stay fixed. How does a standard convolution layer’s parameter count change?","answer":"It stays fixed, while the number of output positions and computation typically grow.","explanation":"Parameters depend on kernel size and channel counts, not spatial extent. Computation still grows because the shared filters must be evaluated at more positions.","options":["It doubles.","It quadruples.","It stays fixed, while the number of output positions and computation typically grow.","It becomes zero because the filter no longer fits."],"correct":2},{"id":"nn095","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 18","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Apply max pooling with 2×2 windows, stride 2 and no padding to [[1,1,2,4],[5,6,7,8],[3,2,1,0],[1,2,3,4]]. Give the 2×2 output.","answer":"[[6,8],[3,4]].","explanation":"The four nonoverlapping windows have maxima 6,8,3,4 in row order. Pooling acts independently in each channel and introduces no learned kernel coefficients."},{"id":"nn096","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 18–19","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 16×16×32 feature tensor is max-pooled with K=2,S=2,P=0. Give the output shape and number of trainable pooling parameters.","answer":"8×8×32, with 0 trainable parameters.","explanation":"Pooling reduces spatial size, preserves channel count and chooses local values. It does not learn a separate filter for each output channel."},{"id":"nn097","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 21","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"After two stages of pooling, a feature tensor is 8×8×64. How many values enter the classifier if it is flattened?","answer":"4096","explanation":"Flattening yields 4096 values per example. Batch size is a separate leading dimension and is not included in this per-example feature count.","value":4096,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn098","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 17,29","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Three consecutive 3×3 convolutions have stride 1 and no dilation. What is one output unit’s theoretical receptive-field width in the original input?","answer":"7","explanation":"Start with width 1 and add 2 for each stride-1 3×3 layer. The result is 7×7 in two dimensions; the learned effective influence can be smaller.","value":7,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn099","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 17 (stride-aware receptive-field extension)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"From the input, apply a 3×3 stride-1 convolution, then 2×2 stride-2 pooling, then another 3×3 stride-1 convolution. No dilation is used. What is the final receptive-field width?","answer":"8","explanation":"Track jump j and receptive field r: (r,j)=(1,1)→(3,1)→(4,2)→(8,2). The final kernel spans inputs two pixels apart in original coordinates.","value":8,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn100","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slide 29","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Compare one 7×7 convolution with three 3×3 convolutions, all with 16 input and output channels, stride 1. Ignore all biases. How many fewer weights does the three-layer stack use?","answer":"5632","explanation":"The single layer has 49·16²=12,544 weights; the stack has 27·16²=6,912. Both have a 7×7 theoretical receptive field, and the stack can include additional nonlinearities.","value":5632,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn101","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 5,14,18 (equivariance/invariance clarified)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Explain translation equivariance of a stride-1 convolution and why it does not prove exact translation invariance of an entire CNN classifier.","answer":"Translating the input generally translates the feature map correspondingly when the boundary treatment permits it. Invariance would mean the final output stays unchanged. Pooling, stride, padding and the classifier can alter this relation; local pooling may provide limited tolerance to small shifts.","explanation":"Equivariance preserves transformation structure, while invariance removes sensitivity to a transformation. Finite image boundaries and downsampling limit exact equivariance."},{"id":"nn102","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5 slides 19,34","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Global average pooling is applied to a 7×7×512 feature tensor. How many scalar values per example enter the final classifier?","answer":"512","explanation":"Each channel is averaged over its 49 spatial locations. The operation maps 25,088 values to 512 summaries and does not mix channels.","value":512,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn103","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 22","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For one batch-normalization channel use x=10, μ_B=6, σ_B²=4, ε=0, γ=2 and β=−1. Compute y=γ(x−μ_B)/sqrt(σ_B²+ε)+β.","answer":"3","explanation":"The normalized value is 2, then the learned scale and shift give 3. ε=0 is specified only to simplify this arithmetic; real implementations use a positive stabilizer.","value":3,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn104","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 22","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A standard per-channel batch-normalization layer has 64 channels with learnable γ and β. How many trainable affine parameters does BN add? Exclude running-statistics buffers.","answer":"128","explanation":"There is one scale and one shift per channel. Running means and variances are state used at inference, rather than trainable affine parameters.","value":128,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn105","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 22","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"During evaluation of the CNN described in the lecture, what batch-normalization statistics should normally be used?","answer":"Running statistics collected during training, with the learned γ and β.","explanation":"Training uses channel-wise batch/spatial statistics; evaluation normally uses stored running estimates. Using training mode during evaluation can make predictions depend on other evaluation examples.","options":["Mean and variance fitted to the test labels.","Fresh per-batch statistics from the test set in training mode.","Running statistics collected during training, with the learned γ and β.","A single mean across every channel of every image."],"correct":2},{"id":"nn106","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 23 (inverted-dropout convention specified)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Training uses inverted dropout with drop probability p=0.25. If an activation 6 is retained, what value is sent onward after the standard 1/(1−p) scaling?","answer":"8","explanation":"The keep probability is 0.75. Scaling retained values by 1/0.75 preserves their expectation, so no such random masking/scaling is applied at ordinary evaluation.","value":8,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn107","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 23","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which proposed data augmentation requires checking whether it preserves the task’s label?","answer":"Any horizontal flip, because orientation can change the meaning in some tasks such as reading text.","explanation":"An augmentation encodes a task assumption. A flip can be valid for some object categories but harmful for text, asymmetric symbols or direction labels.","options":["Any horizontal flip, because orientation can change the meaning in some tasks such as reading text.","Only flips of large images; small images always preserve labels.","No augmentation needs a label check if it is random.","Only transforms with learnable parameters."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn108","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 24 (evaluation practice clarified)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Training accuracy rises while held-out accuracy stalls. Which split should guide regularization and learning-rate choices, and which split should be reserved for the final report?","answer":"Use a validation split to guide model and hyperparameter choices. Reserve the test split for a final assessment after those choices are settled.","explanation":"The lecture suggests watching train versus test accuracy; for defensible model selection, repeated tuning against a test set instead consumes it as validation data and makes the final estimate optimistic."},{"id":"nn109","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 24 (momentum convention specified)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"SGD momentum is defined here by v_t=0.9v_(t−1)+g_t and w_t=w_(t−1)−0.1v_t. If v_(t−1)=2, g_t=3 and w_(t−1)=1, compute w_t.","answer":"0.52","explanation":"The velocity becomes 4.8, so the parameter becomes 1−0.48=0.52. Momentum conventions differ; the recurrence in the prompt fixes which one is used.","value":0.52,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn110","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5 slide 24","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The lecture uses a OneCycle learning-rate schedule. Describe the main learning-rate pattern and why it should not be confused with the momentum update itself.","answer":"The learning rate first warms up to a larger value and then decays. The schedule changes the step-size multiplier over training; momentum separately combines the current gradient with past update information.","explanation":"A scheduler and an optimizer are distinct parts of the training procedure. The lecture’s warm-up/decay description is the relevant course-level pattern, not a guarantee of improvement for every dataset."},{"id":"nn111","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slide 26","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For the LeNet-style spatial path shown in the lecture, start at 32×32 and use valid 5×5 convolution, 2×2 stride-2 pooling, valid 5×5 convolution and the same pooling again. Give the four spatial widths in order.","answer":"28,14,10,5.","explanation":"A valid stride-1 convolution reduces each width by 4. A 2×2 stride-2 pooling layer then halves the even width. This prepares the final 16×5×5 tensor before the classifier."},{"id":"nn112","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slides 25,27","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which bundle best explains the lecture’s account of AlexNet’s success?","answer":"ReLU, dropout, data augmentation and GPU training enabled a larger useful convolutional model.","explanation":"Architecture, optimization and computation all matter. ReLU helped trainability; dropout and augmentation addressed generalization; GPUs made the expensive model practical.","options":["Only replacing all convolution layers with fully connected layers.","ReLU, dropout, data augmentation and GPU training enabled a larger useful convolutional model.","Removing every nonlinearity made a deeper linear map.","Using fixed Sobel filters eliminated the need for training."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn113","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slides 28–29","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can stacked small kernels be preferable to a single larger kernel in the VGG design discussed in the lecture?","answer":"They can give the same theoretical receptive field with fewer weights and more nonlinear transformations, assuming matched channel widths.","explanation":"The parameter comparison assumes a specified channel schedule. Extra layers add nonlinearities; similar receptive fields do not make the functions or compute costs identical.","options":["They can give the same theoretical receptive field with fewer weights and more nonlinear transformations, assuming matched channel widths.","A 3×3 kernel immediately sees the whole image.","They guarantee fewer activation-memory values in every implementation.","They eliminate the need for padding."],"correct":0},{"id":"nn114","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slide 30","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An Inception module has four branches with the same spatial dimensions and channel counts 64,128,32,32. After concatenating along channels, how many output channels are present?","answer":"256","explanation":"Concatenation retains all branch channels, so their depths add. Spatial dimensions must match; elementwise addition would have different compatibility rules.","value":256,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn115","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slide 31","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A 28×28×192 tensor goes directly through a 5×5 convolution with 32 outputs. Alternatively, a 1×1 convolution reduces channels to 16 before the same 5×5→32 layer. Count multiply operations only, preserve spatial size, and ignore biases. What is direct_cost/bottleneck_cost? Give three decimals.","answer":"9.677419355","explanation":"The direct cost is 120,422,400 multiplies. The bottleneck costs 2,408,448 + 10,035,200 = 12,443,648, giving a ratio about 9.677. The output shapes match, but the learned functions need not.","value":9.677419355,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"nn116","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slides 32–33","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Consider the residual addition y=x+F(x) before any following ReLU. If the desired mapping is the identity, what should the residual branch learn?","answer":"F(x)=0","explanation":"The residual branch learns H(x)−x, so identity H(x)=x requires zero residual. A post-addition ReLU would additionally clip negative components, which is why the prompt specifies the pre-ReLU value.","options":["F(x)=x","F(x)=0","F(x)=−x","F(x)=2x"],"correct":1},{"id":"nn117","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slides 33–34","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A residual branch changes a feature map from 32×32×64 to 16×16×128. What must the shortcut do before elementwise addition?","answer":"Match both spatial size and channel count, for example with a stride-2 1×1 projection.","explanation":"Addition pairs corresponding entries and therefore needs matching shapes. A projection shortcut can change channels and downsample spatially.","options":["Nothing; addition can concatenate incompatible tensors.","Match both spatial size and channel count, for example with a stride-2 1×1 projection.","Only flatten the input.","Discard the residual branch entirely."],"correct":1},{"id":"nn118","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slides 32–33,38","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A deeper plain CNN has worse training error than a shallower one. Why does this observation support an optimization/degradation explanation rather than merely an overfitting explanation?","answer":"Overfitting usually concerns a gap between strong training fit and weaker held-out performance. Worse training error shows that the deeper model is having trouble learning even the training mapping. Residual paths make identity-like solutions and gradient flow easier.","explanation":"Depth increases representational possibilities but can make optimization harder. Residual connections help this difficulty; they do not guarantee better accuracy in every shallow or short-training experiment."},{"id":"nn119","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slide 35 (depthwise-separable count derived)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A standard 3×3 convolution maps 32 channels to 64. A depthwise-separable alternative uses one 3×3 kernel per input channel followed by a 1×1 map 32→64. Ignore biases. How many weights does the separable alternative use?","answer":"2336","explanation":"The depthwise stage has 288 weights and the pointwise stage has 2048: total 2336, versus 18,432 for the standard layer. Channel mixing occurs in the 1×1 stage.","value":2336,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"nn120","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5 slides 35–36","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Match the distinctive idea to DenseNet, EfficientNet, ViT and ConvNeXt: (a) jointly scale depth/width/resolution, (b) concatenate earlier feature maps, (c) use embedded image patches with attention, (d) modernize a convolutional network with depthwise kernels, LayerNorm and GELU.","answer":"(a) EfficientNet; (b) DenseNet; (c) ViT; (d) ConvNeXt.","explanation":"These are architecture families with different design choices. DenseNet concatenation differs from ResNet addition; ViT operates on patch tokens; ConvNeXt remains convolutional despite borrowing design choices associated with Transformers."},{"id":"im001","topic":"Vision foundations","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 1, §1.1, printed pp. 3-5 (PDF pp. 29-31)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"A single image is consistent with both a small nearby object and a larger distant object. What does this illustrate?","options":["A higher bit depth uniquely recovers object size","Vision is an inverse problem whose observations can underdetermine scene properties","Every visible object has a unique 3D reconstruction","Image classification directly measures physical distance"],"correct":1,"answer":"Vision is an inverse problem whose observations can underdetermine scene properties","explanation":"Perspective can preserve the same image when scene scale and distance change together. Recovering the scene requires additional observations, assumptions, or learned information."},{"id":"im002","topic":"Vision foundations","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 1, §1.1, printed pp. 8-10 (PDF pp. 34-36)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An edge detector appears plausible on three photographs. Which evaluation most directly follows the book's approach to checking correctness and limits?","options":["Only add more attractive example images","Only compare its runtime with another detector","Test known synthetic edges, vary noise, then test representative real images","Choose thresholds on the final test set until results look good"],"correct":2,"answer":"Test known synthetic edges, vary noise, then test representative real images","explanation":"Synthetic inputs establish a known answer; controlled noise measures sensitivity; representative real imagery tests whether the model matches actual conditions."},{"id":"im003","topic":"Vision foundations","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 1, §1.2, printed pp. 19-22 (PDF pp. 45-48)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which combination explains the major growth of learned visual recognition described in Chapter 1?","options":["Large annotated datasets, greater computation including GPUs, and improved learning methods","Removing evaluation datasets and using only hand-coded rules","Replacing all images by single scalar brightness values","Assuming every scene is a blocks world"],"correct":0,"answer":"Large annotated datasets, greater computation including GPUs, and improved learning methods","explanation":"The chapter connects progress to training data, computational resources, and technical advances. This is historical context, not a claim about what the lecturer will ask."},{"id":"im004","topic":"Vision foundations","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 1, §§1.1 and 1.3, printed pp. 3-8 (PDF pp. 29-34)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Explain why knowing how an image is formed helps solve a vision task. Give one concrete ambiguity.","answer":"An image-formation model links scene geometry, illumination, reflectance, optics, and sensing to measured pixels. Inverting this model helps identify which unknowns can be estimated and which need assumptions. For example, a dark pixel could arise from dark material, weak illumination, or a surface tilted away from a light.","explanation":"The same observed pixel can have several causes. A model narrows valid explanations; it does not automatically make the inverse unique."},{"id":"im005","topic":"Image classification","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 3-10","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"An image of the same dog moves by a few pixels while its label stays dog. Which requirement is central to a useful classifier?","options":["The pixel array must be identical","The classifier must change class whenever the viewpoint changes","The classifier should tolerate label-preserving appearance changes","Every background pixel must belong to the dog class"],"correct":2,"answer":"The classifier should tolerate label-preserving appearance changes","explanation":"Classification must bridge the semantic gap: pixel values can change greatly while the object category remains fixed. A useful representation suppresses irrelevant variation."},{"id":"im006","topic":"Image classification","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 5-10","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which pair correctly distinguishes intraclass variation from fine-grained classification?","options":["Intraclass: two different dog breeds within dog; fine-grained: distinguishing those breeds","Intraclass: changing the class label from dog to truck; fine-grained: detecting exposure","Intraclass: all pixels fixed; fine-grained: all labels identical","Intraclass: only the camera sensor changes; fine-grained: only image size changes"],"correct":0,"answer":"Intraclass: two different dog breeds within dog; fine-grained: distinguishing those breeds","explanation":"Intraclass variation concerns different appearances within one category. Fine-grained classification requires separating visually similar subcategories such as breeds."},{"id":"im007","topic":"Image classification","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 7-10","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A classifier trained mainly on birds against blue sky fails on birds in dense foliage. Which lecture challenge most directly matches this failure?","options":["Only ADC resolution","Only label encoding","Only nearest-neighbor ties","Background clutter and a nonrepresentative training distribution"],"correct":3,"answer":"Background clutter and a nonrepresentative training distribution","explanation":"The category may remain bird while the background changes. A classifier can use a background shortcut if the training set does not represent deployment conditions."},{"id":"im008","topic":"Image classification","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 25","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Flatten one 32×32 RGB CIFAR-10 image without discarding channels. How many scalar coordinates does it contain?","value":3072,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"3072 scalar coordinates","explanation":"Each of the 32×32 spatial locations has three channel values; flattening changes arrangement, not the number of values.","steps":["32×32 = 1024 spatial locations.","1024×3 = 3072 channel values."]},{"id":"im009","topic":"Image classification","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 27-28","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For one ImageNet example, the true label is ranked fourth among the model's predicted labels. How is that example scored?","options":["Incorrect for both top-1 and top-5","Correct for top-5, incorrect for top-1","Correct for top-1, incorrect for top-5","Correct only if all five labels match the true label"],"correct":1,"answer":"Correct for top-5, incorrect for top-1","explanation":"Top-1 checks only the highest-ranked prediction. Top-5 counts success when the true class is among the five highest-ranked labels."},{"id":"im010","topic":"Image classification","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 23-31","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which dataset-to-task association is supported by Lecture 2?","options":["Omniglot: character categories and few-shot learning","MIT Places: only ten handwritten digit classes","MNIST: 1000 object categories","CIFAR-100: image caption sentences only"],"correct":0,"answer":"Omniglot: character categories and few-shot learning","explanation":"The lecture uses Omniglot to motivate few-shot learning and Places for scene categories. MNIST is a digit dataset; CIFAR-100 has 100 classes."},{"id":"im011","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 33-56","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For feature vectors a=(2,5,1) and b=(6,3,4), compute the L1 (Manhattan) distance.","value":9,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"9","explanation":"L1 sums absolute coordinate differences; it does not square them.","steps":["|2−6|=4, |5−3|=2, |1−4|=3.","4+2+3=9."]},{"id":"im012","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 56","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For a=(2,5,1) and b=(6,3,4), compute the L2 (Euclidean) distance. Give a decimal within 0.001.","value":5.385164807134504,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"√29 ≈ 5.385","explanation":"Euclidean distance is the square root of the sum of squared differences. The squared distance alone would be 29.","steps":["Differences are −4, 2, −3.","Squared sum =16+4+9=29.","L2=√29≈5.3851648."]},{"id":"im013","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 56-58","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Query q=(0,0), training point A=(3,0), point B=(2,2). With k=1, which statement is correct?","options":["Both L1 and L2 choose A","L1 chooses B; L2 chooses A","L1 chooses A; L2 chooses B","Both metrics tie"],"correct":2,"answer":"L1 chooses A; L2 chooses B","explanation":"L1 distances are 3 and 4, so A is closer. L2 distances are 3 and √8≈2.828, so B is closer. The distance metric can change the predicted class."},{"id":"im014","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 32-36","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"The nearest training image has label cat, but the next four nearest have label dog. What does 1-NN predict?","options":["Dog, because it uses five labels","Cat, because only the nearest label is used","Always an equal-probability tie","The mean of cat and dog"],"correct":1,"answer":"Cat, because only the nearest label is used","explanation":"1-NN copies the closest example's label. A k=5 vote would be a different classifier."},{"id":"im015","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 52-55","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"The five nearest training labels, in increasing distance order, are A,B,B,A,B. What does unweighted 5-NN predict?","options":["A, because it is closest","A and B must tie","No prediction is possible without image size","B, with three votes"],"correct":3,"answer":"B, with three votes","explanation":"Unweighted kNN counts votes among the selected neighbors. B has three votes and A has two."},{"id":"im016","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 55","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Does an odd k guarantee that multiclass unweighted kNN cannot have a vote tie?","options":["No: with k=3, three different classes can each receive one vote","Yes: parity removes every possible multiclass tie","Yes: distances are necessarily different","No: odd k is forbidden"],"correct":0,"answer":"No: with k=3, three different classes can each receive one vote","explanation":"Odd k removes a two-class equal split, but more than two classes can tie. A deterministic tie policy must still be specified."},{"id":"im017","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 50-55","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Increasing k often reduces isolated noisy regions in a kNN boundary. What is the relevant tradeoff?","options":["Training labels disappear from memory","L1 becomes identical to L2","The boundary may become smoother but small genuine class regions can be lost","Test data become unnecessary"],"correct":2,"answer":"The boundary may become smoother but small genuine class regions can be lost","explanation":"Voting over a larger neighborhood reduces sensitivity to one outlier but can combine examples from different underlying class regions. k remains task-dependent."},{"id":"im018","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 37-42","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"With N stored examples and D scalar features, what is the basic cost of brute-force 1-NN prediction for one query?","options":["O(1) regardless of N and D","O(ND) distance work, commonly summarized as O(N) when D is fixed","O(D) regardless of N","O(log N) automatically without an index"],"correct":1,"answer":"O(ND) distance work, commonly summarized as O(N) when D is fixed","explanation":"The query is compared with each of N examples, and each distance uses D coordinates. The lecture's O(N) statement treats dimension as fixed; faster indexing needs additional methods."},{"id":"im019","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 35-41","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Lecture 2 calls nearest-neighbor training O(1). State the assumption behind that simplification and distinguish it from storing the dataset.","answer":"The classifier can retain a reference to already-loaded training arrays without fitting model weights, so that assignment is O(1). Loading or copying N examples with D values each costs O(ND) work and storing them requires O(ND) values. Brute-force prediction is expensive because all stored examples are compared.","explanation":"No parameter optimization is needed, but the data are not free to load or store. This distinction resolves the apparent mismatch between the lecture shorthand and actual implementation."},{"id":"im020","topic":"Validation","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 62-68","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"You compare k∈{1,3,5} and L1 versus L2. Which split should decide these hyperparameters?","options":["The final test split after every experiment","The training split only","A validation split or cross-validation using the development data","All labeled data including the final test labels"],"correct":2,"answer":"A validation split or cross-validation using the development data","explanation":"Training stores or learns the classifier; validation selects k and metric; the untouched test split estimates final performance."},{"id":"im021","topic":"Validation","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 64-68","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For 1-NN with distinct feature vectors and no conflicting duplicate labels, every training example is queried against a set that includes itself. Why is the resulting accuracy unhelpful for choosing k?","options":["Each example has distance zero to itself, so this does not test generalization","Every training example has infinite distance to itself","It proves k=1 is optimal on every future dataset","It evaluates only the test distribution"],"correct":0,"answer":"Each example has distance zero to itself, so this does not test generalization","explanation":"The example can retrieve its own label. Perfect resubstitution accuracy under these assumptions says little about new examples. The assumptions avoid the lecture shorthand's duplicate-label caveat."},{"id":"im022","topic":"Validation","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 69-70","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Five validation-fold accuracies for a candidate are 0.70,0.80,0.75,0.85,0.90. What is their unweighted mean as a fraction?","value":0.8,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"0.80 (80%)","explanation":"For equal-sized folds the usual simple average gives each held-out fold equal weight. The independent test set remains outside this process.","steps":["Sum =0.70+0.80+0.75+0.85+0.90=4.00.","Mean =4.00/5=0.80."]},{"id":"im023","topic":"Validation","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 67-69","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A student chooses k after running fifty candidates on the final test labels. Why is the reported best test accuracy suspect?","options":["kNN cannot use labels","The test split is now part of model selection and can produce an optimistically selected estimate","Cross-validation always forces k=1","Any test split makes the model nonlinear"],"correct":1,"answer":"The test split is now part of model selection and can produce an optimistically selected estimate","explanation":"Selecting the best of many noisy test estimates uses test information. A fresh held-out evaluation is needed to estimate performance after this selection."},{"id":"im024","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 76-81","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why might kNN on learned visual features work better than kNN on raw image pixels?","options":["Learned features make every possible pair equally distant","They always remove the need for a train/test split","They guarantee perfect recognition for any label set","They can bring semantically similar images closer despite shifts or illumination changes"],"correct":3,"answer":"They can bring semantically similar images closer despite shifts or illumination changes","explanation":"Raw pixel distance can respond strongly to transformations irrelevant to category. A trained feature representation can encode more useful similarity, although quality depends on its training and the task."},{"id":"im025","topic":"Geometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36 (PDF pp. 62)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Convert the homogeneous 2D point (12,−6,3) to Cartesian coordinates. Enter its x-coordinate.","value":4,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"(x,y)=(4,−2), so x=4","explanation":"A finite homogeneous point is normalized by its last nonzero coordinate.","steps":["x=12/3=4.","y=−6/3=−2."]},{"id":"im026","topic":"Geometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36 (PDF pp. 62)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which homogeneous point represents the same finite Cartesian point as (2,4,1)?","options":["(2,4,0)","(4,8,2)","(4,8,1)","(0,0,0)"],"correct":1,"answer":"(4,8,2)","explanation":"Nonzero scalar multiples represent the same projective point. The all-zero vector is not a valid homogeneous point, and w=0 denotes a point at infinity."},{"id":"im027","topic":"Geometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36-37 (PDF pp. 62-63)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The parallel 2D lines x=1 and x=3 are represented homogeneously. Their intersection is best described as:","options":["No valid homogeneous representation exists for either line","The origin","A finite point at x=2","A point at infinity in the vertical direction"],"correct":3,"answer":"A point at infinity in the vertical direction","explanation":"The line vectors (1,0,−1) and (1,0,−3) have cross product proportional to (0,1,0). Parallel lines meet at an ideal point with last coordinate zero."},{"id":"im028","topic":"Geometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 37-38 (PDF pp. 63-64)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which operation yields the homogeneous line joining two distinct homogeneous 2D points p and q?","options":["p×q","p+q with its final coordinate set to one","p·q, a scalar","The Euclidean distance between p and q"],"correct":0,"answer":"p×q","explanation":"The cross product gives a line vector orthogonal to both point vectors, satisfying the line incidence equation for both endpoints."},{"id":"im029","topic":"Geometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36-38 (PDF pp. 62-64)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For the Cartesian line 3x+4y−10=0, what is the unsigned perpendicular distance from the origin?","value":2,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"2 coordinate units","explanation":"Divide the magnitude of the constant term by the norm of the normal coefficients. The sign of a normalized line equation depends on orientation, so this question asks unsigned distance.","steps":["Normal norm =√(3²+4²)=5.","Distance =|−10|/5=2."]},{"id":"im030","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, Table 2.1, printed pp. 40-42 (PDF pp. 66-68)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which ordered list gives the degrees of freedom of 2D translation, rigid, similarity, affine, and projective transformations?","options":["2,3,4,6,8","2,4,6,8,9","3,6,7,12,15","1,2,3,4,5"],"correct":0,"answer":"2,3,4,6,8","explanation":"Translation has two offsets; rigid adds one angle; similarity adds uniform scale; affine has six coefficients; a 3×3 homography has nine entries minus one overall scale."},{"id":"im031","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, printed pp. 41-42 (PDF pp. 67-68)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A nonsingular affine transform can shear a square into a parallelogram. Which property must it preserve?","options":["Every angle","Every Euclidean distance","Parallelism of parallel lines","The area of every shape"],"correct":2,"answer":"Parallelism of parallel lines","explanation":"A general affine matrix preserves lines and parallelism, but shear or nonuniform scale can change lengths, angles, and area."},{"id":"im032","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, printed pp. 41-42 (PDF pp. 67-68)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which transformation class preserves angles but can change every length by the same positive factor?","options":["General homography","Similarity transform","General affine shear","Arbitrary mesh warp"],"correct":1,"answer":"Similarity transform","explanation":"A similarity is uniform scale, rotation, and translation: x′=sRx+t. It changes distance scale while preserving angular relationships."},{"id":"im033","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, printed pp. 40 (PDF pp. 66)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use Cartesian axes with x right, y up. Rotate p=(2,1) by +90° counterclockwise around the origin, then translate by t=(3,−2). Enter the final x-coordinate.","value":2,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"(x′,y′)=(2,0), so x′=2","explanation":"The order is explicit: R acts on the point before t is added. Reversing the order produces a different result.","steps":["A +90° rotation gives (−1,2).","(−1,2)+(3,−2)=(2,0)."]},{"id":"im034","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.1.1 and 2.1.3, printed pp. 40-49 (PDF pp. 66-75)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Column vectors are used. Transform A is applied first and B second. Which matrix acts on the original point?","options":["A+B","AB","A−B","BA"],"correct":3,"answer":"BA","explanation":"B(Ax)=(BA)x. Matrix composition is generally not commutative; the rightmost transform acts first."},{"id":"im035","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, Eq. 2.22, printed pp. 42 (PDF pp. 68)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Points transform by x′=Hx with a nonsingular homography. How should a homogeneous line l transform to preserve incidence lᵀx=0?","options":["l′=Hl","l′=Hᵀl","l′=H⁻ᵀl","l′=l/H element by element"],"correct":2,"answer":"l′=H⁻ᵀl","explanation":"Then l′ᵀx′=lᵀH⁻¹Hx=lᵀx. Lines are covectors and transform differently from points."},{"id":"im036","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.2, Table 2.2, printed pp. 43-45 (PDF pp. 69-71)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"How many degrees of freedom does a 3D rigid transform have?","options":["3 total: translation only","6: three translation and three rotation","9 independent rotation entries plus three translation","15 because every rigid transform is projective"],"correct":1,"answer":"6: three translation and three rotation","explanation":"The orthogonality and determinant constraints reduce the nine rotation-matrix entries to three independent rotation parameters, plus three offsets."},{"id":"im037","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.3, printed pp. 45-47 (PDF pp. 71-73)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can Euler-angle parameters be awkward for smoothly estimating a 3D orientation?","options":["Rotation order matters and certain parameter configurations have singularities such as gimbal lock","All rotation matrices are singular","A rotation requires changing vector length","Euler angles make translations impossible"],"correct":0,"answer":"Rotation order matters and certain parameter configurations have singularities such as gimbal lock","explanation":"Rotations about different axes generally do not commute, and Euler parameterizations can lose local freedom at special configurations. These are parameterization issues, not failures of physical rotation."},{"id":"im038","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.3, printed pp. 48-49 (PDF pp. 74-75)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For a unit quaternion q encoding a rotation, which quaternion represents exactly the same rotation?","options":["q+(1,1,1,1)","2q without normalization","Any unit quaternion","−q"],"correct":3,"answer":"−q","explanation":"The unit quaternion representation is a double cover: antipodal quaternions q and −q map to the same rotation matrix."},{"id":"im039","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.3, Eq. 2.39, printed pp. 49 (PDF pp. 75)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use quaternion ordering (qx,qy,qz,qw). A +90° rotation about unit axis (0,0,1) has qw=cos(θ/2). What is qw? Give a decimal within 0.001.","value":0.7071067811865476,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"√2/2 ≈ 0.707107","explanation":"A quaternion uses the half-angle; substituting cos90° would incorrectly give zero.","steps":["θ/2=45°.","qw=cos45°=√2/2≈0.707107."]},{"id":"im040","topic":"Transforms","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.3, printed pp. 47-50 (PDF pp. 73-76)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Which first-order formula applies to a small axis-angle rotation vector ω, expressed in radians?","options":["Rv≈v+ω×v","Rv≈ωv element by element","Rv≈v+ω with no cross product","Rv≈v/||ω||"],"correct":0,"answer":"Rv≈v+ω×v","explanation":"Rodrigues' formula linearizes to R≈I+[ω]× near zero rotation. The cross product gives the infinitesimal change perpendicular to the original vector."},{"id":"im041","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, printed pp. 53-58 (PDF pp. 79-84)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Use a virtual image plane in front of the pinhole: u=fX/Z+cx. With f=800 pixels, (X,Z)=(2,8) metres and cx=320 pixels, what is u?","value":520,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"u=520 pixels","explanation":"The ratio X/Z is dimensionless and the focal length supplies pixel units. This uses positive forward depth and the stated virtual-plane convention.","steps":["X/Z=2/8=0.25.","u=800×0.25+320=520."]},{"id":"im042","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, printed pp. 51-54 (PDF pp. 77-80)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Under the model u=fX/Z, v=fY/Z, compare P=(2,1,4) and Q=(4,2,8).","options":["Q projects twice as far from the image center","They project to the same image coordinates","P must be invisible because X is positive","They have the same Euclidean distance to the camera"],"correct":1,"answer":"They project to the same image coordinates","explanation":"Both have X/Z=1/2 and Y/Z=1/4. Points along one viewing ray share a projected location, so a single pixel does not uniquely determine depth."},{"id":"im043","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, printed pp. 51-53 (PDF pp. 77-79)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"When is scaled orthography a reasonable approximation to perspective?","options":["When depth varies enormously compared with camera distance","Only when every point lies behind the camera","When object depth variation is small compared with its distance from the camera","Whenever the image is RGB"],"correct":2,"answer":"When object depth variation is small compared with its distance from the camera","explanation":"Then the perspective denominator varies little over the object and can be approximated by a common scale. This is often useful for distant objects with shallow depth."},{"id":"im044","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, printed pp. 55-59 (PDF pp. 81-85)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"In P=K[R|t], what is the main distinction between K and [R|t]?","options":["K stores all object labels; [R|t] stores colors","K is only a depth map; [R|t] is only gamma correction","They are always identical matrices","K describes image calibration; [R|t] maps world points into the camera coordinate frame"],"correct":3,"answer":"K describes image calibration; [R|t] maps world points into the camera coordinate frame","explanation":"The conventional K includes focal scales, skew, and principal point. The extrinsics describe orientation and position relative to the world."},{"id":"im045","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, Eq. 2.60, printed pp. 58 (PDF pp. 84)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A camera has image width W=1000 pixels and horizontal field of view 90°. Use f=W/[2 tan(FOV/2)]. What is f in pixels?","value":500,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"500 pixels","explanation":"The half-angle belongs inside the tangent and W and f use the same coordinate units.","steps":["FOV/2=45° and tan45°=1.","f=1000/(2×1)=500."]},{"id":"im046","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, printed pp. 61-62 (PDF pp. 87-88)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"When can a single homography align two ideal pinhole views without per-pixel depth?","options":["For a planar scene, or pure camera rotation about the same projection center","For every translated camera and arbitrary 3D scene","Only if both views contain exactly one pixel","Whenever the cameras have the same exposure"],"correct":0,"answer":"For a planar scene, or pure camera rotation about the same projection center","explanation":"A plane provides a common projective mapping; pure rotation changes rays without depth-dependent translation. General translated views of a scene with varying depth produce parallax."},{"id":"im047","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.5, printed pp. 64 (PDF pp. 90)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For normalized undistorted ray coordinates (x,y)=(0.5,0), use x̂=x(1+κ1r²), κ1=0.2, r²=x²+y², and κ2=0. What is x̂?","value":0.525,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"x̂=0.525","explanation":"The polynomial is applied in normalized ray coordinates before focal scaling and principal-point offset. Units and distortion direction are explicitly stated.","steps":["r²=0.5²=0.25.","Scale=1+0.2×0.25=1.05.","x̂=0.5×1.05=0.525."]},{"id":"im048","topic":"Projection","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.5, printed pp. 63-65 (PDF pp. 89-91)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why is a general homography insufficient to undo visible radial lens distortion?","options":["A homography always changes exposure","Radial distortion is the same as a rigid 3D motion","Homographies preserve straight lines, while radial distortion can bend them","Radial distortion only changes alpha values"],"correct":2,"answer":"Homographies preserve straight lines, while radial distortion can bend them","explanation":"Lens distortion introduces a nonlinear mapping of ray coordinates. A projective matrix alone cannot straighten arbitrary curved projections of world lines."},{"id":"im049","topic":"Photometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, printed pp. 68-69 (PDF pp. 94-95)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which quantities are the directional arguments of a BRDF at a fixed surface point and wavelength?","options":["Only the object's class and image filename","Incoming and outgoing light directions relative to the surface frame","Only exposure duration","Only the Euclidean distance to the image center"],"correct":1,"answer":"Incoming and outgoing light directions relative to the surface frame","explanation":"The BRDF describes how incoming illumination is redistributed into outgoing directions. Directional surface behavior explains why appearance can depend on both lighting and view."},{"id":"im050","topic":"Photometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, printed pp. 70-71 (PDF pp. 96-97)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use normalized Lambertian intensity I=ρL max(0,n·l), with unit n,l, no ambient/specular term, ρ=0.6, L=2, and angle(n,l)=60°. What is I?","value":0.6,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"I=0.6 in the stated normalized intensity units","explanation":"The cosine accounts for foreshortening. The question defines the normalization so a separate BRDF π factor is not needed.","steps":["n·l=cos60°=0.5.","I=0.6×2×0.5=0.6."]},{"id":"im051","topic":"Photometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, printed pp. 70-72 (PDF pp. 96-98)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For a purely Lambertian patch with fixed light, normal, and albedo, what happens to its radiance when only the viewing direction changes?","options":["It changes by the cube of the angle","It necessarily becomes zero","It changes exactly as a mirror highlight","It is independent of viewing direction in the ideal model"],"correct":3,"answer":"It is independent of viewing direction in the ideal model","explanation":"Diffuse outgoing radiance is view-independent in the ideal Lambertian model. Specular reflectance behaves differently."},{"id":"im052","topic":"Photometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, printed pp. 71-72 (PDF pp. 97-98)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"In the Phong specular term, what does increasing the exponent do when the peak scale is fixed?","options":["Makes the highlight narrower around the reflection direction","Makes every view equally bright","Removes the diffuse cosine term from physics","Changes a point light into an area light"],"correct":0,"answer":"Makes the highlight narrower around the reflection direction","explanation":"For a cosine between zero and one, a larger exponent suppresses values away from the peak more strongly. This produces a tighter highlight."},{"id":"im053","topic":"Photometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.1, printed pp. 67 (PDF pp. 93)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A point light illuminates a small patch. Keeping incidence angle and visibility fixed, double the source-to-patch distance. What fraction of the original illumination remains under inverse-square falloff?","value":0.25,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"1/4 =0.25","explanation":"The same emitted light spreads over an area proportional to distance squared. This comparison isolates distance from surface orientation.","steps":["Original scale ∝1/r².","New scale ∝1/(2r)²=1/(4r²).","Ratio=1/4."]},{"id":"im054","topic":"Photometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, printed pp. 73-74 (PDF pp. 99-100)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A renderer with only direct point-light diffuse and specular terms misses soft shadows and colored light reflected from nearby walls. Explain what was omitted.","answer":"It omitted extended-source visibility and multi-bounce light transport. An area light can produce a penumbra because only part of the source is visible. Nearby surfaces can reflect light into the patch, producing indirect illumination and color bleeding. A constant ambient term is only a crude approximation.","explanation":"Global illumination models interactions beyond a single light-to-surface-to-camera path. Ray/path tracing and radiosity are discussed as ways to model these effects."},{"id":"im055","topic":"Optics","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, Eq. 2.97, printed pp. 75 (PDF pp. 101)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A thin lens has focal length f=50 mm and object distance zo=1000 mm. Using 1/zo+1/zi=1/f, find image distance zi in mm (within 0.001).","value":52.63157894736842,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"zi=1000/19≈52.631579 mm","explanation":"Use consistent distance units and solve for the reciprocal of image distance. At a finite object distance the focused image plane is slightly farther than f.","steps":["1/zi=1/50−1/1000=19/1000 mm⁻¹.","zi=1000/19≈52.631579 mm."]},{"id":"im056","topic":"Optics","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, Eq. 2.98, printed pp. 76 (PDF pp. 102)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"A 50 mm lens is set to f/4. Its aperture diameter d=f/N. What is d in mm?","value":12.5,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"12.5 mm","explanation":"A higher f-number means a smaller opening for the same focal length.","steps":["d=50/4=12.5 mm."]},{"id":"im057","topic":"Optics","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, printed pp. 75-79 (PDF pp. 101-105)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Keeping focal length and focus distance fixed, what usually follows when the aperture diameter is reduced?","options":["More collected light and shallower depth of field","Less collected light and greater geometric depth of field","Exactly the same exposure and blur for every scene","All chromatic aberration disappears automatically"],"correct":1,"answer":"Less collected light and greater geometric depth of field","explanation":"A smaller aperture reduces irradiance and the geometric circle of confusion for defocused points, increasing depth of field. At very small apertures diffraction can limit sharpness."},{"id":"im058","topic":"Optics","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, printed pp. 76-79 (PDF pp. 102-105)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"With scene and shutter time fixed, approximate image irradiance as proportional to 1/N². Changing from f/4 to f/8 leaves what fraction of the original irradiance?","value":0.25,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"0.25 (one quarter)","explanation":"Doubling the f-number halves aperture diameter and quarters its area. This is a two-stop decrease.","steps":["Ratio=(4/8)²=1/4=0.25."]},{"id":"im059","topic":"Optics","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, printed pp. 77-79 (PDF pp. 103-105)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A photograph is darker near its corners and shows colored fringes along high-contrast edges. Which pairing best explains these two effects?","options":["Gamma correction; connected components","Nearest-neighbor ties; projective depth","Vignetting; chromatic aberration","Matting; histogram binning"],"correct":2,"answer":"Vignetting; chromatic aberration","explanation":"Vignetting reduces peripheral brightness. Wavelength-dependent refraction can cause differing color magnification or focus, producing fringes."},{"id":"im060","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, Fig. 2.23, printed pp. 79-84 (PDF pp. 105-110)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which description correctly relates RAW sensing to a typical processed JPEG?","options":["JPEG pixels necessarily equal the original integrated photon counts","Typical JPEG values have undergone processing such as demosaicing, white balance, tone/gamma mapping, and compression","RAW contains no sensor noise","Gamma mapping must happen before light enters the lens"],"correct":1,"answer":"Typical JPEG values have undergone processing such as demosaicing, white balance, tone/gamma mapping, and compression","explanation":"Measured sensor data and display-ready encoded pixels are distinct stages. Quantitative radiometry must account for processing and clipping."},{"id":"im061","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, printed pp. 81-84 (PDF pp. 107-110)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In low light, why does increasing analog gain/ISO not create missing scene information?","options":["It amplifies the measured signal and noise but does not collect extra photons by itself","It physically doubles the lens diameter in every camera","It guarantees noiseless measurements","It changes every pixel into a depth measurement"],"correct":0,"answer":"It amplifies the measured signal and noise but does not collect extra photons by itself","explanation":"Gain changes the signal scale. Photon collection depends on factors such as aperture, sensor area, and integration time. Higher gain is often useful but cannot recover information never sensed."},{"id":"im062","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, printed pp. 82-83 (PDF pp. 108-109)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Why can a smaller pixel pitch reduce per-pixel signal-to-noise ratio when sensor technology and exposure are comparable?","options":["Smaller pixels necessarily receive more photons","Noise becomes mathematically impossible","Each pixel samples every wavelength separately","A smaller sensing area tends to collect fewer photons"],"correct":3,"answer":"A smaller sensing area tends to collect fewer photons","explanation":"More pixels can fit in a fixed chip area, but fewer photons per pixel increase relative photon-count uncertainty. Absolute Poisson variance falls with count while signal-to-noise ratio scales as the square root of count; these are different quantities."},{"id":"im063","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, printed pp. 83 (PDF pp. 109)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For photon-limited sensing, which noise model is more appropriate than assuming constant-variance additive Gaussian noise?","options":["A label permutation model","A homography","A Poisson count model whose variance depends on photon count","A perfect deterministic threshold"],"correct":2,"answer":"A Poisson count model whose variance depends on photon count","explanation":"Photon arrivals are counts, and a Poisson model has variance equal to its mean before subsequent scaling/processing. This differs from impulsive salt-and-pepper corruption."},{"id":"im064","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.1, printed pp. 84-85 (PDF pp. 110-111)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A one-dimensional signal is sampled at fs=100 samples per unit. What is the Nyquist limit in cycles per unit?","value":50,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"50 cycles per unit","explanation":"The Nyquist limit is half the sampling frequency. Unique reconstruction also requires appropriate bandlimiting and assumptions; equality alone can hide phase-dependent edge cases.","steps":["fNyquist=fs/2=100/2=50."]},{"id":"im065","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.1, printed pp. 84-87 (PDF pp. 110-113)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You halve image width and height. What operation should precede keeping every second sample to reduce aliasing?","options":["High-pass sharpening only","Low-pass filtering for the new sampling limit","Independent thresholding of each channel","Adding random phase to every pixel"],"correct":1,"answer":"Low-pass filtering for the new sampling limit","explanation":"After decimation the allowable frequency range is smaller. Prefiltering removes detail that would otherwise fold into lower-frequency patterns."},{"id":"im066","topic":"Digital imaging","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.1, printed pp. 85-87 (PDF pp. 111-113)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a PSF from an MTF?","options":["PSF is a spatial impulse response; MTF is the magnitude of its Fourier transform","MTF is the complete color-label lookup table","PSF is always an 8×8 DCT block","MTF stores only per-pixel alpha coverage"],"correct":0,"answer":"PSF is a spatial impulse response; MTF is the magnitude of its Fourier transform","explanation":"The optical transfer function is the complex Fourier transform of the PSF; its magnitude is the MTF. This links blur in space to attenuation of spatial frequencies."},{"id":"im067","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, printed pp. 89-93 (PDF pp. 115-119)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Two different spectral distributions yield the same tristimulus response for a given observer under a given condition. They are:","options":["Necessarily identical at every wavelength","A pair of affine transformations","Guaranteed to match under every illuminant and sensor","Metamers under that condition"],"correct":3,"answer":"Metamers under that condition","explanation":"Three channel responses compress spectral information. Different spectra can match under one condition and differ under another illumination or spectral sensitivity."},{"id":"im068","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, Eq. 2.105, printed pp. 90 (PDF pp. 116)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"For XYZ=(2,3,5), compute chromaticity x=X/(X+Y+Z).","value":0.2,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"x=0.2","explanation":"Chromaticity ratios discard the overall intensity scale. They are undefined when the denominator is zero.","steps":["X+Y+Z=10.","x=2/10=0.2."]},{"id":"im069","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, printed pp. 91-92 (PDF pp. 117-118)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why use a perceptual color space such as Lab instead of interpreting equal RGB differences as equal perceived differences?","options":["RGB distances are always exact perceptual distances","Perceived color differences depend on location in color space, so Lab aims for greater perceptual uniformity","Lab makes every colored object white","Lab removes the need for a reference white"],"correct":1,"answer":"Perceived color differences depend on location in color space, so Lab aims for greater perceptual uniformity","explanation":"Lab uses nonlinear transformations and a reference white to make differences more aligned with perception. It is an approximation, not a guarantee of perfect perceptual equality."},{"id":"im070","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, printed pp. 93-94 (PDF pp. 119-120)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"In a standard Bayer 2×2 color-filter block, how many samples are green?","options":["Zero","One","Two","Four"],"correct":2,"answer":"Two","explanation":"The standard pattern has two green filters, one red, and one blue. Demosaicing estimates the missing colors at each sensor location."},{"id":"im071","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, printed pp. 94 (PDF pp. 120)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which simple operation models white balancing before clipping?","options":["Apply a separate multiplicative gain to each color channel","Swap every foreground and background pixel","Only take a spatial gradient","Replace RGB by distance to the nearest zero pixel"],"correct":0,"answer":"Apply a separate multiplicative gain to each color channel","explanation":"A diagonal color transform rescales channels to compensate for a color cast. More general color corrections can use a full 3×3 matrix."},{"id":"im072","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, Eqs. 2.111-2.112, printed pp. 94-96 (PDF pp. 120-122)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For this exercise, gamma encoding is e=I^(1/2) with linear I∈[0,1]. An encoded pixel has e=0.5. What is its decoded linear intensity I?","value":0.25,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"I=0.25","explanation":"Invert the stated encoding with I=e². The exponent convention is explicit to avoid the conflicting wording around Eq. 3.7 in the supplied draft.","steps":["e=√I.","I=e²=0.5²=0.25."]},{"id":"im073","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, printed pp. 95-98 (PDF pp. 121-124)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why should physically accurate exposure blending generally be done after decoding gamma/tone encoding to linear light?","options":["The mean of encoded values always equals the encoded mean of light","Encoded values have no possible inverse","Linear light is required only for binary images","Encoded arithmetic can give incorrect radiometric mixtures because the encoding is nonlinear"],"correct":3,"answer":"Encoded arithmetic can give incorrect radiometric mixtures because the encoding is nonlinear","explanation":"Linear radiance adds according to light transport. A nonlinear encoding generally does not commute with averaging; decode, blend, and then encode for display."},{"id":"im074","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.3, printed pp. 98-100 (PDF pp. 124-126)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which part of conventional lossy JPEG directly discards transform precision?","options":["Lossless entropy coding alone","Quantization of DCT coefficients","Naming the file","The mathematical existence of cosine bases"],"correct":1,"answer":"Quantization of DCT coefficients","explanation":"Quantization maps ranges of coefficient values onto a smaller set. DCT and entropy coding can be reversible at sufficient precision, but lossy coefficient quantization is not."},{"id":"im075","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.3, printed pp. 98 (PDF pp. 124)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why do many image codecs preserve luma more finely than chroma?","options":["Humans cannot see color at all","Chroma contains every edge while luma contains none","Human vision is generally more sensitive to fine luminance detail than fine color detail","It makes all compression lossless"],"correct":2,"answer":"Human vision is generally more sensitive to fine luminance detail than fine color detail","explanation":"Separating luma and color-difference channels allows chroma subsampling with less visible impact than comparable luma loss, although artifacts can still appear."},{"id":"im076","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.3, Eq. 2.120, printed pp. 100-101 (PDF pp. 126-127)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For normalized images with peak Imax=1 and MSE=0.01, compute PSNR=10 log10(Imax²/MSE) in dB.","value":20,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"20 dB","explanation":"Use the peak for the stated numeric range. Using 255 here would mix normalized and 8-bit conventions.","steps":["Imax²/MSE=1/0.01=100.","10log10(100)=20 dB."]},{"id":"im077","topic":"Color and compression","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.3, printed pp. 100-101 (PDF pp. 126-127)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An aggressively quantized JPEG shows a visible grid of 8×8 block boundaries. What explains this?","options":["All genuine scenes contain such a grid","The camera focal length must be eight pixels","Nearest-neighbor training creates an 8×8 Voronoi grid","Different blocks are transformed and quantized separately, which can create discontinuities"],"correct":3,"answer":"Different blocks are transformed and quantized separately, which can create discontinuities","explanation":"Independent block coding can create mismatched boundaries when coefficient precision is lost. PSNR measures error but does not fully characterize how objectionable such artifacts look."},{"id":"im078","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.1, printed pp. 112 (PDF pp. 138)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"An 8-bit point operation is g=clip(1.5f+20,0,255), with f=180. What is g?","value":255,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"255","explanation":"The affine value is above the 8-bit range, so clipping removes the excess. Clipping is a nonlinear part of the full operation.","steps":["1.5×180+20=290.","clip(290,0,255)=255."]},{"id":"im079","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.1, printed pp. 112 (PDF pp. 138)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Without clipping, which operator is linear in its input image?","options":["h(f)=f+20","h(f)=f²","h(f)=3f","h(f)=√f"],"correct":2,"answer":"h(f)=3f","explanation":"Multiplication by a fixed scalar satisfies superposition and homogeneity. A nonzero additive offset makes an affine operator, not a linear one."},{"id":"im080","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.1.1-3.1.2, printed pp. 112-113 (PDF pp. 138-139)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For positive unclipped RGB values, which operation preserves normalized channel ratios R/(R+G+B), G/(R+G+B), B/(R+G+B)?","options":["Adding the same nonzero offset to R,G,B","Multiplying R,G,B by the same positive factor","Replacing each channel by its square","Multiplying each channel by an arbitrary different factor"],"correct":1,"answer":"Multiplying R,G,B by the same positive factor","explanation":"A common scale cancels from numerator and denominator. Equal additive offsets generally change ratios; per-channel gains are used for color balancing and deliberately change them."},{"id":"im081","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.3, printed pp. 113-114 (PDF pp. 139-140)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use straight (unpremultiplied) foreground F=0.8, background B=0.2, and foreground alpha α=0.25. Compute C=αF+(1−α)B in linear light.","value":0.35,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"C=0.35","explanation":"Alpha is foreground opacity or coverage, not background weight. The question specifies straight colors and linear light.","steps":["αF=0.25×0.8=0.20.","(1−α)B=0.75×0.2=0.15.","C=0.20+0.15=0.35."]},{"id":"im082","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.3, printed pp. 114 (PDF pp. 140)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You store premultiplied foreground P=αF. Which composite-over-opaque-background equation should use P?","options":["C=P+(1−α)B","C=αP+(1−α)B","C=P+αB","C=(1−α)P+B"],"correct":0,"answer":"C=P+(1−α)B","explanation":"P already includes alpha. Multiplying it again by alpha would make semitransparent foreground pixels too dark."},{"id":"im083","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.4, printed pp. 117 (PDF pp. 143)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 4-level grayscale histogram for levels 0,1,2,3 is [1,2,1,0]. Define equalization by s(i)=(L−1)CDF(i), L=4, without rounding or subtracting CDFmin. What is s(1)?","value":2.25,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"s(1)=2.25","explanation":"Different implementations may round or subtract the first nonzero CDF. This exercise fixes the convention so the numeric answer is unambiguous.","steps":["N=4.","CDF(1)=(1+2)/4=0.75.","s(1)=3×0.75=2.25."]},{"id":"im084","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.4, printed pp. 116-118 (PDF pp. 142-144)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which statement about global histogram equalization is correct for a finite discrete image?","options":["It guarantees exactly the same count in every output bin","It uses an intensity CDF; discretization can prevent a perfectly flat output histogram","It moves pixels to new spatial coordinates","It requires convolution with an edge kernel"],"correct":1,"answer":"It uses an intensity CDF; discretization can prevent a perfectly flat output histogram","explanation":"The CDF remaps intensities monotonically. Finite counts, repeated input values, quantization, and rounding limit exact uniformity."},{"id":"im085","topic":"Point operators","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.4, printed pp. 117-119 (PDF pp. 143-145)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Separate histogram equalization in nonoverlapping blocks creates visible block boundaries. Which remedy is described in the reading?","options":["Delete all boundary pixels","Use no histogram information","Use only the brightest pixel in each block","Smoothly interpolate neighboring transfer functions; limit contrast if noise is amplified"],"correct":3,"answer":"Smoothly interpolate neighboring transfer functions; limit contrast if noise is amplified","explanation":"Adaptive equalization blends local mappings to reduce discontinuities. CLAHE limits excessive local contrast amplification, which can make noise conspicuous."},{"id":"im086","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2, printed pp. 120 (PDF pp. 146)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"At one 1D location, the neighborhood [f(i−1),f(i),f(i+1)] is [2,4,8]. Kernel offsets [h(−1),h(0),h(1)]=[−1,0,1]. Compute correlation g(i)=Σk f(i+k)h(k).","value":6,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"Correlation g(i)=6","explanation":"Correlation uses the kernel in the displayed offset order. Convolution reverses it and would produce −6.","steps":["g=(−1)×2+0×4+1×8=6."]},{"id":"im087","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2, printed pp. 120 (PDF pp. 146)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"At one 1D location, [f(i−1),f(i),f(i+1)]=[2,4,8] and [h(−1),h(0),h(1)]=[−1,0,1]. Compute convolution g(i)=Σk f(i−k)h(k).","value":-6,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"Convolution g(i)=−6","explanation":"The minus offset in f reverses the input association relative to correlation. Symmetric kernels hide this distinction, but this derivative kernel does not.","steps":["For k=−1: f(i+1)h(−1)=−8.","For k=0: contribution=0.","For k=1: f(i−1)h(1)=2.","Sum=−8+2=−6."]},{"id":"im088","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2, printed pp. 122 (PDF pp. 148)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Away from boundaries, what does shift invariance of a fixed filter mean?","options":["Shifting an input and then filtering gives the shifted version of the filtered input","Every output pixel has the same value","The filter must be nonlinear","A separate learned kernel is required at each location"],"correct":0,"answer":"Shifting an input and then filtering gives the shifted version of the filtered input","explanation":"The operator uses the same relative neighborhood and coefficients everywhere. Boundary extension can affect whether this statement holds on a finite image."},{"id":"im089","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2, padding, printed pp. 123-124 (PDF pp. 149-150)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For 1D input [9,9,9], apply the centered average kernel [1/3,1/3,1/3] at the leftmost pixel using zero outside the array. What output is obtained?","value":6,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"6","explanation":"Zero padding introduces a dark neighbor. Replicate padding would give 9, illustrating why a border convention matters.","steps":["Neighborhood=[0,9,9].","Average=(0+9+9)/3=6."]},{"id":"im090","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.1, printed pp. 124 (PDF pp. 150)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A separable 7×7 kernel is applied as horizontal 7-tap and vertical 7-tap filters. Ignoring overhead, how many multiply-add terms are used per output pixel across both passes?","value":14,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"14 terms per pixel","explanation":"A direct 7×7 calculation uses 49 terms. Separability reduces K² work to approximately 2K.","steps":["Horizontal pass:7 terms.","Vertical pass:7 terms.","Total=14."]},{"id":"im091","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.1, printed pp. 124-125 (PDF pp. 150-151)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"For a nonzero 2D kernel regarded as a matrix, what condition makes it exactly separable into one horizontal and one vertical kernel?","options":["It has rank one, so it is an outer product","It has full rank","Every entry must be negative","Its dimensions must be prime"],"correct":0,"answer":"It has rank one, so it is an outer product","explanation":"An outer product vhᵀ has rank one. In the SVD, exactly one nonzero singular value indicates one-term separability."},{"id":"im092","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.2, printed pp. 126-127 (PDF pp. 152-153)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A Sobel x-derivative combines a horizontal central difference with vertical smoothing. Which image structure should give a strong response?","options":["A constant-intensity patch only","A purely horizontal edge with no x variation","A vertical edge, where intensity changes across x","Every region with alpha zero"],"correct":2,"answer":"A vertical edge, where intensity changes across x","explanation":"The derivative direction is perpendicular to the edge orientation: an x derivative measures left-to-right change, so it emphasizes vertical edges."},{"id":"im093","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.3, printed pp. 127-128 (PDF pp. 153-154)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"What allows a first-order Gaussian derivative filter to be steered to direction u=(cosθ,sinθ)?","options":["Thresholding the image before every possible angle","Taking a linear combination cosθ Gx+sinθ Gy","Using only a fixed isotropic scalar mean","Changing the image's gamma exponent to θ"],"correct":1,"answer":"Taking a linear combination cosθ Gx+sinθ Gy","explanation":"The directional derivative is the dot product of the gradient with the direction. Two basis responses let you evaluate many orientations cheaply."},{"id":"im094","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.3, integral images, printed pp. 129 (PDF pp. 155)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A zero-padded integral-image lookup for a rectangle gives bottom-right 40, above-right 12, bottom-left 9, above-left 3. Using inclusion-exclusion, what is the rectangle sum?","value":22,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"22","explanation":"Subtract the two excluded strips and add the doubly-subtracted corner back. The lookup conventions are specified by their roles.","steps":["Sum=40−12−9+3=22."]},{"id":"im095","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.3, recursive filtering, printed pp. 130-131 (PDF pp. 156-157)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"What distinguishes an IIR recursive filter from a fixed finite-support FIR filter?","options":["IIR is always nonlinear","FIR cannot use negative coefficients","FIR has no output pixels","IIR uses prior output values and can have an impulse response of unbounded duration"],"correct":3,"answer":"IIR uses prior output values and can have an impulse response of unbounded duration","explanation":"A recursive dependence can propagate an impulse indefinitely in principle. FIR output uses a finite set of input values directly."},{"id":"im096","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.1, printed pp. 132-133 (PDF pp. 158-159)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"A five-pixel neighborhood is [10,11,250,12,9]. What is its median-filter output?","value":11,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"11","explanation":"Sorting gives [9,10,11,12,250]. The isolated high value does not dominate the median as it would an average.","steps":["Sort:9,10,11,12,250.","Select the middle (third) value:11."]},{"id":"im097","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.1, Eq. 3.33, printed pp. 133 (PDF pp. 159)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For fixed positive normalized weights, minimizing Σj wj|fj−g| versus Σj wj(fj−g)² leads respectively to:","options":["A weighted mean; a weighted median","A weighted median; a weighted mean","A Fourier phase; an alpha matte","A nearest-neighbor label; a homography"],"correct":1,"answer":"A weighted median; a weighted mean","explanation":"Absolute deviation favors a median, which resists extremes. Squared deviation gives the mean and penalizes large residuals more strongly."},{"id":"im098","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, printed pp. 133-134 (PDF pp. 159-160)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Why is ordinary bilateral filtering nonlinear in the input image?","options":["The spatial coordinates are integers","All Gaussian filters are nonlinear","Its range weights depend on the input intensity differences","The normalized weights always sum to zero"],"correct":2,"answer":"Its range weights depend on the input intensity differences","explanation":"The coefficients change when the image changes. A weighted sum with fixed coefficients is linear; bilateral image-dependent weights are not fixed."},{"id":"im099","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, printed pp. 134 (PDF pp. 160)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In a bilateral filter, let range standard deviation σr become extremely large while the spatial kernel stays fixed. What limiting behavior follows?","options":["All neighbors receive zero weight","It tends toward the normalized spatial Gaussian blur","It becomes a binary erosion","It necessarily returns the maximum value"],"correct":1,"answer":"It tends toward the normalized spatial Gaussian blur","explanation":"The range factor exp(−ΔI²/(2σr²)) approaches one, so intensity differences no longer suppress cross-edge averaging."},{"id":"im100","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, Eq. 3.34, printed pp. 134 (PDF pp. 160)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A bilateral calculation has selected values [10,20] and total domain×range weights [1,0.5]. What is its normalized output?","value":13.333333333333334,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"40/3≈13.333333","explanation":"Both the weighted sum and the sum of weights are needed. The kernel is already evaluated, so no σ values are required.","steps":["Weighted sum=10×1+20×0.5=20.","Weight sum=1.5.","Output=20/1.5=40/3."]},{"id":"im101","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, printed pp. 138-139 (PDF pp. 164-165)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Binary foreground is 1 and a fixed symmetric structuring element is used. Which sequence is opening?","options":["Dilation then erosion","Median then multiplication","Erosion then dilation","Erosion twice followed by thresholding at zero"],"correct":2,"answer":"Erosion then dilation","explanation":"Opening can remove foreground structures too small for the element. Closing is the reverse sequence and can fill small background gaps or holes."},{"id":"im102","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, printed pp. 138-139 (PDF pp. 164-165)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A binary neighborhood contains three foreground 1s under a 3×3 all-ones structuring element. For the center, what do dilation and erosion output?","options":["Dilation 1; erosion 0","Dilation 0; erosion 1","Both 1","Both 0"],"correct":0,"answer":"Dilation 1; erosion 0","explanation":"Dilation asks whether at least one foreground sample is present. Erosion asks whether all nine samples are foreground. Here the count is 3."},{"id":"im103","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, printed pp. 139-140 (PDF pp. 165-166)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A queried foreground pixel is at (3,4) and the nearest background pixel under the stated Manhattan metric is at (0,2). What is its distance-transform value?","value":5,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"5 pixels in Manhattan distance","explanation":"The question explicitly gives the nearest background pixel and metric. Euclidean distance would instead be √13.","steps":["|3−0|+|4−2|=3+2=5."]},{"id":"im104","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, printed pp. 141 (PDF pp. 167)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Only two foreground pixels are present at coordinates (0,0) and (1,1). How many foreground connected components exist under N4 and N8 adjacency respectively?","options":["One; two","Two; one","Two; two","One; one"],"correct":1,"answer":"Two; one","explanation":"They touch diagonally. N4 connects only horizontal/vertical neighbors; N8 additionally permits diagonal connections."},{"id":"im105","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, guided filtering, printed pp. 136-137 (PDF pp. 162-163)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In joint/cross-bilateral filtering of noisy image f using clean guide h, which description is correct?","options":["The output must equal h everywhere","The guide supplies the range-similarity weights, while f supplies the values being averaged","The guide can only be used as a final filename","The output always equals an unweighted median of h"],"correct":1,"answer":"The guide supplies the range-similarity weights, while f supplies the values being averaged","explanation":"The guide determines where to avoid mixing across edges. Good alignment matters; guide edges may otherwise transfer inappropriate structure into the output."},{"id":"im106","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, printed pp. 142-143 (PDF pp. 168-169)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A fixed LSI filter receives a sinusoid of one spatial frequency. What can happen to the output away from boundaries?","options":["Its frequency must double","Its frequency becomes random","Its amplitude and phase can change while its frequency remains the same","The output must be binary"],"correct":2,"answer":"Its amplitude and phase can change while its frequency remains the same","explanation":"Complex sinusoids are eigenfunctions of LSI filters. The frequency response describes the amplitude gain and phase shift at each frequency."},{"id":"im107","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, Eq. 3.57, printed pp. 144 (PDF pp. 170)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use normalized forward DFT F(k)=(1/N)Σn f(n)exp(−j2πkn/N). For f=[2,4,6,8], what is the DC coefficient F(0)?","value":5,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"F(0)=5","explanation":"At zero frequency every exponential equals one, so this normalized convention returns the mean. An unnormalized DFT would return the sum 20.","steps":["N=4.","F(0)=(2+4+6+8)/4=5."]},{"id":"im108","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, printed pp. 144 (PDF pp. 170)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which complexity comparison is correct for computing all N DFT bins?","options":["Direct DFT O(N); FFT O(N²)","Direct DFT O(N²); FFT O(N log N)","Both are automatically O(1)","FFT computes a different transform from the DFT"],"correct":1,"answer":"Direct DFT O(N²); FFT O(N log N)","explanation":"The FFT is an algorithm for the same DFT and reorganizes the work. FFT does not change the mathematical transform or create new image information."},{"id":"im109","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2 and 3.4, printed pp. 122-144 (PDF pp. 148-170)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Using the conventional unnormalized forward DFT and 1/N inverse, which relation holds for circular convolution g=f*h?","options":["G(k)=F(k)H(k)","G(k)=F(k)+H(k)","G(k)=F(k)/H(k) for every H including zero","G(k)=|F(k)| with phase discarded"],"correct":0,"answer":"G(k)=F(k)H(k)","explanation":"Convolution in space becomes multiplication in frequency with this normalization. A normalized forward DFT would introduce a scale factor, so the convention is stated."},{"id":"im110","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2 and 3.4, printed pp. 122-148 (PDF pp. 148-174)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"To compute full linear convolution of finite 1D arrays of lengths N and M using FFT multiplication, what padding length is sufficient?","options":["Only max(N,M), with no further conditions","Exactly one sample","Any length smaller than both N and M","At least N+M−1, preventing circular wrap-around of the linear result"],"correct":3,"answer":"At least N+M−1, preventing circular wrap-around of the linear result","explanation":"A raw DFT multiplication implements periodic/circular convolution. Padding allows the full linear support to fit without overlap. This is an implementation consequence of the convolution model."},{"id":"im111","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.2, Eq. 3.22, printed pp. 126 (PDF pp. 152)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use unsharp masking g=f+λ(f−b), where b is a blurred image. At a pixel f=100, b=80, λ=0.5, with no clipping. What is g?","value":110,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"110","explanation":"The high-frequency residual is f−b=20. Adding part of it accentuates local detail but may also amplify noise or halos.","steps":["Residual=100−80=20.","g=100+0.5×20=110."]},{"id":"im112","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4.1, printed pp. 147-148 (PDF pp. 173-174)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"In a DCT block, the DC basis is constant. Which property does its coefficient encode (up to the transform's scaling)?","options":["The count of connected foreground components","The camera's rotation axis","The block's average intensity","Only the diagonal edge orientation"],"correct":2,"answer":"The block's average intensity","explanation":"The DC coefficient measures the projection onto a constant basis. AC coefficients represent changing patterns within the block."},{"id":"im113","topic":"Fourier","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2.2 and 3.4.2, printed pp. 126-149 (PDF pp. 152-175)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can sharpening increase apparent detail without recovering the true detail destroyed by severe blur or clipping?","answer":"Sharpening boosts differences or selected frequencies already present. If blur suppresses a frequency strongly, inverse amplification also boosts noise; if a frequency or clipped value is lost entirely, no ordinary sharpening operation uniquely recovers it. Reconstruction then needs additional information or assumptions.","explanation":"Apparent crispness and correct restoration are different goals. Frequency-response reasoning identifies which information remains in the measurements."},{"id":"im114","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.1, printed pp. 150-151 (PDF pp. 176-177)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For 1D linear interpolation between f(0)=10 and f(1)=30, what is f(0.25)?","value":15,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"15","explanation":"Linear interpolation weights the endpoints by 1−t and t.","steps":["t=0.25.","f=0.75×10+0.25×30=15."]},{"id":"im115","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.1, printed pp. 150-152 (PDF pp. 176-178)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"What is the important difference between an interpolating kernel and an approximating kernel?","options":["Interpolating kernels must always have infinite support","An interpolating reconstruction passes through the original samples; an approximating reconstruction need not","An approximating kernel has no formula","Interpolating and approximating are identical terms"],"correct":1,"answer":"An interpolating reconstruction passes through the original samples; an approximating reconstruction need not","explanation":"The reading distinguishes cubic interpolants from cubic B-spline approximation. A smoother result need not reproduce each original sample exactly."},{"id":"im116","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.1, printed pp. 151-152 (PDF pp. 177-178)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which tradeoff can occur with a sharp windowed-sinc or cubic interpolation kernel that has negative lobes?","options":["It can retain sharper detail but create overshoot or ringing near strong changes","It always yields a binary image","It removes every source of aliasing without any assumptions","It cannot produce values outside the input sample range"],"correct":0,"answer":"It can retain sharper detail but create overshoot or ringing near strong changes","explanation":"Negative lobes help sharpen the frequency response but can produce oscillatory overshoot. Interpolation method should match the task and tolerable artifacts."},{"id":"im117","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.2, printed pp. 153-154 (PDF pp. 179-180)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"After decimating a signal by a factor of 4, which maximum retained input frequency is appropriate in original cycles per input sample for an ideal prefilter?","options":["0.5","2.0","0.125","4.0"],"correct":2,"answer":"0.125","explanation":"The output sampling frequency is 1/4 of the input, so its Nyquist limit measured in input units is (1/4)/2=1/8. Boundary equality needs the usual sampling assumptions."},{"id":"im118","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.3, printed pp. 155-156 (PDF pp. 181-182)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"core","prompt":"An octave Gaussian pyramid starts at 256×256. Each next level halves both dimensions. How many pixels are in level 2 (level 0 is the input)?","value":4096,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"4096 pixels (64×64)","explanation":"An octave halves width and height, so each level has one quarter of the preceding pixel count.","steps":["Level0:256×256.","Level1:128×128.","Level2:64×64=4096."]},{"id":"im119","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.3, printed pp. 157 (PDF pp. 183)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Let Gℓ be Gaussian levels and E be the exact expansion operator used during construction. Define Lℓ=Gℓ−E(Gℓ+1). Which information is needed for exact reconstruction without quantization?","options":["Only the finest L0","All residual L levels and the coarsest Gaussian level","Only all image histograms","Only the spatial filter name"],"correct":1,"answer":"All residual L levels and the coarsest Gaussian level","explanation":"Reconstruction uses Gℓ=Lℓ+E(Gℓ+1) recursively. The coarsest low-pass image carries information missing from residual bands."},{"id":"im120","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.3, printed pp. 157 (PDF pp. 183)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"At a pixel, Gaussian level Gℓ=50 and the expanded next level E(Gℓ+1)=44. What is Laplacian residual Lℓ=Gℓ−E(Gℓ+1)?","value":6,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"6","explanation":"The residual can be positive or negative; it stores the detail not represented by the expanded coarse image.","steps":["Lℓ=50−44=6.","Reconstruction gives6+44=50."]},{"id":"im121","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2.3 and 3.5.3, printed pp. 127-158 (PDF pp. 153-184)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Which statement correctly relates LoG and DoG?","options":["They are exactly the same formula for every pair of scales","LoG is a spatial translation and DoG is an alpha channel","DoG is always a low-pass image","LoG differentiates a Gaussian-smoothed image; a difference of nearby Gaussian scales approximates a related band-pass response"],"correct":3,"answer":"LoG differentiates a Gaussian-smoothed image; a difference of nearby Gaussian scales approximates a related band-pass response","explanation":"LoG is the Laplacian of Gaussian. DoG subtracts two differently smoothed images; nearby scales give an approximation related to a scale-normalized LoG."},{"id":"im122","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.4, printed pp. 159-161 (PDF pp. 185-187)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A critically sampled separable 2D wavelet stage on an even-sized N-pixel image produces LL,LH,HL,HH. Ignoring boundary extension, how many coefficients are stored in total?","options":["N/4 in total","4N in total","N in total: four subbands with N/4 each","N² in total"],"correct":2,"answer":"N in total: four subbands with N/4 each","explanation":"Each axis is downsampled by two in each subband. The four N/4-sized bands retain a total of N coefficients. Some overcomplete wavelet families intentionally store more."},{"id":"im123","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.4, printed pp. 162-163 (PDF pp. 188-189)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Why are the prediction and update steps in a wavelet lifting scheme reversible?","options":["The predictor must correctly predict every input sample","The stored difference and updated sequence let the same operations be undone in reverse order with signs reversed","They discard every odd sample","Every coefficient must be zero"],"correct":1,"answer":"The stored difference and updated sequence let the same operations be undone in reverse order with signs reversed","explanation":"Prediction stores the error rather than throwing it away. Reversing the update restores the even sequence; reversing prediction restores the odd sequence, regardless of prediction accuracy."},{"id":"im124","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.5, printed pp. 165-167 (PDF pp. 191-193)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For multiband image blending, which construction reduces broad color seams without smearing all fine texture across the seam?","options":["Blend Laplacian image bands with corresponding Gaussian-mask levels, then reconstruct","Blur the two full images infinitely before cutting them","Use one hard binary boundary at every frequency","Discard every band except the finest one"],"correct":0,"answer":"Blend Laplacian image bands with corresponding Gaussian-mask levels, then reconstruct","explanation":"The mask is broader at coarse scales and narrower at fine scales. Low-frequency exposure differences blend over larger regions while high-frequency textures mix locally."},{"id":"im125","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6, printed pp. 168 (PDF pp. 194)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Which equation describes changing the spatial domain rather than only the image values?","options":["g(x)=f(x)²","g(x)=f(h(x))","g(x)=2f(x)+5","g(x)=clip(f(x),0,255)"],"correct":1,"answer":"g(x)=f(h(x))","explanation":"A warp changes which source location supplies an output pixel. A point operation changes the value at a corresponding location."},{"id":"im126","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, printed pp. 169-171 (PDF pp. 195-197)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Why can simple forward warping with rounded destination coordinates leave holes under magnification?","options":["Each destination necessarily receives too many source values","The source and destination always have the same grid","Mapped source samples may skip some destination locations","It computes the inverse homography twice"],"correct":2,"answer":"Mapped source samples may skip some destination locations","explanation":"Source samples are pushed onto a grid that may be denser or differently positioned. A destination-driven inverse warp queries every output pixel, avoiding this particular coverage problem."},{"id":"im127","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, printed pp. 170-171 (PDF pp. 196-197)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A forward 1D coordinate transform is x′=2x+3. During inverse warping, what source coordinate supplies destination x′=11?","value":4,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"x=4","explanation":"Inverse sampling solves for source position rather than applying the forward formula to a destination coordinate.","steps":["11=2x+3.","x=(11−3)/2=4."]},{"id":"im128","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.5.1 and 3.6.1, printed pp. 150-172 (PDF pp. 176-198)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Bilinearly sample a 2×2 patch with f(0,0)=0, f(1,0)=10, f(0,1)=20, f(1,1)=30 at (x,y)=(0.25,0.5). What value is obtained?","value":12.5,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"12.5","explanation":"Coordinates and corner order are explicit. Bilinear weights are products of the horizontal and vertical linear weights.","steps":["Weights:(0,0)0.375;(1,0)0.125;(0,1)0.375;(1,1)0.125.","Value=0×0.375+10×0.125+20×0.375+30×0.125=12.5."]},{"id":"im129","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, Eq. 2.21, printed pp. 41 (PDF pp. 67)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A homography maps (x,y,1) by H=[[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0.1,0,1]]. For (x,y)=(2,4), enter the Cartesian transformed x-coordinate (within 0.001).","value":1.6666666666666667,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"x′=2/1.2=5/3≈1.666667; y′=4/1.2≈3.333333","explanation":"Matrix multiplication yields homogeneous (2,4,1.2). Perspective division is essential; taking the first two components directly would be wrong.","steps":["w′=0.1×2+1=1.2.","x′=2/1.2=5/3."]},{"id":"im130","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, printed pp. 171-174 (PDF pp. 197-200)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why can inverse warping still alias when it strongly minifies an image?","options":["Inverse warping changes every label randomly","Each output pixel can represent a footprint covering many source pixels, so point interpolation alone may not remove excessive high frequencies","It always leaves unqueried destination pixels","The inverse of a nonsingular matrix does not exist"],"correct":1,"answer":"Each output pixel can represent a footprint covering many source pixels, so point interpolation alone may not remove excessive high frequencies","explanation":"Inverse warping addresses how to obtain a value for each destination pixel, but antialiasing also requires filtering according to the local source footprint."},{"id":"im131","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, Eq. 3.77, printed pp. 173 (PDF pp. 199)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A MIP-map uses octave levels. If one output pixel spans r=8 source-pixel widths, what ideal pyramid level ℓ=log2 r is selected?","value":3,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"ℓ=3","explanation":"Three halvings correspond to an eightfold sampling-rate reduction. Fractional rates can blend adjacent levels.","steps":["8=2³.","ℓ=log2(8)=3."]},{"id":"im132","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, printed pp. 172-174 (PDF pp. 198-200)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A warp stretches x but strongly compresses y. Why can an isotropic blur be a poor antialiasing choice?","options":["It cannot affect any frequency","It is mathematically identical to the optimal oriented filter","It makes every edge circular","It can blur unnecessarily along x while still needing stronger filtering along y"],"correct":3,"answer":"It can blur unnecessarily along x while still needing stronger filtering along y","explanation":"The source footprint is direction-dependent. Anisotropic or elliptical filtering follows the local mapping more closely than one scalar blur radius."},{"id":"im133","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.2, printed pp. 175-177 (PDF pp. 201-203)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Why use a mesh-based warp instead of one global affine transform to raise the corners of a mouth while keeping the rest of a face nearly fixed?","options":["A local warp allows different displacements in different regions","An affine transform has infinitely many independent parameters per pixel","A mesh warp can never move pixels","All face expressions are a single global translation"],"correct":0,"answer":"A local warp allows different displacements in different regions","explanation":"A small global parameter set moves the entire image under one rule. A mesh interpolates control-point displacements and can model localized deformation."},{"id":"im134","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.2, printed pp. 176 (PDF pp. 202)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In a triangular mesh warp, three non-collinear vertex correspondences specify which local transform inside each triangle?","options":["A full 3D rigid transform without projection","An arbitrary 8-DoF homography uniquely","A 2D affine transform","Only a gamma correction"],"correct":2,"answer":"A 2D affine transform","explanation":"An affine transform has six parameters and each 2D point match gives two equations. Three non-collinear matches suffice, and barycentric coordinates provide interpolation within the triangle."},{"id":"im135","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.3, printed pp. 177-178 (PDF pp. 203-204)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"core","prompt":"Why does directly cross-dissolving two faces often show ghosted duplicate features?","options":["Alpha never allows fractional values","Corresponding features are not geometrically aligned before blending","Every face image must have only one pixel","Cross-dissolve is always nonlinear in image values"],"correct":1,"answer":"Corresponding features are not geometrically aligned before blending","explanation":"A blend mixes intensities at the same coordinates even when the features occupy different positions. Morphing aligns features at an intermediate geometry before mixing appearance."},{"id":"im136","topic":"Warping","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.3, printed pp. 177-178 (PDF pp. 203-204)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Describe the main steps for a feature-based morph at time t=0.5 between two images with corresponding landmarks.","answer":"Interpolate each landmark halfway between its positions in the two images. Build a warp from each image into that common intermediate geometry. Resample each image using the warp, then blend the two warped images with weights 0.5 and 0.5. Corresponding features should coincide before the appearance blend.","explanation":"The geometry interpolation and intensity interpolation solve different parts of the problem. Good correspondence and valid nonfolding warps remain necessary.","steps":["Compute common midpoint geometry.","Warp and resample each input toward those midpoint landmarks.","Blend the aligned inputs equally."]},{"id":"im137","topic":"Geometry","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 38 (PDF pp. 64)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 3D line segment joins p=(1,2,3) to q=(5,6,7). For r=(1−λ)p+λq with λ=0.25, enter r's z-coordinate.","value":4,"tolerance":1e-06,"answer":"r=(2,3,4), so z=4","explanation":"Convex interpolation between endpoints gives a point on the segment. The six endpoint coordinates describe a segment; an infinite 3D line itself has four geometric degrees of freedom.","steps":["z=(1−0.25)×3+0.25×7=2.25+1.75=4."]},{"id":"im138","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, region statistics, printed pp. 141 (PDF pp. 167)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A three-pixel set has pixel-center coordinates (0,0),(2,0),(2,3). What is its centroid x-coordinate?","value":1.3333333333333333,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"x̄=4/3≈1.333333; ȳ=1","explanation":"The centroid is the mean of the pixel-center coordinates, treating each listed pixel equally. The set need not be connected to have a centroid.","steps":["x̄=(0+2+2)/3=4/3."]},{"id":"im139","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.3, printed pp. 155-159 (PDF pp. 181-185)","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Ignoring rounding, an infinite octave Gaussian pyramid stores N+N/4+N/16+… samples. Its total approaches:","options":["N/4","N","4N/3","4N"],"correct":2,"answer":"4N/3","explanation":"The geometric series has ratio 1/4 and sum N/(1−1/4)=4N/3. Ordinary pyramids are overcomplete relative to the original image; a finite pyramid stores slightly less."},{"id":"im140","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, printed pp. 134-136 (PDF pp. 160-162)","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Explain how repeated edge-dependent local smoothing relates to anisotropic diffusion, and why unlimited repetition can remove useful structure.","answer":"Each iteration moves a pixel toward nearby values, with reduced coupling across large differences. This resembles a diffusion process with a conductance that depends on local image variation. Repeated smoothing can progressively suppress detail; without a suitable data-fidelity constraint and stopping rule, it need not preserve the original image.","explanation":"The reading links local adaptive averaging to anisotropic diffusion and cautions about unanchored iteration. An edge-stopping conductance should reduce coupling across large differences; the draft's isolated monotonicity wording is inconsistent with its Gaussian range formula."},{"id":"xi001","topic":"Geometry","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36-38 (PDF pp. 62-64)","prompt":"In Cartesian coordinates, intersect the lines x+y−5=0 and 2x−y−1=0. Their homogeneous line vectors are (1,1,−5) and (2,−1,−1). Enter the Cartesian y-coordinate, in coordinate units. Give the exact integer.","value":3,"tolerance":0,"answer":"3 coordinate units","explanation":"The cross product of the line vectors is (−6,−9,−3). Dividing by its last coordinate gives (2,3). Independently, adding the Cartesian equations gives 3x=6, then y=5−2=3. Homogeneous scale and sign do not change this intersection.","steps":["Form the homogeneous intersection l₁×l₂.","Normalize by its nonzero last coordinate.","Check the resulting point in both line equations."]},{"id":"xi002","topic":"Geometry","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 37-38 (PDF pp. 63-64)","prompt":"The plane is 2x−y+2z−6=0. Define positive signed distance in the direction of its normal (2,−1,2). Find the signed Euclidean distance from P=(4,1,2), in coordinate units. Answer within 0.001.","value":1.6666666666666667,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"5/3 ≈ 1.667 coordinate units","explanation":"Substitution gives 2·4−1+2·2−6=5. The normal has length √(4+1+4)=3, so the signed distance is 5/3. Using the unnormalized plane residual alone would incorrectly report 5.","steps":["Evaluate the plane equation at P.","Divide by the normal's Euclidean length.","Retain the sign fixed by the stated normal orientation."]},{"id":"xi003","topic":"Geometry","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36-38 (PDF pp. 62-64)","prompt":"Use Cartesian axes and the Euclidean metric. Orthogonally project P=(4,1) onto the line 2x−y−3=0. Enter the projected point's x-coordinate, in coordinate units, within 0.001.","value":2.4,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"2.4 coordinate units","explanation":"For normal n=(2,−1), the plane residual at P is 4 and n·n=5. The closest point is P−n(4/5)=(2.4,1.8). It lies on the line, and its displacement from P is parallel to n; both conditions verify orthogonal projection.","steps":["Compute r=2·4−1−3=4.","Subtract (r/5)(2,−1) from P.","Check 2·2.4−1.8−3=0."]},{"id":"xi004","topic":"Transforms","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, printed pp. 41-42 (PDF pp. 67-68)","prompt":"A triangle has area 4 square coordinate units. Apply the 2D affine map p′=Ap+t, with A=[[2,1],[0,3]] and any translation t. Find its transformed area in square coordinate units. Give the exact integer.","value":24,"tolerance":0,"answer":"24 square coordinate units","explanation":"An affine map scales all planar areas by |det A|. Here det A=2·3−1·0=6, so the area becomes 6·4=24. Translation moves the triangle without changing its area. The off-diagonal shear term affects shape but does not add another area factor."},{"id":"xi005","topic":"Projection","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.1.2 and 2.1.4, printed pp. 44-45 and 56-58 (PDF pp. 70-71 and 82-84)","prompt":"Use column vectors and world-to-camera extrinsics p_c=Rp_w+t, with R=[[0,−1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,1]] and t=(2,−3,1) metres. Find the camera centre's world x-coordinate in metres. Give the exact integer.","value":3,"tolerance":0,"answer":"3 metres","explanation":"The camera centre C is the world point whose camera coordinates are zero: RC+t=0. Since R is a rotation, C=−Rᵀt=(3,2,−1) metres. The vector t itself is the world origin's camera coordinate, not the camera centre in world coordinates. Checking R(3,2,−1)+t gives zero."},{"id":"xi006","topic":"Transforms","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.3, Rodrigues formula, printed pp. 46-47 (PDF pp. 72-73)","prompt":"In right-handed Cartesian coordinates, actively rotate the column vector v=(1,0,0) by +90° about unit axis n=(0,1/√2,1/√2), using the right-hand rule. Enter the rotated vector's z-component, within 0.001. Components are dimensionless.","value":-0.7071067811865475,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"−1/√2 ≈ −0.707","explanation":"Rodrigues gives Rv=v cos θ+(n×v) sin θ+n(n·v)(1−cos θ). Here n·v=0 and cos 90°=0, so Rv=n×v=(0,1/√2,−1/√2). The negative z-component follows from the stated active rotation and right-handed cross product."},{"id":"xi007","topic":"Projection","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, camera intrinsics, printed pp. 55-59 (PDF pp. 81-85)","prompt":"A calibrated image has horizontal principal point c_x=320 pixels. A crop moves the coordinate origin to old pixel (100,50). Then coordinates are resized by exactly u′=2(u−100), v′=2(v−50); use this stated coordinate rule, with no half-pixel offset. What is the new horizontal principal point in pixels? Give the exact integer.","value":440,"tolerance":0,"answer":"440 pixels","explanation":"The principal point follows the same image-coordinate transform as every projected point. Cropping gives 320−100=220, then resizing gives 2·220=440 pixels. Focal length in horizontal pixel units also doubles, but it does not replace the principal-point offset."},{"id":"xi008","topic":"Projection","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, printed pp. 54-58 (PDF pp. 80-84)","prompt":"An ideal pinhole camera uses u=f_x X/Z+c_x with f_x=800 pixels and c_x=320 pixels, no skew or distortion, Z>0. A point lies on the horizontal centre row at u=720 pixels. Find its horizontal ray angle from the +Z optical axis toward +X, in degrees, within 0.01.","value":26.56505117707799,"tolerance":0.01,"answer":"arctan(0.5) ≈ 26.57°","explanation":"The normalized ray has X/Z=(720−320)/800=0.5. Its angle in the XZ plane is arctan(X/Z), giving about 26.565°. The ratio is not itself an angle in degrees, and the principal-point offset must be removed first."},{"id":"xi009","topic":"Optics","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, thin lens, printed pp. 75-76 (PDF pp. 101-102)","prompt":"A thin lens has f=50 mm. A real object is z_o=250 mm from the lens and has height 15 mm. Use 1/z_o+1/z_i=1/f and the magnitude of transverse magnification |m|=z_i/z_o. Find the focused image's height magnitude in mm, within 0.001.","value":3.75,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"3.75 mm","explanation":"The image distance is z_i=(1/50−1/250)⁻¹=62.5 mm. The magnification magnitude is 62.5/250=0.25, giving 15·0.25=3.75 mm. The real image is inverted, but the question asks for its positive height magnitude."},{"id":"xi010","topic":"Digital imaging","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, sensor noise, printed pp. 83-84 (PDF pp. 109-110)","prompt":"A pixel collects a mean signal of N=900 electrons. Shot noise is Poisson, so its count variance is N. Independent zero-mean read noise has standard deviation 10 electrons. Ignore dark current and quantization. Compute signal-to-noise ratio as mean signal divided by total noise standard deviation, a dimensionless ratio, within 0.01.","value":28.460498941515414,"tolerance":0.01,"answer":"900/√1000 ≈ 28.46","explanation":"Independent noise variances add: total variance is 900+10²=1000. The standard deviation is √1000 electrons, so SNR is 900/√1000≈28.46. Adding standard deviations directly would give the wrong result."},{"id":"xi011","topic":"Digital imaging","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, ADC resolution, printed pp. 83-84 (PDF pp. 109-110)","prompt":"A 10-bit endpoint-inclusive uniform quantizer represents normalized intensity I by integer code q=round(1023I), using nearest-integer rounding. For I=0.6, find q. Give the exact integer code; this value is not a tie.","value":614,"tolerance":0,"answer":"614","explanation":"Ten bits provide 1024 codes numbered 0 through 1023. The unrounded code is 1023·0.6=613.8, whose nearest integer is 614. The largest code is 1023, not 1024; the explicit endpoint convention determines the scale."},{"id":"xi012","topic":"Color and compression","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, gamma and linear intensity, printed pp. 94-96 (PDF pp. 120-122)","prompt":"Define gamma encoding exactly as e=√I for linear-light intensity I∈[0,1]. A stored value is e=0.4. Apply a physical exposure gain of 4 to I, then re-encode with the same rule. No clipping occurs. Find the new dimensionless encoded value, within 0.001.","value":0.8,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"0.8","explanation":"Decode first: I=e²=0.16. Exposure gain gives I′=4·0.16=0.64, then e′=√0.64=0.8. Multiplying the encoded value by 4 would produce 1.6 and does not implement a linear-light exposure change. This exercise uses its stated equation, avoiding the draft's inconsistent inverse-gamma wording."},{"id":"xi013","topic":"Point operators","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.3, printed pp. 113-114 (PDF pp. 139-140)","prompt":"In linear light, top layer A has straight grayscale value F_A=0.8 and alpha α_A=0.5. Lower layer B has F_B=0.2 and α_B=0.4, over a fully transparent background. Composite A over B, then convert the result to straight grayscale by dividing its premultiplied value by its resulting alpha. Find that straight value, dimensionless, within 0.001.","value":0.6285714285714287,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"0.44/0.7 ≈ 0.629","explanation":"Premultiplied values are P_A=0.4 and P_B=0.08. Over gives P=P_A+(1−α_A)P_B=0.44 and α=α_A+(1−α_A)α_B=0.7. The straight value is P/α=0.44/0.7≈0.628571. Using an opaque-background equation would discard the transparency of B."},{"id":"xi014","topic":"Filtering","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2 and 3.4.1, printed pp. 119-122 and 146-147 (PDF pp. 145-148 and 172-173)","prompt":"In a constant region, nine pixels have independent additive noise, each with variance 16 intensity units squared. Average all nine with equal weights 1/9, away from image boundaries. Find the output noise variance in intensity units squared, within 0.001.","value":1.7777777777777777,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"16/9 ≈ 1.778 intensity units squared","explanation":"For independent noise, the variance of a weighted sum is the sum of squared weights times each variance. Thus Var=9·(1/9)²·16=16/9. Noise standard deviation decreases by a factor of 3; variance decreases by a factor of 9."},{"id":"xi015","topic":"Filtering","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.2, Gaussian smoothing, printed pp. 125-126 (PDF pp. 151-152)","prompt":"Apply two normalized continuous Gaussian smoothing kernels in succession, with isotropic standard deviations σ₁=1.5 and σ₂=2 coordinate units. Ignore sampling, truncation and boundaries. What is the equivalent single Gaussian's standard deviation, in coordinate units, within 0.001?","value":2.5,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"2.5 coordinate units","explanation":"Convolution of normalized Gaussians adds their variances, not their standard deviations. The equivalent variance is 1.5²+2²=6.25, giving σ=√6.25=2.5. The stated continuous, untruncated model makes this relationship exact."},{"id":"xi016","topic":"Filtering","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.3, recursive filtering, printed pp. 130-131 (PDF pp. 156-157)","prompt":"A causal 1D recursive smoother is g[n]=0.8g[n−1]+0.2f[n], with g[−1]=0. For f[0]=10, f[1]=0, f[2]=0 intensity units, compute g[2] in intensity units, within 0.001. Process samples in increasing n.","value":1.28,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"1.28 intensity units","explanation":"The recursion gives g[0]=2, g[1]=1.6 and g[2]=1.28. The response persists after the nonzero input sample because earlier output is fed back. Evaluating every location against the initial zero state would incorrectly erase this memory."},{"id":"xi017","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, printed pp. 138-139 (PDF pp. 164-165)","prompt":"On the infinite 1D integer grid, foreground pixels are exactly {0,1,2,5}, with background everywhere else. Use symmetric structuring element S={−1,0,1}. Perform binary opening: erosion by S followed by dilation by S. How many foreground pixels remain? Give the exact integer count.","value":3,"tolerance":0,"answer":"3 foreground pixels","explanation":"Erosion retains only centre 1, because only its three-pixel neighborhood is entirely foreground. Dilation of {1} restores {0,1,2}. The isolated pixel 5 is removed, leaving three pixels. The infinite-grid convention removes any border ambiguity."},{"id":"xi018","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, printed pp. 133-134 (PDF pp. 159-160)","prompt":"For one bilateral-filter output, the centre intensity is 100 and the selected neighborhood values are [100,110,140] intensity units. All three specified spatial weights are 1. Range weights are exp(−(v−100)²/(2σ_r²)), with σ_r=10 intensity units. Normalize by the sum of the combined weights. Find the output in intensity units, within 0.01.","value":103.78296923292419,"tolerance":0.01,"answer":"Approximately 103.78 intensity units","explanation":"The weights are 1, exp(−0.5) and exp(−8). The normalized result is [100+110exp(−0.5)+140exp(−8)]/[1+exp(−0.5)+exp(−8)]≈103.782969. The distant intensity 140 contributes very little even though its spatial weight is equal to the others."},{"id":"xi019","topic":"Fourier","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, filter frequency response, printed pp. 142-145 (PDF pp. 168-171)","prompt":"On an infinite 1D grid, let f[n]=cos(πn/2), with input amplitude 1 and spatial frequency 1/4 cycle per sample. Apply the centred box average g[n]=(f[n−1]+f[n]+f[n+1])/3. Find the output sinusoid's nonnegative amplitude, dimensionless, within 0.001.","value":0.3333333333333333,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"1/3 ≈ 0.333","explanation":"For a sinusoid at angular frequency ω, this symmetric filter has response H(ω)=[1+2cos ω]/3. At ω=π/2, H=1/3. Equivalently, averaging the periodic input [1,0,−1,0] yields [1/3,0,−1/3,0]. No boundary treatment or phase shift is involved."},{"id":"xi020","topic":"Fourier","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, DFT and shifting, printed pp. 144-145 (PDF pp. 170-171)","prompt":"Use the unnormalized forward DFT F[k]=Σ from n=0 to 7 of f[n]exp(−j2πkn/8). Define a circular delay g[n]=f[(n−2) mod 8]. Assume F[1]≠0. Find the phase angle of G[1]/F[1] in radians, in the interval (−π,π], within 0.001.","value":-1.5707963267948966,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"−π/2 ≈ −1.571 radians","explanation":"A circular delay by 2 multiplies bin k by exp(−j2πk·2/8). At k=1 the multiplier is exp(−jπ/2)=−j, whose principal phase is −π/2. Delay and advance have opposite signs under the stated forward-DFT convention."},{"id":"xi021","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.4, wavelet analysis and synthesis, printed pp. 159-163 (PDF pp. 185-189)","prompt":"For this two-sample orthonormal wavelet calculation, define a=(x₀+x₁)/√2 and d=(x₀−x₁)/√2. The coefficients are a=5√2 and d=√2 intensity units. Reconstruct x₀ in intensity units. Give the exact integer.","value":6,"tolerance":0,"answer":"6 intensity units","explanation":"Adding the two analysis equations gives a+d=√2x₀. Therefore x₀=(a+d)/√2=6; x₁=(a−d)/√2=4. The explicit orthonormal normalization distinguishes these coefficients from an average-and-difference convention."},{"id":"xi022","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.5.3 and 3.5.5, printed pp. 157 and 165-167 (PDF pp. 183 and 191-193)","prompt":"At one pixel of a two-level Laplacian blend, fine residuals are L_A=6 and L_B=−2. The expanded coarsest images have values E(G_A)=100 and E(G_B)=140. Use A-weight 0.75 for the fine residual, but A-weight 0.25 for the coarse contribution; each B-weight is 1 minus its A-weight. Expansion is linear. Reconstruct the output value in intensity units, without clipping, within 0.001.","value":134,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"134 intensity units","explanation":"The blended fine residual is 0.75·6+0.25·(−2)=4. The expanded coarse contribution is 0.25·100+0.75·140=130. Laplacian synthesis adds these terms, giving 134. Multiband blending can use different mask weights at different spatial scales."},{"id":"xi023","topic":"Warping","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.2, triangular affine warps, printed pp. 175-176 (PDF pp. 201-202)","prompt":"Use Cartesian image coordinates, x right and y up. A triangular warp maps source vertices (0,0),(2,0),(0,2) to destination vertices (1,1),(5,1),(1,7), respectively, using the affine map within the triangle. For source point (0.5,0.5), enter the destination y-coordinate in coordinate units, within 0.001.","value":2.5,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"2.5 coordinate units","explanation":"The source point has barycentric weights (0.5,0.25,0.25). An affine map preserves these weights, so destination y=0.5·1+0.25·1+0.25·7=2.5. Equivalently, the local y-map is y′=1+3y."},{"id":"xi024","topic":"Warping","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, trilinear MIP-mapping, printed pp. 173 (PDF pp. 199)","prompt":"An octave MIP-map uses fractional level ℓ=log₂r, with r measured in source-pixel widths per output-pixel width. Here r=√8. Samples at the corresponding location in levels 1 and 2 are 20 and 44 intensity units. Trilinearly blend those level samples using the fractional part of ℓ. Find the output in intensity units, within 0.001.","value":32,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"32 intensity units","explanation":"Because √8=2^(3/2), the level is ℓ=1.5. Its fractional part is 0.5, so the level blend is (1−0.5)·20+0.5·44=32. This blends already-resampled level values; it is distinct from choosing the nearest integer level."},{"id":"xi025","topic":"Geometry","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, homogeneous points and lines, printed pp. 36-38 (PDF pp. 62-64)","prompt":"Two nearly parallel image lines intersect at homogeneous point (a,b,w), with w very close to zero after a fixed normalization. Why can the reported finite Cartesian intersection be highly uncertain?","options":["Every cross product discards the lines' intercepts.","Homogeneous coordinates forbid distant finite points.","Dividing by a very small w can amplify small line-measurement errors.","The intersection must be the Cartesian origin whenever w is small."],"correct":2,"answer":"Dividing by a very small w can amplify small line-measurement errors.","explanation":"Cartesian coordinates are (a/w,b/w). Near parallelism makes their denominator small, so slight measurement changes can produce large changes in the finite intersection. An exactly zero w describes a point at infinity; a small nonzero w does not make the point invalid."},{"id":"xi026","topic":"Transforms","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.1 and 2.1.2, printed pp. 38-39 and 43-45 (PDF pp. 64-65 and 69-71)","prompt":"A 3D affine map has homogeneous matrix [[A,t],[0ᵀ,1]]. Apply it to the point at infinity (d_x,d_y,d_z,0). What is its transformed homogeneous representation?","options":["(Ad,0), so translation does not affect the direction.","(Ad+t,1), so every direction becomes a finite point.","(d+t,0), so only translation affects the direction.","(Ad,t_x+t_y+t_z), so its last coordinate depends on translation."],"correct":0,"answer":"(Ad,0), so translation does not affect the direction.","explanation":"The translation column is multiplied by the last coordinate, zero. The result is therefore (Ad,0). This explains why translating a coordinate frame does not change a line's direction, while the linear part can rotate, scale or shear it."},{"id":"xi027","topic":"Transforms","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.1.2-2.1.3, printed pp. 44-47 (PDF pp. 70-73)","prompt":"A student calls A=[[1,1,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]] a proper 3D rotation because det A=1. Which observation rejects that claim?","options":["A proper rotation must have all diagonal entries zero.","Every proper rotation must have determinant −1.","A cannot rotate points without a nonzero translation vector.","AᵀA≠I; its columns are not an orthonormal basis, so it changes some lengths and angles."],"correct":3,"answer":"AᵀA≠I; its columns are not an orthonormal basis, so it changes some lengths and angles.","explanation":"A rotation requires both RᵀR=I and det R=+1. Here A is a shear: it maps (0,1,0) to (1,1,0), increasing its length from 1 to √2. Unit determinant controls volume, not preservation of all Euclidean distances."},{"id":"xi028","topic":"Transforms","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, projective transformations, printed pp. 41-42 (PDF pp. 67-68)","prompt":"A nonsingular homography is H=[[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0.2,0.5,1]], acting on column vector (x,y,1). What happens to finite source points on 0.2x+0.5y+1=0?","options":["They all map to the finite point (0,0).","Their transformed last coordinate is zero, so they map to the destination line at infinity.","They are unaffected because H has determinant 1.","They prove that H is singular and cannot transform any other points."],"correct":1,"answer":"Their transformed last coordinate is zero, so they map to the destination line at infinity.","explanation":"The transformed vector is (x,y,0.2x+0.5y+1). On the stated line its last coordinate vanishes, so there is no finite Cartesian division. H remains nonsingular: projective transformations can send a finite line to the line at infinity."},{"id":"xi029","topic":"Projection","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, perspective and parallax, printed pp. 54-62 (PDF pp. 80-88)","prompt":"An ideal camera translates horizontally by b metres with no rotation, fixed intrinsics, and unchanged optical-axis direction. Static points have depths 2 m and 8 m. Under u=fX/Z, how do the magnitudes of their horizontal image displacements compare?","options":["The 2 m point moves four times as far in pixels as the 8 m point.","The 8 m point moves four times as far in pixels as the 2 m point.","Both points move equally because the camera translation is the same.","Neither moves because only camera rotation changes image coordinates."],"correct":0,"answer":"The 2 m point moves four times as far in pixels as the 8 m point.","explanation":"Camera translation changes each point's camera X coordinate by −b while preserving Z, giving Δu=−fb/Z. Thus displacement magnitude is inversely proportional to depth, and the ratio is 8/2=4. This depth-dependent parallax prevents a single 2D translation from aligning a general scene."},{"id":"xi030","topic":"Projection","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, calibration matrix, printed pp. 55-58 (PDF pp. 81-84)","prompt":"A calibration convention uses K=[[800,20,320],[0,600,240],[0,0,1]] on normalized ray (x,y,1). Changing y while holding x fixed changes the pixel u coordinate. Which intrinsic term causes this coupling?","options":["The horizontal principal point 320, because it multiplies y.","The vertical focal length 600, because it appears in the first row.","The skew term 20, because u=800x+20y+320.","A world-to-camera translation hidden in the last row of K."],"correct":2,"answer":"The skew term 20, because u=800x+20y+320.","explanation":"The first row directly gives u=800x+20y+320. The off-diagonal skew coefficient couples normalized vertical position into horizontal pixel position. Principal points add constants, and extrinsic translation belongs outside the intrinsic matrix."},{"id":"xi031","topic":"Photometry","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, Lambertian shading, printed pp. 70-72 (PDF pp. 96-98)","prompt":"Use I=ρL max(0,n·l), with known unit directions, no ambient or specular term, and unclipped measurements. Which joint change preserves every intensity wherever the new albedo remains physically allowed?","options":["Multiply both ρ and L by 2.","Multiply ρ by 2 and divide L by 2.","Divide both ρ and L by 2.","Add the same positive constant to both ρ and L."],"correct":1,"answer":"Multiply ρ by 2 and divide L by 2.","explanation":"The observations depend on the product ρL, so (2ρ)(L/2)=ρL. Even with a known surface normal, unknown albedo and unknown light strength are not separately identified by this product alone. Physical albedo bounds can restrict possible scales but do not generally give a unique decomposition."},{"id":"xi032","topic":"Photometry","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.2.1-2.2.2, lighting and visibility, printed pp. 66-74 (PDF pp. 92-100)","prompt":"A patch faces a point light with n·l>0, but another object completely blocks that source. A model includes only that direct source, with no ambient or indirect illumination. What change is needed to avoid predicting a brightly lit patch?","options":["Replace the viewing direction with the light direction in every term.","Increase the Phong exponent until the diffuse term vanishes.","Force the patch normal to point away from the source.","Apply a source-visibility factor of zero to its direct illumination contribution."],"correct":3,"answer":"Apply a source-visibility factor of zero to its direct illumination contribution.","explanation":"The cosine term describes incidence angle, not whether light can reach the patch. A blocked source has visibility zero, eliminating its direct contribution while keeping the actual surface normal. Indirect or ambient terms could still illuminate a real shadow, but they are excluded by this question's model."},{"id":"xi033","topic":"Optics","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, aperture and diffraction, printed pp. 76-79 (PDF pp. 102-105)","prompt":"At fixed focal length, wavelength, shutter time and scene, double the f-number N of an ideal diffraction-limited lens. Use diffraction spot diameter proportional to N and image irradiance proportional to 1/N². Which pair of changes follows?","options":["Spot diameter halves; irradiance quadruples.","Spot diameter is unchanged; irradiance halves.","Spot diameter doubles; irradiance becomes one quarter.","Spot diameter doubles; irradiance stays unchanged."],"correct":2,"answer":"Spot diameter doubles; irradiance becomes one quarter.","explanation":"Doubling N halves aperture diameter d=f/N. Diffraction spreads light more widely, while the aperture area and irradiance fall by a factor of four. Stopping down can improve depth of field, yet it does not improve diffraction-limited sharpness indefinitely."},{"id":"xi034","topic":"Digital imaging","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, sensing and limited range, printed pp. 81-84 (PDF pp. 107-110)","prompt":"Several different bright scene radiances saturate the same RAW sensor channel and are all stored as its maximum value. Why can reducing that stored value in software fail to recover highlight texture?","options":["Lowering any image value always introduces motion blur.","RAW data uses no numerical representation, so software cannot edit it.","A gain of less than one reverses sensor saturation exactly.","Saturation is a many-to-one mapping, so the distinct original radiances were not retained."],"correct":3,"answer":"Saturation is a many-to-one mapping, so the distinct original radiances were not retained.","explanation":"Scaling the clipped maximum produces a darker flat region, not the missing radiance differences. A shorter exposure can preserve those differences at capture time if other limits permit. This is different from merely displaying otherwise unclipped RAW data with a dark tone curve."},{"id":"xi035","topic":"Digital imaging","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.1, printed pp. 84-86 (PDF pp. 110-112)","prompt":"Sample zero-phase continuous cosines cos(2π·60t) and cos(2π·40t) at t=n/100 for every integer n, without a prefilter. Frequencies are cycles per second. What is true of the two sample sequences?","options":["They are identical because the 60 Hz cosine aliases to 40 Hz at this sampling rate.","They are distinct because 60 Hz is greater than 40 Hz.","They become distinguishable by low-pass filtering only the already-stored samples.","Both become all-zero sequences because neither frequency is a DFT DC term."],"correct":0,"answer":"They are identical because the 60 Hz cosine aliases to 40 Hz at this sampling rate.","explanation":"At the sample locations, cos(1.2πn)=cos(2πn−0.8πn)=cos(0.8πn). The measurements cannot distinguish those continuous inputs. A suitable capture-time prefilter removes the out-of-band 60 Hz component before this ambiguity is introduced."},{"id":"xi036","topic":"Color and compression","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.3, chroma subsampling, printed pp. 98-100 (PDF pp. 124-126)","prompt":"Thin colored lettering has almost the same luma as its background but a large chroma difference. Why can chroma subsampling damage its edges even when the luma channel is retained at full resolution?","options":["Subsampling chroma must increase the spatial resolution of all color channels.","The lettering's edge information is concentrated in high-frequency chroma, which the subsampling removes or averages.","Full-resolution luma uniquely determines all missing chroma values.","Chroma subsampling changes only file metadata, so any visible difference is unrelated."],"correct":1,"answer":"The lettering's edge information is concentrated in high-frequency chroma, which the subsampling removes or averages.","explanation":"Retaining luma protects brightness detail, but a nearly equal-luma color boundary relies on chroma detail. Lower-resolution chroma cannot represent arbitrarily thin colored structures. The usual perceptual benefit of prioritizing luma is a statistical tradeoff, not a guarantee for every image."},{"id":"xi037","topic":"Color and compression","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, RGB gamut, printed pp. 89-91 (PDF pp. 115-117)","prompt":"A physically valid XYZ color transforms to linear RGB=(−0.1,0.4,0.2) in a particular display-primary basis. Assume the conversion is correct. What does the negative component mean?","options":["The source spectrum contains a negative number of photons.","The color must be neutral gray because two components are positive.","Applying gamma encoding will allow the display to emit a negative red primary.","That color lies outside the gamut obtainable by nonnegative mixtures of those display primaries."],"correct":3,"answer":"That color lies outside the gamut obtainable by nonnegative mixtures of those display primaries.","explanation":"A coordinate basis can assign negative coefficients to valid colors lying outside its positive-primary cone. The display cannot physically emit a negative primary, so gamut mapping or clipping changes the desired color. Negative RGB coordinates do not make the original XYZ color physically invalid."},{"id":"xi038","topic":"Point operators","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.4, printed pp. 115-118 (PDF pp. 141-144)","prompt":"Two grayscale images have exactly the same histogram but different spatial arrangements. Apply the same deterministic global histogram-equalization convention separately to each. Which statement is justified?","options":["Their pixel arrays must become identical after equalization.","They use the same intensity lookup table and have the same output histogram, but their spatial structures can remain different.","One image must use a different lookup table because its edges are in different locations.","Equalization reconstructs the spatial arrangement discarded by the input histogram."],"correct":1,"answer":"They use the same intensity lookup table and have the same output histogram, but their spatial structures can remain different.","explanation":"A global equalization map depends on intensity counts, not pixel locations. Equal histograms therefore produce the same map and output counts. Mapping values at their original locations does not rearrange pixels or make their geometric structure equal."},{"id":"xi039","topic":"Point operators","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.1.3 and 3.5.1, printed pp. 113-114 and 150-151 (PDF pp. 139-140 and 176-177)","prompt":"In linear light, interpolate halfway between opaque red RGBA=(1,0,0,1) and transparent black RGBA=(0,0,0,0), then composite over opaque black. Which procedure avoids darkening the red edge through its invisible neighbor's straight RGB value?","options":["Interpolate only alpha and keep both endpoint straight RGB values unchanged.","Set the transparent endpoint's alpha to 1 before interpolation.","Premultiply RGB by alpha before interpolation; interpolate premultiplied RGB and alpha, then use the premultiplied over operation.","Interpolate straight RGB and alpha separately, then multiply by alpha twice."],"correct":2,"answer":"Premultiply RGB by alpha before interpolation; interpolate premultiplied RGB and alpha, then use the premultiplied over operation.","explanation":"Correct halfway premultiplied values are P=(0.5,0,0), α=0.5, giving red 0.5 over black. Separate straight interpolation gives F=(0.5,0,0) and α=0.5, then contributes only red 0.25. Premultiplication prevents invisible endpoint RGB from creating this dark fringe."},{"id":"xi040","topic":"Filtering","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2 and 3.2.2, printed pp. 120-122 and 126-127 (PDF pp. 146-148 and 152-153)","prompt":"Away from boundaries, correlate an unclipped 1D image with kernel [−1,0,1]. Add a constant c to every input sample before filtering. What happens to the derivative response?","options":["It stays unchanged because the kernel coefficients sum to zero.","It increases by c because every filter must pass DC with unit gain.","It reverses sign because correlation always negates an intensity offset.","It becomes zero at every location, regardless of the original image."],"correct":0,"answer":"It stays unchanged because the kernel coefficients sum to zero.","explanation":"The added contribution is cΣh=c(−1+0+1)=0. Zero-sum derivative kernels suppress uniform brightness offsets. Clipping or a boundary convention that introduces an unequal outside offset can break this simple cancellation; both are excluded here."},{"id":"xi041","topic":"Filtering","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2.1, SVD filter decomposition, printed pp. 124-125 (PDF pp. 150-151)","prompt":"A 2D convolution kernel is exactly K=u₁v₁ᵀ+u₂v₂ᵀ, with both terms nonzero and rank K=2. Ignore boundaries. How can its convolution be computed exactly using separable operations?","options":["Use only the first term; rank 2 guarantees it equals the full kernel.","Compute one horizontal-vertical convolution for each outer product and add their outputs.","Apply the two outer-product filters successively, multiplying their frequency responses.","Transpose K before filtering, which always reduces its rank to 1."],"correct":1,"answer":"Compute one horizontal-vertical convolution for each outer product and add their outputs.","explanation":"Each outer product is separable, and convolution is linear in the kernel: f*K=f*(u₁v₁ᵀ)+f*(u₂v₂ᵀ). Sequential application would convolve the kernels with each other instead of summing them. Keeping just one term would generally be an approximation."},{"id":"xi042","topic":"Filtering","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.1.1 and 3.2, printed pp. 111-112 and 119-122 (PDF pp. 137-138 and 145-148)","prompt":"For the pair of intensities [0,2], define clipping as clip(x,0,1) and averaging as the arithmetic mean of the pair. What is the ordered pair of scalar outputs (clip each value then average, average then clip)?","options":["(1,1)","(0,1)","(0.5,0.5)","(0.5,1)"],"correct":3,"answer":"(0.5,1)","explanation":"Clipping first changes the pair to [0,1], whose mean is 0.5. Averaging first gives 1, which remains 1 after clipping. Clipping is nonlinear, so it cannot generally be commuted through a linear filter. The order of image-processing stages can change the result."},{"id":"xi043","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.3, printed pp. 138-139 (PDF pp. 164-165)","prompt":"A large binary foreground region contains a single background-pixel hole, far from its outer boundary. Using a symmetric 3×3 all-ones structuring element, which two-stage morphological operation fills this small hole and then contracts the temporary expansion?","options":["Erosion followed by erosion.","Erosion followed by dilation, which is opening.","Dilation followed by erosion, which is closing.","A pointwise inversion followed by another pointwise inversion."],"correct":2,"answer":"Dilation followed by erosion, which is closing.","explanation":"Dilation fills the hole and expands the foreground. The following erosion contracts the expanded region while leaving the filled interior hole foreground. Opening targets small foreground protrusions or isolated foreground elements instead. The distant-boundary assumption isolates the hole behavior."},{"id":"xi044","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, range-kernel tradeoffs, printed pp. 133-136 (PDF pp. 159-162)","prompt":"In a flat but noisy patch, an ordinary bilateral filter includes its centre with positive spatial weight. Its range standard deviation is made much smaller than the typical differences caused by noise. All selected noisy intensities are distinct. Which failure is likely?","options":["Almost every neighbor is rejected by the range kernel, so the normalized output stays near the noisy centre and denoising is weak.","The filter automatically estimates the clean constant intensity exactly from one pixel.","The range kernel becomes constant, so the result is a strong spatial blur.","All intensities must become zero because small standard deviation removes normalization."],"correct":0,"answer":"Almost every neighbor is rejected by the range kernel, so the normalized output stays near the noisy centre and denoising is weak.","explanation":"A very narrow range kernel treats noise-induced intensity differences like real edges. The centre has zero range difference and retains weight, while distinct neighbors receive negligible weight. Strong edge preservation can therefore prevent averaging the very noise the filter is meant to remove."},{"id":"xi045","topic":"Fourier","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.4-3.4.2, filtering and restoration, printed pp. 142-149 (PDF pp. 168-175)","prompt":"At one frequency a known blur has transfer magnitude |H|=0.001. The observed Fourier coefficient contains additive measurement noise. Why is direct inverse filtering by division by H unstable?","options":["Division by H removes noise while leaving the attenuated signal unchanged.","A small H means that the original signal at that frequency was necessarily zero.","The inverse multiplies both signal and measurement noise by about 1000; attenuation alone does not distinguish them.","The DFT coefficient is always real, so division by a small complex number is prohibited."],"correct":2,"answer":"The inverse multiplies both signal and measurement noise by about 1000; attenuation alone does not distinguish them.","explanation":"For Y=HX+N, dividing gives Y/H=X+N/H. The noise term is strongly amplified when H is small, and a zero H contains no recoverable observation of that frequency. Regularized or statistical restoration balances recovery against amplification using additional assumptions."},{"id":"xi046","topic":"Fourier","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4.1, two-dimensional Fourier transforms, printed pp. 146-147 (PDF pp. 172-173)","prompt":"Use an N×N periodic image f[x,y]=cos(2πky/N), where x runs horizontally, y vertically, and integer 0<k<N/2. In frequency coordinates (k_x,k_y), where are its two nonzero DFT peaks?","options":["At (0,k) and (0,−k), with frequency indices interpreted modulo N.","At (k,0) and (−k,0), because the stripes run horizontally.","Only at (0,0), because the image is constant along x.","Uniformly on a circle of radius k, because a cosine has no orientation."],"correct":0,"answer":"At (0,k) and (0,−k), with frequency indices interpreted modulo N.","explanation":"The image varies only in y, so its horizontal frequency is zero. Expanding the cosine into two complex exponentials gives vertical frequencies +k and −k. Horizontal stripe direction is perpendicular to the direction in which the intensity varies."},{"id":"xi047","topic":"Warping","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.1, resampling and multi-pass transforms, printed pp. 170-175 (PDF pp. 196-201)","prompt":"Two invertible affine adjustments are needed, first T₁ then T₂. What approach generally reduces extra interpolation blur from repeatedly sampling the image?","options":["Round every intermediate coordinate to the nearest integer before applying T₂.","Compose the coordinate map T₂T₁, inverse-map the final grid to the original image, and resample once with appropriate filtering for the combined map.","Average T₁ and T₂ elementwise and treat that average as the composition.","Skip antialias filtering whenever two maps have been composed."],"correct":1,"answer":"Compose the coordinate map T₂T₁, inverse-map the final grid to the original image, and resample once with appropriate filtering for the combined map.","explanation":"Coordinate transformations can be composed before touching pixel values. This avoids applying an interpolation kernel to an already-interpolated raster. The final sampling still needs a reconstruction kernel and suitable antialias filtering wherever the combined map minifies the source."},{"id":"xi048","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5.3, Laplacian synthesis, printed pp. 157-159 (PDF pp. 183-185)","prompt":"During Laplacian-pyramid reconstruction, add constant error b to every pixel of the coarsest Gaussian image, while keeping all residual bands exact. Every expansion stage is linear and preserves constants; ignore boundaries. What error appears in the final reconstructed image?","options":["No error, because every residual is a high-pass image.","Error only at the source location of one coarsest pixel.","A spatial derivative of b, which is always zero.","A constant offset b throughout the reconstructed image."],"correct":3,"answer":"A constant offset b throughout the reconstructed image.","explanation":"At each synthesis level, the residual is added to the expanded coarse image. With exact residuals, only the coarse error propagates. Expansion preserves the constant b, so every finer level retains that same offset. Exact residuals do not correct an erroneous base image."},{"id":"xi049","topic":"Transforms","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, homography model and degrees of freedom, printed pp. 41-42 (PDF pp. 67-68)","prompt":"Four source points (0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(3,0) must map to themselves under a nonsingular 2D homography. A student says four correspondences determine the homography uniquely. Give a family of distinct homographies satisfying these correspondences and explain the missing geometric constraint.","answer":"H_s=diag(1,s,1), for any nonzero s, fixes all four points. Their collinearity leaves the off-line behavior unconstrained; four correspondences require a nondegenerate configuration, not merely a count of eight equations.","explanation":"Every listed point has y=0, so scaling its y-coordinate by any nonzero s has no effect. H_s is nonsingular, and different s give different mappings elsewhere. The nominal two constraints per correspondence need not be independent. For a general four-point homography setup, a configuration with no three collinear avoids this particular degeneracy."},{"id":"xi050","topic":"Transforms","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.1, rigid transforms and composition, printed pp. 40-42 (PDF pp. 66-68)","prompt":"Using column vectors, derive the affine expression for rotating 2D points by R about a pivot c rather than about the origin. State the order of homogeneous translation/rotation factors and verify that the pivot remains fixed.","answer":"p′=R(p−c)+c=Rp+(c−Rc). The homogeneous composition is T(c)R_hT(−c), acting right to left. Substituting p=c gives p′=c.","explanation":"Translate the pivot to the origin, rotate, then translate back. The translation term is c−Rc, not merely c. Writing Rp+c generally rotates about the origin and moves the pivot; checking p=c exposes that mistake."},{"id":"xi051","topic":"Projection","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1.4, scaled orthography, printed pp. 51-54 (PDF pp. 77-80)","prompt":"Perspective gives u=fX/(Z₀+δ), while a scaled-orthographic approximation uses û=fX/Z₀. Assume X≠0, Z₀>0, Z₀+δ>0. Derive the relative coordinate error |û−u|/|u|, and use it to express when a 5% error limit is satisfied.","answer":"|û−u|/|u|=|δ|/Z₀. Therefore this coordinate error is at most 5% when |δ|≤0.05Z₀.","explanation":"Subtract the two expressions to obtain û−u=fXδ/[Z₀(Z₀+δ)]. Divide its magnitude by |u|=|fX|/(Z₀+δ); the focal length, X and actual depth cancel, leaving |δ|/Z₀. The result quantifies the shallow-depth condition for weak perspective. The X≠0 condition prevents a zero denominator in this relative error."},{"id":"xi052","topic":"Photometry","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.2, diffuse and ambient shading terms, printed pp. 70-72 (PDF pp. 96-98)","prompt":"Use a normalized diffuse-plus-ambient model I_j=ρc_j+a, where c_j is known cosine illumination and ρ,a are unchanged between shots. Measurements are (c₁,I₁)=(0.8,0.5) and (c₂,I₂)=(0.4,0.3), all dimensionless and unclipped. Find ρ and a, then explain why two shots with the same c cannot generally identify both.","answer":"ρ=(0.5−0.3)/(0.8−0.4)=0.5 and a=0.1. If both cosines are equal, both equations have the same coefficient row and constrain only the combination ρc+a.","explanation":"Subtracting the observations removes the ambient offset and isolates ρ. Substitution into either equation then gives a. Distinct known illumination cosines supply independent constraints; repeated identical cosine illumination does not separate an unknown slope from an unknown intercept."},{"id":"xi053","topic":"Optics","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, focus and blur circles, printed pp. 75-77 (PDF pp. 101-103)","prompt":"For an axial point and an ideal thin lens of aperture diameter d, let the fixed sensor distance be s and the point's focused image distance be z_i. Use blur-circle diameter c=d|1−s/z_i|. Assume the observed c permits two positive z_i values corresponding to real objects beyond the focal distance. Explain why one unsigned blur diameter cannot identify whether the point focuses in front of or behind the sensor, and give one additional measurement that can resolve the ambiguity.","answer":"The absolute value loses the sign of 1−s/z_i. The same c can give z_i=s/(1+c/d) or z_i=s/(1−c/d), on opposite sides of s when 0<c<d. A second image with a different known focus/sensor setting can distinguish the candidates under the same lens model.","explanation":"Defocus magnitude records the distance from ideal focus without its sign. Both candidate image distances are physically allowed by the prompt, so a single measured circle size does not identify the branch. Comparing the predicted blur sizes at a second known focus setting supplies another constraint, assuming a static scene and consistent blur measurement."},{"id":"xi054","topic":"Digital imaging","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3, exposure and sensor noise, printed pp. 81-84 (PDF pp. 107-110)","prompt":"A static scene yields mean N electrons per short exposure. Shot-noise variance is N and each independent read adds variance σ_r². Compare summing n short exposures with one exposure n times as long, at equal aperture and equal total expected signal. Ignore saturation, motion, dark current and quantization. Derive each total variance and explain when the long exposure has an SNR advantage.","answer":"Both signals have mean nN. The sum of n short reads has variance nN+nσ_r²; the one long read has variance nN+σ_r². For n>1 and nonzero read noise, the long exposure has lower variance and higher SNR. In the shot-noise-only limit they are equal.","explanation":"Independent Poisson counts add, so both capture strategies have the same total shot-noise variance nN. Reading the sensor n times adds n independent read-noise variances, whereas the long exposure incurs one. The excluded effects explain why this model result is not a universal recommendation for every photographic scene."},{"id":"xi055","topic":"Color and compression","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3.2, spectral color and white balance, printed pp. 87-94 (PDF pp. 113-120)","prompt":"A diagonal RGB white-balance transform makes one neutral reference patch neutral under a new illuminant. Why does that not guarantee that all colored materials now match their appearance under the old illuminant? State the underlying limitation of three channel gains.","answer":"Each sensor channel integrates the product of illuminant spectrum, material reflectance and sensor sensitivity. Changing the illuminant changes different materials differently. Gains fitted to a neutral patch correct three channel responses for that reference but are only an approximation to full spectral color constancy.","explanation":"The neutral patch constrains channel scaling, not every material spectrum. Broad, overlapping channel sensitivities and differing reflectances prevent a general illumination change from always being represented by one diagonal RGB matrix. Materials that matched under one illuminant can also cease matching under another because of metamerism."},{"id":"xi056","topic":"Point operators","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1.3, matting equation, printed pp. 113-114 (PDF pp. 139-140)","prompt":"For one linear-light RGB pixel, C=αF+(1−α)B. The composite C and background B are known, but foreground RGB F and scalar alpha α are unknown. Explain why the pixel alone generally does not determine both. Demonstrate with C=(0.5,0.5,0.5), B=(0,0,0), and give two physically valid (F,α) solutions.","answer":"There are three observed channel equations but four unknown scalars. Both F=(1,1,1), α=0.5 and F=(0.5,0.5,0.5), α=1 give the stated C. Foreground-color information, known opaque/transparent regions or spatial priors are needed to resolve the ambiguity.","explanation":"Known background does not reveal whether the half-gray observation came from a translucent white foreground or an opaque gray foreground. Both use F and α in [0,1]. Matting therefore needs information beyond an isolated composite pixel; alpha is not simply equal to its intensity."},{"id":"xi057","topic":"Filtering","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §§3.2 and 3.4.1, printed pp. 119-122 and 146-147 (PDF pp. 145-148 and 172-173)","prompt":"Let e[n] be independent zero-mean input noise with variance σ² on an infinite 1D grid. Filter it by y[n]=(e[n−1]+e[n]+e[n+1])/3. Derive Cov(y[n],y[n+1]) and explain why the output noise is no longer independent even though its variance is reduced.","answer":"The adjacent averages share e[n] and e[n+1], so Cov(y[n],y[n+1])=2σ²/9. Each output variance is σ²/3, giving adjacent correlation coefficient 2/3 when σ²>0. Overlapping averaging windows create correlated output noise.","explanation":"Expand the product of the two zero-mean averages. Cross-terms involving distinct independent noise samples have expectation zero, while the two shared samples each contribute σ²/9. Lower marginal variance does not imply independent neighboring errors; both outputs partly reuse the same random measurements."},{"id":"xi058","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3.2, joint bilateral filtering, printed pp. 134 and 136-137 (PDF pp. 160 and 162-163)","prompt":"A joint bilateral filter B_h uses an external guide h for all range weights and normalizes by weights determined only by h and position. Hold that guide fixed while varying the filtered image f. Is B_h linear in f? Is it generally shift invariant? Contrast this with an ordinary bilateral filter that uses f itself as the guide.","answer":"With h fixed, B_h is linear in f: B_h(af+bg)=aB_h(f)+bB_h(g). It is generally not shift invariant because the fixed guide creates position-dependent weights. Ordinary bilateral filtering is generally nonlinear because changing f also changes its weights and normalization.","explanation":"For a fixed guide, each output is a fixed weighted sum of input intensities; its denominator is independent of f. This gives superposition even though the weights may vary spatially. In the ordinary bilateral case, scaling or adding images can alter intensity differences used to compute range weights, so that superposition does not hold in general."},{"id":"xi059","topic":"Fourier","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, DFT shifting property, printed pp. 144-145 (PDF pp. 170-171)","prompt":"Use an unnormalized forward DFT with negative exponential and circular indexing. Explain why Fourier magnitude alone does not generally determine an image's spatial placement. Demonstrate with a 1D unit impulse at sample 0 and another at sample 1 in an N-sample array, N>1.","answer":"The impulse at 0 has F[k]=1, while the impulse at 1 has F[k]=exp(−j2πk/N). Both have |F[k]|=1 for every k, but their phases and spatial positions differ. A circular translation changes phase while preserving Fourier magnitude.","explanation":"DFT magnitudes record the strength of frequency components, while phase aligns those components in space. The two different arrays are a concrete counterexample to unique reconstruction from magnitude. The shift multiplier has unit magnitude, so no magnitude-only comparison can locate that shift."},{"id":"xi060","topic":"Warping","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","source":"Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6.2, triangular mesh warping, printed pp. 175-176 (PDF pp. 201-202)","prompt":"Two adjacent nondegenerate triangles in a valid, nonfolding mesh use separate affine warps. Their shared source vertices have exactly the same destination correspondences in both triangles. Explain why the coordinate map agrees continuously along their common edge, and whether its spatial derivatives must also agree there.","answer":"Every shared-edge point is (1−t)a+tb and maps to (1−t)a′+tb′ in either triangle, so the map is position-continuous along the edge. The affine Jacobians can differ because the third vertices differ, so derivative continuity is not guaranteed.","explanation":"An affine map is determined on the shared edge by its two endpoint correspondences, making the restrictions of both triangle maps identical there. Away from that edge, each third vertex supplies a different constraint and can change the local Jacobian. A piecewise affine mesh therefore guarantees this C0 agreement but generally permits a derivative kink."},{"id":"xn001","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 56–57: L1 and L2 distances; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Before kNN prediction, the same fixed offset vector c is added to every stored training vector and to every query. What happens to L1 and L2 distances?","answer":"Every pairwise query-to-training distance stays fixed for both metrics.","explanation":"The difference is (q+c)−(x+c)=q−x. Both norms therefore receive exactly the same coordinate differences. Applying the offset only to queries would be a different operation.","options":["Both distances increase by the norm of c.","L1 distances change, but L2 distances stay fixed.","Every pairwise query-to-training distance stays fixed for both metrics.","Class labels must be changed to compensate."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn002","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 56–57: L1 and L2 distances; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A known permutation rearranges feature coordinates identically in the query and every training vector. With the original distance-tie rule retained, which kNN predictions can change?","answer":"Neither L1-based nor L2-based predictions.","explanation":"Permuting coordinates does not change the sum of absolute differences or the sum of squared differences. Distances and their ties are unchanged. This is a common permutation of both vectors, not a spatial shift of only the query.","options":["Only L1-based predictions.","Neither L1-based nor L2-based predictions.","Only L2-based predictions.","All predictions must change because pixel positions have moved."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn003","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 64–65: training-set accuracy; derived counterexample","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A deterministic 1-NN classifier receives two identical feature vectors with different labels A and B. It uses a fixed distance-tie rule and sees no extra sample identity. Can it correctly classify both vectors when queried separately?","answer":"No: identical inputs receive the same deterministic prediction.","explanation":"Both queries induce the same distances, ties and prediction. Since their required labels differ, at least one must be wrong. A zero distance alone does not guarantee perfect training accuracy when conflicting duplicates exist.","options":["Yes, because each query has a zero-distance neighbor.","Yes, if L2 is replaced with L1.","No, because an odd k guarantees the prediction is wrong.","No: identical inputs receive the same deterministic prediction."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn004","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 56–57: Euclidean distance; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For unweighted kNN, an implementation ranks neighbors by Σd_j² instead of sqrt(Σd_j²). It uses the same tie rule and finite real distances. What changes?","answer":"Neighbor ordering and predictions are unchanged.","explanation":"Square root is strictly increasing on nonnegative numbers, so it preserves ordering and ties. This justification concerns unweighted neighbor selection; replacing actual distances in a distance-weighting formula can change the vote weights.","options":["Neighbor ordering and predictions are unchanged.","The nearest point becomes the most distant point.","The result is equivalent to L1 distance.","The change preserves only 1-NN, never k>1."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn005","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 47–55: k-nearest-neighbor voting; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An unweighted kNN classifier uses k=N, including all N stored training examples for every query. Class B has a unique largest count in the stored set. What decision rule results?","answer":"Predict B for every query.","explanation":"Every query receives the same complete set of training labels. The majority is therefore B regardless of the distances. Very large k can erase meaningful local class structure.","options":["Choose the label of the nearest point.","Predict B for every query.","Predict a different class whenever the query moves.","Produce a linear boundary separating all classes."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn006","topic":"Validation","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 68–70: independent held-out assessment; applied annotation protocol","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Two copies of the same photograph have contradictory category labels for the same classification task. Which label-resolution policy best preserves the meaning of a later model comparison?","answer":"Resolve the annotation using the task definition and independent evidence, without choosing labels to favor validation results.","explanation":"Annotations define the evaluation target. Selecting them to improve model scores lets model outcomes influence the supposedly independent assessment. A consistent annotation policy should use evidence about the image and task; genuinely ambiguous images may require a predetermined exclusion or ambiguity policy.","options":["Keep whichever label improves the current model’s validation accuracy.","Try each label and keep the one making the most candidates agree.","Resolve the annotation using the task definition and independent evidence, without choosing labels to favor validation results.","Set each validation label equal to the model’s prediction."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn007","topic":"Validation","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 69–70: cross-validation; applied preprocessing protocol","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"During each cross-validation fold, a feature normalizer estimates one mean and variance per coordinate. Which procedure respects the held-out fold?","answer":"Estimate statistics from that fold’s training subset and apply them to its validation subset.","explanation":"Estimated preprocessing values are part of the fitted pipeline. Each held-out fold must be transformed using statistics estimated without its examples. A fixed transformation with no estimated data-dependent values is a different case.","options":["Estimate statistics using the whole dataset before making folds.","Estimate statistics from that fold’s training subset and apply them to its validation subset.","Estimate validation statistics separately and refit the classifier on them.","Use the test labels to decide which normalization statistics look correct."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn008","topic":"Validation","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 68–70: hyperparameter selection; applied experiment control","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You compare k=1 and k=7, but evaluate each candidate on a different random validation split. Which change makes their comparison easier to interpret?","answer":"Evaluate both on the same predetermined split or the same cross-validation folds.","explanation":"A shared split reduces the chance that different sample difficulty explains the measured difference. The comparison should hold the evaluation protocol fixed while changing the candidate hyperparameter.","options":["Evaluate both on the same predetermined split or the same cross-validation folds.","Give the larger k a larger validation set.","Select the split that favors each candidate most.","Train both candidates on their validation labels first."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn009","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 8–22: affine scores; pp. 70–78: softmax; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For every class j, replace score s_j(x) by s_j(x)+u·x+c using the same u and c. What changes for a fixed input?","answer":"Both argmax and softmax probabilities remain unchanged.","explanation":"The added expression has the same scalar value for every class of that input. It cancels from pairwise score differences and from softmax’s numerator-to-denominator ratio. Scores themselves may change even though the predictions and probabilities do not.","options":["Argmax changes but softmax does not.","Softmax changes but argmax does not.","Both argmax and softmax probabilities remain unchanged.","All class scores become identical."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn010","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 8–22: affine scores and geometric view; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Every class weight and bias is multiplied by −1. For an input whose original class scores are all distinct, which original class now has the highest score?","answer":"The original lowest-scoring class.","explanation":"Multiplication by a negative number reverses strict inequalities. The original minimum becomes the new maximum. The familiar argmax-preservation result requires a positive common scale.","options":["The original highest-scoring class.","The original lowest-scoring class.","The class with the smallest weight norm.","No class can be selected after this transformation."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn011","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 8–22: affine scores; Lecture 2, PDF p. 8: illumination changes; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Every pixel coordinate of an input is increased by the same scalar d, without clipping. In an affine multiclass classifier, each class weight row has the same sum r. What follows for the scores?","answer":"Every score increases by d·r, so pairwise gaps and argmax are unchanged.","explanation":"For row j, the score change is Σ_i w_ji d=dΣ_i w_ji=d·r. Equal row sums make this a common shift of the scores. Without equal sums, a uniform input brightness change can alter class gaps.","options":["Every score increases by d·r, so pairwise gaps and argmax are unchanged.","Every score becomes equal because all row sums match.","Only the largest original score changes.","The bias must disappear for every class."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn012","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 19–30: geometric view of linear classification; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A multiclass classifier predicts argmax_j(w_j·x+b_j). Ignore ties. What geometric property does the region assigned to one particular class have?","answer":"It is an intersection of half-spaces and is therefore convex, possibly empty.","explanation":"Class c wins when (w_c−w_j)·x+(b_c−b_j)>0 for every competing class j. Each condition describes an open half-space, and their intersection is convex. Multiple classes permit multiple boundary pieces, but they do not make one class’s region an arbitrary shape.","options":["It can be any disconnected union of rings.","It must consist of exactly one line.","It is an intersection of half-spaces and is therefore convex, possibly empty.","It is always a sphere centered on that class’s weight vector."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn013","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF p. 53: squared multiclass hinge alternative; Lecture 4, slides 25–28: local derivatives","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For L=Σ_(j≠y)[max(0,s_j−s_y+Δ)]², exactly two incorrect classes have positive margins 0.5 and 2. All other margins are negative. What is ∂L/∂s_y? No averaging is used.","answer":"−5","explanation":"Each active squared term m² contributes 2m·(−1) to the correct-score derivative. The sum is −2(0.5)−2(2)=−5. Squaring makes larger margin violations exert larger derivatives, unlike the ordinary unsquared hinge.","options":["−2","−5","+5","0"],"correct":1},{"id":"xn014","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 42–53: multiclass hinge; pp. 70–89: softmax cross-entropy","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A model confidently ranks a wrong class above the true class. Multiplying all logits by a positive factor that grows without bound keeps that wrong class on top. What happens to true-label softmax cross-entropy?","answer":"It grows without bound.","explanation":"If s_wrong>s_y, their probability ratio exp(a(s_wrong−s_y)) grows with scale a. The true probability tends to zero, so −ln p_y diverges. Increasing confidence helps cross-entropy only when it concentrates mass on the correct class.","options":["It tends to zero because confidence increases.","It becomes exactly the hinge loss.","It stays constant because argmax is unchanged.","It grows without bound."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn015","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 58–62: mean data loss plus regularization; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An objective is mean_i L_i(W)+λ‖W‖². You duplicate every training example exactly once, with no other changes. What happens to this mathematical objective as a function of W?","answer":"It is unchanged.","explanation":"The sum of example losses and the number of examples both double, leaving their mean unchanged. The penalty is unchanged. Training schedules or stochastic updates could still differ, but the stated objective does not.","options":["It is unchanged.","Its data term doubles but its penalty stays fixed.","Its penalty doubles but its data term stays fixed.","Its minimum must occur at W=0."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn016","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 58–64: L2 regularization; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"All input coordinates are doubled, and every bias-free linear weight is halved to preserve predictions. What happens to λ‖W‖² if λ is unchanged?","answer":"It becomes one quarter as large.","explanation":"Predictions satisfy (W/2)(2x)=Wx. The squared norm satisfies ‖W/2‖²=‖W‖²/4. A weight penalty depends on the coordinate scale as well as the represented predictions.","options":["It doubles.","It is unchanged.","It becomes one quarter as large.","It becomes four times as large."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn017","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 70–78: softmax and negative log true-class probability; applied debugging","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A cross-entropy implementation computes −ln(max_j p_j) instead of −ln(p_y). Which serious failure can this introduce?","answer":"It can reward a confidently wrong prediction because the true label is ignored.","explanation":"The supervised loss must index the probability of the supplied target y. A model that assigns 0.99 to a wrong class would receive a small loss from −ln(max p), even though it is almost certain of the wrong answer.","options":["It correctly measures the same loss for every input.","It can reward a confidently wrong prediction because the true label is ignored.","It makes every probability sum to more than one.","It always returns a negative loss."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn018","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 4, printed pp. 72–76: divergence and alpha; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For a smooth scalar loss, one update from w_t uses its derivative at w_t. The loss increases substantially after the step. Which conclusion is justified?","answer":"The step may be too large; a correct local derivative does not guarantee improvement for any finite step.","explanation":"Gradient descent gives a local decreasing direction when the gradient is nonzero, but an excessive step can overshoot. A numerical gradient check can separately test the derivative implementation; reducing the step tests a different possibility.","options":["The derivative must have the wrong sign in every case.","The learning rate must be zero.","The model has proved it cannot fit the data.","The step may be too large; a correct local derivative does not guarantee improvement for any finite step."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn019","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 5, printed pp. 80–89: gradients for multiple inputs; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For p=w·x+b and L=(p−y)²/2, an example has x₁=−x₂≠0. Which relation between the two weight gradients must hold for that example?","answer":"g₁=−g₂; they can both be zero.","explanation":"The gradients are (p−y)x₁ and (p−y)x₂. Opposite feature values give opposite gradients, including the possible all-zero case. The current weights affect the shared residual, but not this ratio.","options":["They are both positive.","They are both zero whenever b≠0.","g₁=−g₂; they can both be zero.","The larger current weight has the larger gradient."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn020","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slide 39: pretrained networks and freezing the backbone","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A pretrained backbone is frozen and only a new classifier is trained. New training data require a useful feature that the frozen backbone never represents. Which limitation follows?","answer":"The classifier can only use the existing features; fine-tuning the backbone may be needed.","explanation":"A frozen feature extractor does not receive parameter updates. The head can recombine the features it is given, but it cannot create a missing backbone representation by changing those frozen filters.","options":["The new classifier can change the backbone filters through its loss alone.","The classifier can only use the existing features; fine-tuning the backbone may be needed.","Freezing guarantees the best possible accuracy.","The training labels become unnecessary."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn021","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4, slides 11, 25 and 30: hidden units and matrix gradients; derived symmetry exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Two hidden units have identical incoming weights, biases and outgoing connections, and use the same deterministic activation. They are trained on the same batches with the same update rule. Why may their redundancy persist?","answer":"Their forward values and corresponding gradients match, so symmetric updates preserve equality.","explanation":"The identical units compute the same function and receive the same backward signal through identical outgoing connections. Their updates therefore remain identical unless some mechanism breaks the symmetry. This is a derivation from the network and gradient equations, not a claim that all neurons in real brains behave this way.","options":["They necessarily receive different gradients because their array indices differ.","The loss must be zero whenever units match.","The units become kNN neighbors.","Their forward values and corresponding gradients match, so symmetric updates preserve equality."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn022","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 4, printed pp. 66–71: derivatives; ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85: input-scaled residuals","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"At the current parameters, every training residual p_i−y_i is exactly zero. The objective contains only half-squared data loss and no regularization. Must every fitted weight also be zero?","answer":"No: zero residuals make the data gradients zero, while fitted weights can be nonzero.","explanation":"Each data gradient contains the residual as a factor. Fitting targets can require nonzero weights; for example p=wx with x=1,y=2 is fitted by w=2. A separate regularizer would contribute its own gradient.","options":["No: zero residuals make the data gradients zero, while fitted weights can be nonzero.","Yes: all stationary points have zero weights.","Yes: predictions cannot be correct with nonzero weights.","No: zero residuals force all weight gradients to be positive."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn023","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slides 28–29: sigmoid local derivative and BCE logit gradient","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For a sigmoid output p=σ(z) with binary cross-entropy, a helper already returns dL/dz=p−y. The code multiplies that value by p(1−p) again before propagating into z’s inputs. What is wrong?","answer":"The sigmoid derivative is counted twice, shrinking the gradient incorrectly.","explanation":"The simplification p−y already includes the loss derivative and the sigmoid derivative. Once a value is a gradient with respect to z, upstream inputs of z should receive the derivatives of their own connection to z, without another sigmoid factor.","options":["Nothing: every node derivative must be applied twice.","The helper output is actually dL/dp for all p.","The sigmoid derivative is counted twice, shrinking the gradient incorrectly.","The result only differs when p=0.5."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn024","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slide 33: wrong ReLU mask; applied debugging","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A ReLU backward method passes an upstream gradient only where dout>0. Its forward preactivation is z=2 and dout=−5. What should the correct backward method return?","answer":"−5, because the forward preactivation was positive.","explanation":"ReLU’s derivative depends on the forward preactivation, not the sign of the incoming gradient. At z>0 its derivative is 1, so an upstream −5 is passed unchanged. A negative derivative signal is perfectly valid.","options":["0, because negative gradients are not allowed.","−5, because the forward preactivation was positive.","+5, because ReLU removes negative signs.","2, because gradients equal cached activations."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn025","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slide 32: central-difference gradient checking; numerical caveat","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"In floating-point arithmetic, a central-difference gradient estimate is recomputed with progressively smaller h. Why can an extremely tiny h make the estimate worse?","answer":"Subtracting nearly equal rounded forward values can amplify numerical cancellation.","explanation":"The numerator f(w+h)−f(w−h) can become comparable to rounding error; dividing that error by tiny 2h amplifies it. The best h depends on precision, function scale and smoothness. A single universal h or relative-error cutoff cannot prove every implementation correct.","options":["Smaller h changes the symbolic analytic derivative.","It converts differentiation into integration.","It guarantees an exactly zero true derivative.","Subtracting nearly equal rounded forward values can amplify numerical cancellation."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn026","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 33: residual addition followed by ReLU; derived exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A residual block returns ReLU(x+F(x)). If its residual branch learns F(x)=0, is the entire block the identity for every real-valued x?","answer":"No: negative components are set to zero by the following ReLU.","explanation":"The addition alone becomes x. The following ReLU outputs max(0,x), which equals x only for nonnegative components. Statements about an easy identity shortcut must account for any operation after the addition.","options":["Yes, because every residual block is exactly an identity.","Yes, because ReLU(x)=x for all x.","No: negative components are set to zero by the following ReLU.","No: the addition changes every positive component."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn027","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 7 and 20: signed edge response and ReLU; applied feature interpretation","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An edge filter gives a negative response when the bright and dark sides are exchanged. A ReLU immediately follows the filter. What happens to that negative response?","answer":"It is set to zero in that feature channel.","explanation":"ReLU clips negative responses to zero. A network may learn another filter for the opposite contrast orientation, but that is a separate learned channel; ReLU does not itself preserve the signed response.","options":["It becomes a positive response of equal magnitude.","It is set to zero in that feature channel.","It remains negative but is halved.","The filter weights are automatically negated."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn028","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slide 31: 1×1 convolution as a per-pixel channel map","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A single stride-1 1×1 convolution has no pooling or dilation. Which input values can influence one output location?","answer":"All channels at that same spatial location.","explanation":"A standard 1×1 filter spans input channels but has spatial width and height 1. It can mix channel information at each pixel, while spatial receptive-field growth requires other operations.","options":["All channels at that same spatial location.","Only the matching input channel at every spatial location.","The complete 3×3 neighborhood in one channel.","Every pixel of the input image."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn029","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 18–19: max and average pooling; derived linearity test","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A pooling operation takes the maximum of a two-value window. Let a=[2,0] and b=[0,2]. Does it satisfy pool(a+b)=pool(a)+pool(b)?","answer":"No: pool(a+b)=2, but pool(a)+pool(b)=4.","explanation":"a+b=[2,2], whose maximum is 2; the separate maxima are 2 and 2, summing to 4. This counterexample proves that max pooling is not additive. A value tie is allowed in the forward maximum; ambiguity concerns derivative routing at the tie.","options":["Yes: max pooling is additive for all inputs.","Yes: the sum of the two separately pooled outputs is 2.","No: pool(a+b)=2, but pool(a)+pool(b)=4.","No: max pooling is undefined when two entries tie."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn030","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 9–13: filters, feature maps and parameter sharing","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"One convolutional output channel has one learned bias. The channel contains 100 spatial output positions. How is that bias normally used?","answer":"Added once to each output position using the same shared value.","explanation":"One filter and one bias define a channel, and they are reused across spatial positions. The bias is added after summing all kernel and input-channel products for each output location.","options":["Added only to the first spatial position.","Replaced with 100 independently learned biases.","Added before each input-channel product is computed.","Added once to each output position using the same shared value."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn031","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 5 and 11: sharing and stride; derived translation exercise","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Consider a stride-2 cross-correlation on an infinite one-dimensional input, with no boundary effects. The input is shifted right by exactly two samples. How does the output shift?","answer":"Right by one output sample.","explanation":"Output index t samples a patch starting at input index 2t. A shift by 2 input samples moves the same patch response to t+1. Shifts aligned with the stride preserve this discrete equivariance; a one-sample shift is not generally a simple integer shift of the output.","options":["Right by one output sample.","Right by two output samples.","It is unchanged for every possible input.","It shifts right by half an output sample."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn032","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 35: depthwise-separable convolution","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A depthwise convolution applies one spatial filter per input channel and is used without any following pointwise convolution. Which ability is absent from that layer?","answer":"Learned mixing between different input channels.","explanation":"Depthwise filtering processes channels separately. The 1×1 pointwise stage of a depthwise-separable convolution supplies the missing learned channel mixing. Removing it changes the represented operation.","options":["Spatial filtering within each channel.","Learned mixing between different input channels.","Reusing a filter at different spatial positions.","Processing more than one input channel."],"correct":1},{"id":"xn033","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 35: EfficientNet compound scaling; slide 31: convolution cost; derived approximation","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use the approximate standard-convolution cost model: cost ∝ depth × channel_width² × image_resolution². Fixed kernels are assumed, and channel widths in successive layers scale together. If depth, width and both image axes are each doubled, by what factor does cost grow?","answer":"32×","explanation":"The depth contributes 2, input/output channel products contribute 2²=4, and spatial positions contribute 2²=4. Their product is 2·4·4=32. This explicit approximation explains why coordinated architecture scaling needs a compute budget; actual heads and boundary effects may depart from it.","options":["8×","16×","32×","64×"],"correct":2},{"id":"xn034","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slides 19 and 39: global average pooling and transfer learning","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A frozen backbone followed by global average pooling returns 512 features per image. Its old classifier predicts 1000 categories, but the new task has 7 categories. Which new trainable head is dimensionally sufficient?","answer":"An affine map 512→7 with 7×512 weights and 7 biases.","explanation":"The classifier consumes the pooled feature vector, whose dimension stays 512 when the backbone is frozen. A 7-row weight matrix and a 7-entry bias vector produce the seven required scores.","options":["A 7×1000 matrix multiplying the original image pixels.","A 512×7 matrix multiplying a 7-dimensional input.","A single scalar bias shared by all seven scores.","An affine map 512→7 with 7×512 weights and 7 biases."],"correct":3},{"id":"xn035","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slide 23: data augmentation; applied evaluation protocol","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A benchmark is defined as one deterministic center crop per test image. The evaluator accidentally uses a different random training crop on each test run. What problem follows?","answer":"The reported score no longer follows the stated evaluation protocol and can vary between runs.","explanation":"Training augmentation and test preprocessing serve different purposes. Random test-time crops can change what is visible and introduce run-to-run variation. A deliberate test-time augmentation protocol is possible, but it must be defined and applied consistently.","options":["The reported score no longer follows the stated evaluation protocol and can vary between runs.","Training accuracy must become zero.","The classifier’s parameter count doubles.","Every random crop necessarily preserves the original label and difficulty."],"correct":0},{"id":"xn036","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slide 22: per-channel batch normalization","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Training-mode convolutional batch normalization receives B×C×H×W data. In the lecture’s per-channel formulation, which values normally contribute to one channel’s mean?","answer":"That channel’s B×H×W values across examples and spatial positions.","explanation":"Each channel is normalized separately, using the batch and spatial axes as its sample axes. Different channels retain separate means, variances, scales and shifts.","options":["Only the C channels of one pixel.","Only the first image in the batch.","That channel’s B×H×W values across examples and spatial positions.","All B×C×H×W values pooled into a single universal mean."],"correct":2},{"id":"xn037","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 56–57: Euclidean distance; derived exact pruning exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A brute-force Euclidean nearest-neighbor search already has a candidate at distance 10. For a new point, successive absolute coordinate differences start [6,7,4,100]. Distances are exact, all squared terms are nonnegative, and a point is rejected as soon as its partial squared distance is strictly greater than 10². After how many coordinates can this point first be rejected?","answer":"3 coordinates","explanation":"Partial squared sums are 36, 85 and 101. The first two are at most 100; after the third, the complete distance must exceed 10 regardless of all remaining coordinates.","value":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn038","topic":"Validation","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 62–70: metric/k hyperparameters and cross-validation; derived planning exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A kNN search compares 4 values of k and 2 distance metrics. Every combination is evaluated in 5 folds, each with a fresh training-set fit or stored-set construction. After selection, one final model is fit to all development examples. Count the total fits, including this final fit and excluding any test-set fitting.","answer":"41 fits","explanation":"There are 4×2=8 candidate combinations. Five fits per candidate require 40 fits, and the final development-set fit adds one. The held-out test set is used for assessment, not for another fit.","value":41,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn039","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 8–22: affine scores and boundaries; derived perturbation exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For classes A and B, the current score gap s_A−s_B is 6. The difference between their weights for feature x₃ is w_A3−w_B3=−2. Hold every other feature fixed. What increase δ in x₃ makes these two scores equal? Do not impose pixel clipping.","answer":"δ=3 feature units","explanation":"The new gap is 6+(−2)δ. Setting it to zero gives δ=3. This is an equal-score boundary, not a claim about which class wins a tie.","value":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn040","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 8–22: affine score; derived centering exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"One affine score is s=2x₁−x₂+3. Define centered inputs x′=x−μ with μ=[5,4]. Keep the weight row [2,−1]. What bias must accompany x′ to reproduce exactly the original score?","answer":"9","explanation":"Substitute x₁=x′₁+5 and x₂=x′₂+4: s=2x′₁−x′₂+(10−4+3). The adjusted bias is 9.","value":9,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn041","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 42–53: summed hinge loss; Lecture 4, slides 25–28: derivatives","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A single example uses summed multiclass hinge loss with Δ=1. Its four scores are [2.5,2.0,4.0,−1.0], and the first class is correct. Compute ∂L/∂s_y. All margins are away from zero; no averaging is used.","answer":"−2","explanation":"Incorrect-class margins are 0.5, 2.5 and −2.5. The first two are active and each contributes −1 to the correct-score derivative. Their sum is −2.","value":-2,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn042","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 42–53: hinge loss; Lecture 4, slide 30: affine gradients; derived batch exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For one incorrect class j, two examples contribute to a mean hinge loss (divide the summed losses by 2). Its hinge term is active for both, and their value of feature x₁ is respectively 2 and −4. No other active term involves row j, and regularization is absent. Compute the gradient of row-j weight W_j1.","answer":"−1","explanation":"An active incorrect-class score derivative is +1. Its weight contribution is therefore x₁. Average the contributions: (2−4)/2=−1.","value":-1,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn043","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 58–64: mean data loss plus L2 penalty; derived comparison","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Candidate A has mean data loss 0.2 and squared weight norm 10. Candidate B has mean data loss 0.5 and squared weight norm 4. The objective is data_loss+λ‖W‖². At what λ do the candidates have equal objective values?","answer":"λ=0.05","explanation":"Solve 0.2+10λ=0.5+4λ, giving 6λ=0.3 and λ=0.05. For larger λ the smaller-norm candidate B is preferred; for smaller λ candidate A is preferred.","value":0.05,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn044","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 70–78: softmax cross-entropy; derived stable calculation","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Three logits are [1000,1002,999], and the first class is correct. Compute −ln p_y without overflowing exponentials. Use natural logarithms. Give three decimals or more.","answer":"≈2.169846","explanation":"Subtract 1002 from all logits: [−2,0,−3]. Then L=2+ln(e^(−2)+1+e^(−3))≈2.169846. Directly exponentiating 1000 is unnecessary.","value":2.1698460195562856,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"xn045","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 70–78: softmax; derived probability-ratio exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"In a multiclass softmax, logits of classes A and B differ by s_A−s_B=ln 7. Other logits are arbitrary finite numbers. What is the probability ratio p_A/p_B?","answer":"7","explanation":"The shared softmax denominator cancels: p_A/p_B=e^(s_A−s_B)=e^(ln 7)=7. This ratio does not specify either individual probability without the other class logits.","value":7,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn046","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 70–78: softmax cross-entropy; Lecture 4, slide 25: chain rule; derived gradient","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For one-hot softmax cross-entropy L=−ln p_y, the predicted probability of the true class is 0.1. Compute ∂L/∂s_y with respect to that class’s logit. No batch averaging or regularization is used.","answer":"−0.9","explanation":"Write L=−s_y+ln Σ_j e^(s_j). Differentiation gives −1+e^(s_y)/Σ_j e^(s_j)=p_y−1=−0.9.","value":-0.9,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn047","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slide 29: BCE logit derivative; slide 30: bias gradients; derived mean-batch exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Three binary examples have sigmoid probabilities [0.8,0.3,0.4] and targets [1,0,1]. A single shared scalar bias is added to their logits. The batch loss is the mean BCE. Compute the bias gradient.","answer":"−1/6 ≈ −0.166667","explanation":"The logit gradients before averaging are p−y=[−0.2,0.3,−0.6]. A shared bias receives their mean: (−0.2+0.3−0.6)/3=−1/6.","value":-0.16666666666666666,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"xn048","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 4, printed pp. 72–76; ch. 5, printed pp. 86–89; derived multi-input stability analysis","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Repeat a single example x=[3,4] with p=w·x and L=(p−y)²/2, no bias. Simultaneous gradient descent makes the prediction residual evolve as r_next=(1−η‖x‖²)r. For a nonzero residual, convergence requires 0<η<η_max. Compute the upper endpoint η_max, which is excluded from the convergent interval.","answer":"η_max=0.08, excluded","explanation":"‖x‖²=9+16=25. The contraction condition is |1−25η|<1, hence 0<η<2/25=0.08. At the endpoint the residual changes sign without shrinking.","value":0.08,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn049","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85: computing residuals before multi-weight updates; derived bug test","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use x=[1,2], initial w=[0,0], target y=1, L=(w·x−y)²/2 and η=0.1. Correct code updates both weights using the original residual. Buggy code updates w₁ first, recomputes the residual, then updates w₂. Compute prediction_correct−prediction_buggy after the updates.","answer":"0.04","explanation":"The original residual is −1, giving correct w=[0.1,0.2] and prediction 0.5. Buggy code changes w₁ to 0.1, then uses residual −0.9 to get w₂=0.18 and prediction 0.46. The difference is 0.04.","value":0.04,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn050","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 4, printed pp. 58–59: gradient updates; ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85; derived sequential-data exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Start at scalar w=0 with no bias. Use L=(wx−y)²/2 and η=0.1. Process (x=1,y=1) with one update, then process (x=2,y=1) with another update at the new weight. What is the final w?","answer":"0.26","explanation":"First gradient=(0−1)·1=−1, so w becomes 0.1. Second prediction is 0.2; gradient=(0.2−1)·2=−1.6, so w becomes 0.1+0.16=0.26. Both gradients must be evaluated at their respective current weights.","value":0.26,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn051","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85; Lecture 4, slides 9 and 16; derived batch cancellation exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A scalar affine model p=wx+b starts at w=b=0. Its two examples are (x=1,y=1) and (x=−1,y=1). Use mean half-squared loss and one simultaneous update with η=0.2. What is the new bias?","answer":"0.2","explanation":"Both residuals are −1. The weight gradient is [−1·1+(−1)·(−1)]/2=0, but the bias gradient is (−1−1)/2=−1. Thus b_new=0−0.2(−1)=0.2. Nonzero losses can cancel for one parameter but not another.","value":0.2,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn052","topic":"Neural networks","source":"Lecture 4, slides 13–15: sigmoid prediction; derived decision-threshold exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A binary sigmoid classifier labels an example positive when p=σ(z)≥0.8. What logit threshold corresponds exactly to p=0.8? Use natural logarithms and give three decimals or more.","answer":"ln 4 ≈ 1.386294","explanation":"Solve p/(1−p)=e^z. At p=0.8, the odds are 0.8/0.2=4, so z=ln 4. The common threshold z=0 corresponds specifically to p=0.5.","value":1.3862943611198906,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"xn053","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slides 25–28: chain rule and copy-gate gradient sums; derived network exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Let h=ReLU(wx), p₁=2h, p₂=−h and L=[(p₁−1)²+(p₂−1)²]/2. At x=2,w=0.5, compute ∂L/∂w. Both output branches share the same h.","answer":"8","explanation":"h=1, giving p=[2,−1] and residuals [1,−2]. The shared h receives 1·2+(−2)(−1)=4. ReLU is active, and ∂h/∂w=x=2, so the weight gradient is 4·2=8.","value":8,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn054","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slide 28: max routing and copy-gate summation; derived graph exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For t=max(a,b)+a, take a=3,b=1 and upstream gradient ∂L/∂t=−2. Compute ∂L/∂a. There is no tie at the max gate.","answer":"−4","explanation":"The max branch selects a and contributes −2. The separate direct a branch also contributes −2. Gradients at the copied input must be added, giving −4.","value":-4,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn055","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 5, slide 18: max pooling; Lecture 4, slide 28: max routing and branch summation; derived exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"One-dimensional max pooling uses length-2 windows, stride 1, no padding, on x=[1,4,3]. Its two outputs receive upstream gradients [2,−0.5]. Compute the gradient of the middle input value 4.","answer":"1.5","explanation":"The middle value is the unique maximum in both windows [1,4] and [4,3]. It therefore receives both contributions: 2+(−0.5)=1.5. Overlapping windows require gradient accumulation.","value":1.5,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn056","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 5, slide 6: shared cross-correlation; Lecture 4, slides 25 and 28: local products and sums; derived exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Use one-dimensional valid stride-1 cross-correlation y₀=a x₀+b x₁, y₁=a x₁+b x₂, no bias. For x=[1,2,4], a=1,b=−1, upstream output gradients are [3,−1]. Compute ∂L/∂a for this single shared filter weight.","answer":"1","explanation":"The shared weight a appears in both outputs. Its gradient is 3x₀+(−1)x₁=3·1−1·2=1. Sharing does not permit separate independently updated copies of a.","value":1,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn057","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 33: projection shortcut; slide 13: convolution parameter count","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A residual shortcut uses a stride-2 1×1 projection from 32 input channels to 64 output channels. Count trainable weights only; this projection has no bias. How many weights are required?","answer":"2048 weights","explanation":"Each of 64 output channels has a 1×1×32 filter: 64·32=2048 weights. Stride changes sampling positions and spatial size, but does not add more learned filter coefficients.","value":2048,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn058","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slide 22: per-channel batch normalization; derived batch-statistics exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For one BN channel, the batch-and-spatial samples are [0,2,4,6]. Use their population variance (divide by 4), ε=0, γ=1 and β=0. What normalized value does the sample 6 produce? Give three decimals or more.","answer":"3/√5 ≈ 1.341641","explanation":"The mean is 3. Squared deviations are 9,1,1,9, so the population variance is 20/4=5. The sample 6 becomes (6−3)/√5=3/√5.","value":1.3416407864998738,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"xn059","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slide 23: dropout; explicitly defined inverted-dropout exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Inverted dropout transforms a fixed activation a=2 into A=0 with probability 0.5 and A=a/(1−0.5) with probability 0.5. Compute Var(A), in squared activation units.","answer":"4 squared activation units","explanation":"A is 0 or 4 with equal probability. E[A]=2 and E[A²]=8, so Var(A)=8−2²=4. Scaling preserves the expectation but does not remove the training-time randomness.","value":4,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn060","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slide 19: global average pooling; Lecture 4, slide 30: affine map; derived equivalence exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 4×4 feature channel is globally averaged and then multiplied by classifier weight 8. An equivalent linear classifier is applied directly to the 16 flattened values, with the same weight at every position. What must that repeated weight be? Biases are unchanged.","answer":"0.5 per spatial value","explanation":"The original contribution is 8·[(Σv_i)/16]=(8/16)Σv_i. Each flattened value therefore needs coefficient 0.5. This equivalence requires tied spatial coefficients; a general FC layer need not have them.","value":0.5,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn061","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 6 and 11: cross-correlation windows and stride; derived coverage exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A one-dimensional input has indices 0 through 9. Valid cross-correlation uses kernel length 3, stride 4, no padding and no dilation, with only fully fitting windows. How many input indices are never covered by any window?","answer":"4 input indices","explanation":"The fully fitting starts are 0 and 4, covering {0,1,2} and {4,5,6}. The next start 8 would require index 10 and is excluded. Uncovered indices are 3,7,8,9: four in total.","value":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn062","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 6, 9–13 and 31: convolution products and computational cost; derived exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A standard convolution has output size 10×12×8, input depth 3 and 3×3 kernels. Compute the number of scalar multiplications for one example, counting 27 products at every output position and ignoring bias, activations and padding shortcuts.","answer":"25,920 multiplications","explanation":"Each output value uses 3·3·3=27 products. There are 10·12·8=960 output values, so the count is 960·27=25,920. Learned weights are shared, but their products are evaluated repeatedly across positions.","value":25920,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn063","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 11 and 17: stride and receptive fields; derived sampling-jump exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Two successive ordinary convolutions each have stride 2 and no dilation. Away from boundaries, how many original input pixels separate the receptive-field centers of adjacent final outputs along one spatial axis? Kernel sizes do not affect this center-to-center spacing.","answer":"4 input pixels","explanation":"The first stride doubles the sampling jump from 1 to 2. The second doubles it from 2 to 4. Receptive-field width is a separate quantity and additionally depends on the kernels.","value":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn064","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 35: DenseNet concatenation; derived channel-growth exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A DenseNet-style block starts with 24 channels. Each of four layers appends 12 newly computed channels by concatenation, keeping all previous channels. No transition layer compresses them. How many channels leave the block?","answer":"72 channels","explanation":"Concatenation retains the starting channels and adds each layer’s 12 channels: 24+4·12=72. This is channel growth from concatenation, not residual elementwise addition.","value":72,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn065","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 35: depthwise-separable convolution; derived cost counterexample","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A standard 3×3 convolution maps 16 input channels to just 1 output channel. Compare a depthwise 3×3 stage (one kernel per input channel) followed by a 1×1 16→1 stage. Ignore biases. How many more weights does the separable construction use than the standard convolution?","answer":"16 more weights","explanation":"Standard: 3·3·16·1=144. Separable: 3·3·16+16·1=160. Difference: 160−144=16. Depthwise separability is not automatically cheaper for every possible output-channel count.","value":16,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn066","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slides 30–31: parallel Inception branches and bottlenecks; derived parameter exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An Inception-style module receives 32 channels. Branch A uses 1×1 convolution to produce 8 channels. Branch B uses 1×1 32→4 reduction followed by 3×3 4→8 convolution. Count weights in these two branches only, ignoring all biases. What is the total?","answer":"672 weights","explanation":"Branch A uses 32·8=256 weights. Branch B uses 32·4=128 for reduction and 3·3·4·8=288 afterward. Total: 256+128+288=672. Both branches receive the original 32-channel input.","value":672,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn067","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 5, slide 33: residual gradient path; Lecture 4, slides 25 and 28: chain rule; derived exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A scalar residual addition is t=x+F(x), with no activation after the addition. At the current x, F′(x)=−0.8 and upstream ∂L/∂t=3. Compute ∂L/∂x, including both paths.","answer":"0.6","explanation":"The direct shortcut contributes 3, and the branch contributes 3(−0.8)=−2.4. Their sum is 0.6, equivalently 3[1+F′(x)]. A shortcut provides a direct path, but branch gradients can still partially cancel it.","value":0.6,"tolerance":1e-06},{"id":"xn068","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slide 36: ViT image patches; derived token-count exercise","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A ViT divides a 224×224 image into nonoverlapping 16×16 spatial patches. All color channels of each patch are embedded together. How many image-patch tokens result, excluding any class token?","answer":"196 image-patch tokens","explanation":"Each axis has 224/16=14 patches, giving 14·14=196 tokens. RGB channels enlarge each patch vector; they do not triple the number of spatial patch tokens.","value":196,"tolerance":0},{"id":"xn069","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp. 47–55 and 68–70: neighbor votes and held-out assessment; derived interpretation","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Five nearest neighbors all vote for cat. Explain why the vote fraction 5/5 is not, by itself, proof that a cat prediction has a calibrated 100% probability of being correct.","answer":"It describes agreement among those five stored labels under the chosen features and metric. The neighbors can be correlated, noisy, misleading or unrepresentative. Reliability must be checked on held-out examples; vote agreement alone does not establish calibrated certainty.","explanation":"The vote fraction is a classifier statistic, not a guarantee about unseen labels. Learned features, k, local data density and label noise affect the relationship between votes and actual accuracy."},{"id":"xn070","topic":"Linear classifiers","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 8–22: affine scores; Lecture 4, slide 4: single-template limitation; derived construction","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Construct one affine binary score that is positive only at (1,1) among the four Boolean inputs (0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1). Predict positive when the score is >0. Give weights, bias and all four scores.","answer":"Choose w=[1,1] and b=−1.5. The scores in the given order are [−1.5,−0.5,−0.5,0.5], so only (1,1) is positive.","explanation":"This implements AND with one affine boundary x₁+x₂=1.5. It gives a concrete contrast with XOR: some Boolean patterns are linearly separable even though XOR is not."},{"id":"xn071","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp. 70–78: softmax cross-entropy; Lecture 4, slide 25: chain rule; derived identity","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"For one-hot softmax cross-entropy, derive the logit gradient g_j=p_j−1[j=y] and show why Σ_j g_j=0. Connect the zero sum to adding a common bias to every class score.","answer":"Write L=−s_y+ln Σ_k e^(s_k). Differentiating gives g_j=−1[j=y]+e^(s_j)/Σ_k e^(s_k)=p_j−1[j=y]. Since probabilities sum to 1 and the one-hot target sums to 1, the gradient sum is 0. A common additive score shift leaves the loss unchanged.","explanation":"The derivative in the all-ones score direction is the gradient sum, so its zero value matches softmax’s common-shift invariance. This concerns a shared shift across classes, not an arbitrary class-specific bias."},{"id":"xn072","topic":"Gradient descent","source":"Trask, ch. 4, printed pp. 66–71; ch. 5, printed pp. 80–85: residual-driven gradients; derived conflicting-target exercise","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A bias-free scalar model p=w sees x=1 twice, with targets 0 and 2. Its loss is the mean half-squared loss. At w=1, compute the loss and gradient, and explain why zero gradient does not mean that every example is fitted.","answer":"The residuals are +1 and −1. The mean half-squared loss is [(1²/2)+(−1)²/2]/2=0.5, while dL/dw=(1−1)/2=0. The opposite gradient contributions cancel, and one deterministic prediction cannot equal both conflicting targets.","explanation":"The loss is L(w)=[w²+(w−2)²]/4, whose minimum occurs at w=1. That minimum is positive because the prescribed model and identical input cannot satisfy both targets."},{"id":"xn073","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slide 32: numerical gradient checks; Lecture 5, slides 22–23: BN and dropout; applied debugging","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You run a central-difference gradient check on a network whose dropout masks and training-mode BN state can change between forward calls. What must be controlled for the numerical and analytic gradients to be comparable?","answer":"All evaluations must represent the same deterministic function of the perturbed parameter. Reuse or disable dropout randomness and keep relevant BN state and inputs consistent. The analytic backward pass must correspond to that same forward configuration. Avoid nondifferentiable activation points when interpreting a smooth gradient check.","explanation":"Central differences compare f(w+h) and f(w−h). If unrelated random masks or state changes also alter f, their effect can overwhelm the tiny parameter perturbation and create a false mismatch."},{"id":"xn074","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slides 16, 30–31 and 33: update order, caches and matrix gradients; applied implementation error","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For y=Wx, backward needs dx=Wᵀg. A custom implementation updates W with the optimizer before calculating dx, while g still refers to the previous forward pass. Explain the error and give a correct ordering.","answer":"Using the updated W differentiates a different affine operation from the one that produced the cached forward output. Compute all needed gradients with the forward-pass parameter values, finish backward, then update parameters. Alternatively, backward must use a safely retained forward-pass W.","explanation":"Backpropagation differentiates the recorded computation. Mutating a parameter before its dependent gradients are calculated breaks that connection, even when the matrix shapes still match."},{"id":"xn075","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 4, slides 22 and 33: accumulated gradients; derived unequal-microbatch exercise","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A batch is split into microbatches of 3 and 1 examples. Each loss call returns its microbatch mean. You want the gradient of the mean over all 4 examples. Give the two multipliers to apply before adding gradients, and explain why averaging the two microbatch means equally is wrong.","answer":"Multiply the 3-example mean gradient by 3/4 and the 1-example mean gradient by 1/4, then add. Equal weighting would give each example in the small microbatch three times the weight of an example in the large one.","explanation":"A full-batch mean weights examples equally. Since each microbatch gradient already divides by its own size, sample-count weighting restores one common denominator of 4. Clear gradients at the appropriate boundary and update after the accumulation."},{"id":"xn076","topic":"Backpropagation","source":"Lecture 5, slides 5–6 and 24: convolution weight sharing; Lecture 4, slide 28: copied-value gradients","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A learned convolution weight is reused at many spatial output positions. Explain why its backward contributions must be summed, and when a mean-loss normalization factor should be applied.","answer":"The same parameter influences every position where it is used, so the chain rule adds the derivative contribution from every use. Any averaging factor comes from the precise loss definition, such as a mean over examples or output positions. Sharing alone does not require dividing by the number of uses.","explanation":"Treating each spatial use as an independently updated copy would remove weight sharing. Adding a further average after the upstream loss gradients already include it would incorrectly rescale the update."},{"id":"xn077","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 14 and 19: shared convolution and global average pooling; derived resolution exercise","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A classifier uses only convolutions, then global average pooling, then a fixed C→K affine head. Why can its head accept different input image sizes, whereas a head attached directly to a flattened spatial tensor often cannot? State one practical condition.","answer":"Global average pooling outputs one value per feature channel, so the head receives C values regardless of the feature map’s height and width. Direct flattening yields H×W×C values and therefore changes the head’s required input width when H or W changes. The convolutional stack must still admit valid spatial sizes, and the input channel count must remain compatible.","explanation":"Parameter sharing makes convolution weights independent of the image’s spatial dimensions; global pooling makes the final vector length independent as well. Different resolutions can still change numerical predictions and computational cost."},{"id":"xn078","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 17–20: receptive field, pooling and ReLU; Lecture 4, slide 28: routed gradients; derived distinction","kind":"short","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A final unit has a theoretical receptive field of 11×11. For one particular image, only a few pixels in that region have a nonzero gradient to the unit. Does that contradict the receptive-field calculation? Explain.","answer":"No. The theoretical receptive field describes the input region that can influence the unit through the architecture. For this particular image, ReLU masks, max-pooling selections, zero weights or cancellation can block some derivative paths, so the nonzero gradient support can be smaller.","explanation":"Receptive-field geometry is an architectural possibility, while the actual derivative depends on values and learned parameters. A nonzero theoretical connection does not guarantee a nonzero local sensitivity on every input."},{"id":"xn079","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slides 22 and 39: BN running statistics and frozen backbones; applied state distinction","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"You freeze a pretrained backbone’s trainable parameters, but leave its BN layers in training mode while training a new head. Have you necessarily frozen the backbone’s entire behavior? Explain which state can still change and how to preserve pretrained BN behavior if that is intended.","answer":"No. BN running means and variances are state buffers rather than ordinary trainable weights, and training-mode forwards can update them even when parameter gradients are disabled. If the intended protocol preserves pretrained BN behavior, keep those backbone BN layers in evaluation mode so they use their stored running statistics.","explanation":"Freezing parameter updates and choosing train/evaluation mode control different things. Another protocol may intentionally adapt BN statistics, but that is a deliberate behavior change and should be specified."},{"id":"xn080","topic":"CNN architectures","source":"Lecture 5, slides 32–33 and 37–38: degradation problem and limited CIFAR experiment","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"The lecture’s short CPU experiment gives VGGMini 70.1% and ResNetMini 63.7% accuracy. Explain why this does not establish that residual connections generally reduce accuracy, and name two controls needed for a broader comparison.","answer":"The result concerns particular small models, a subset of CIFAR-10 and a limited training budget. Residual connections chiefly help optimization of sufficiently deep networks; one shallow experiment does not test every such setting. A broader comparison should control the data split and training budget/hyperparameter tuning, and examine matched architectures across several depths and random runs.","explanation":"The architecture table reports measured evidence within its experiment. General conclusions require comparable conditions and an evaluation matched to the mechanism being tested, rather than treating one measured ordering as a universal theorem."},{"id":"xp001","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Szeliski §§3.1.2 and 3.2; Sample Variant 1, Q4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A grayscale transform is G=aR+bG_channel+cB with fixed coefficients. The same fixed linear spatial filter F is applied independently to each channel. Use real arithmetic, the same linear boundary rule, and no clipping or rounding. How do grayscale-then-filter and filter-then-grayscale compare?","options":["They give exactly the same grayscale image under these assumptions.","They differ because every spatial filter is nonlinear.","Filtering first necessarily preserves more color in the final grayscale image.","They are equal only if all three grayscale coefficients are identical."],"correct":0,"answer":"They give exactly the same grayscale image under these assumptions.","explanation":"Linearity gives F(aR+bG_channel+cB)=aF(R)+bF(G_channel)+cF(B). Both pipelines compute this expression. Channel-dependent filters, clipping, intermediate rounding, or a nonlinear filter can invalidate the equality."},{"id":"xp002","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Szeliski §3.1.1; OpenCV arithmetic operations and subtract documentation: https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d0/d86/tutorial_py_image_arithmetics.html and https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d2/de8/group__core__array.html","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Let a=np.array([[10]],dtype=np.uint8) and b=np.array([[30]],dtype=np.uint8). With unsigned 8-bit output, what values are produced by a-b and cv2.subtract(a,b), respectively?","options":["−20 and −20","236 and 0","0 and 236","20 and 20"],"correct":1,"answer":"236 and 0","explanation":"NumPy uint8 array subtraction wraps modulo 256: −20 mod 256=236. OpenCV subtraction with uint8 output saturates at the representable minimum, so it gives 0. To retain signed differences, convert to a suitable signed or floating type before subtraction."},{"id":"xp003","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski §§3.2 and 3.3.3; Sample Variant 1, Q1","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Define T(v)=1 if v≥100, otherwise 0. At an interior location the three grayscale values are [0,0,255]. Compare A: average these three intensities, then apply T; and B: apply T to each intensity, then average the three binary values. Do not threshold B's final result. Which pair (A,B) is correct?","options":["(1,1)","(0,0)","(0,1/3)","(1/3,0)"],"correct":2,"answer":"(0,1/3)","explanation":"A first obtains 255/3=85 and then T(85)=0. B thresholds to [0,0,1] and averages to 1/3. Thresholding is nonlinear, so changing the order changes the representation and result."},{"id":"xp004","topic":"Validation","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp.68–69 and 82; Szeliski §3.1.4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Before inspecting final test outcomes, a pipeline is fixed to equalize each image using only that image's own intensity histogram. It also does this at deployment. No labels or statistics pooled across test images are used, and its settings were selected on validation data. Is computing a test image's histogram automatically test leakage?","options":["Yes: any calculation involving a test pixel is leakage.","Yes: histogram equalization is always trained using labels.","No, but only if the test labels are used to choose the equalization mapping.","No: this is a predetermined per-image inference transform, although its effect on accuracy still needs evaluation."],"correct":3,"answer":"No: this is a predetermined per-image inference transform, although its effect on accuracy still needs evaluation.","explanation":"An inference algorithm must read the input image. A fixed rule using that input's histogram does not use held-out target information or fit a shared transform to the test population. Selecting its settings after inspecting test outcomes would compromise the final evaluation."},{"id":"xp005","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 9–10; Sample Variant 1, Q4","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A dense-channel CNN first convolution was trained for three-channel RGB input and has weights shaped (8,3,5,5), using (output channels,input channels,kernel height,kernel width). A new pipeline sends a grayscale batch shaped (N,1,H,W). What prevents applying this unchanged layer directly?","options":["Its three input-channel weights per spatial position do not match the one-channel input.","Eight output filters require eight input channels.","A 5×5 kernel cannot process any grayscale image.","Grayscale necessarily changes every image's height and width."],"correct":0,"answer":"Its three input-channel weights per spatial position do not match the one-channel input.","explanation":"Each learned filter spans all three expected input channels. Changing only the tensor to one channel creates a channel mismatch. A deliberate compatible input representation or a changed first layer is required; either can also change the trained model's behavior."},{"id":"xp006","topic":"Nearest neighbors","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp.33 and 57–62","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For 1-NN with Euclidean distance, query q=(0,0), class-A prototype A=(3,0), and class-B prototype B=(0,2). A preprocessing rule multiplies the SECOND coordinate of every vector by 2 and leaves the first unchanged. What happens to the nearest prototype?","options":["It stays B because feature scaling can never change nearest neighbors.","It changes from B to A because distances change from (3,2) to (3,4).","It changes from A to B because Euclidean distance ignores the first coordinate.","Both prototypes always remain tied."],"correct":1,"answer":"It changes from B to A because distances change from (3,2) to (3,4).","explanation":"Before scaling, d(q,A)=3 and d(q,B)=2. After scaling, A=(3,0) and B=(0,4), so A is nearest. Unequal feature scaling changes the metric's weighting; preprocessing and distance choices must be evaluated together."},{"id":"xp007","topic":"Filtering","source":"Szeliski §§3.2.1–3.2.2","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"A separable signed derivative kernel is implemented as a horizontal derivative pass followed by vertical smoothing. After the first pass, all negative values are clipped to zero. Why can this differ from applying the original 2D kernel in real arithmetic?","options":["A separable kernel has no 2D equivalent.","Vertical smoothing must always reverse the image's sign.","Intermediate clipping is nonlinear and removes signed responses needed by the second pass.","Separable filtering necessarily changes the filter's support size."],"correct":2,"answer":"Intermediate clipping is nonlinear and removes signed responses needed by the second pass.","explanation":"Exact separable equivalence assumes that the two linear passes preserve their intermediate values. Clipping negative derivative responses inserts an extra nonlinear operator. A signed or floating intermediate avoids this particular loss of information."},{"id":"xp008","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Sample Variant 1, Q5(d); Lecture 2, PDF p.2","kind":"mcq","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"For the defective class, model A has TP=40, FN=10, FP=80; model B has TP=30, FN=20, FP=5. A deployment requirement says recall TP/(TP+FN) must be at least 0.80. Considering only this requirement, which model qualifies?","options":["B only, because its false-positive count is smaller.","Both, because both have more true positives than false negatives.","Neither, because recall includes false positives in its denominator.","A only; its precision cost should be assessed separately."],"correct":3,"answer":"A only; its precision cost should be assessed separately.","explanation":"A's recall is 40/50=0.80; B's is 30/50=0.60. A satisfies the stated recall constraint, but its 80 false positives are a real additional cost. The question does not establish that A is best under every deployment objective."},{"id":"xp009","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Lecture 2, PDF p.3; Lecture 5, slide 24","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A preprocessed batch contains 32 RGB images of size 224×224, stored densely as float32 values of 4 bytes each. Count only input tensor data, with no metadata or model activations. How many MiB are required? Use 1 MiB=2^20 bytes.","value":18.375,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"18.375 MiB","explanation":"There are 32×224×224×3=4,816,896 scalars. Their storage is 4,816,896×4=19,267,584 bytes, which is 19,267,584/1,048,576=18.375 MiB.","steps":["32×224×224×3=4,816,896 values","4,816,896×4=19,267,584 bytes","19,267,584/2^20=18.375 MiB"]},{"id":"xp010","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Szeliski §3.6; Sample Variant 1, Q4(a)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A 640×360 image (width×height) is resized uniformly to 320×180 and centered vertically in a 320×320 canvas. For this exercise use continuous image coordinates with x'=0.5x and y'=0.5y before padding; ignore half-pixel interpolation conventions. An original landmark is (x,y)=(400,200). What is its y-coordinate on the padded canvas?","value":170,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"170 pixels","explanation":"The resized landmark has y'=100. The unused height is 320−180=140, so centering adds 70 pixels above. The padded coordinate is 100+70=170. The specified coordinate convention makes the calculation independent of interpolation implementation details.","steps":["Resized height=180","Top padding=(320−180)/2=70","Final y=0.5×200+70=170"]},{"id":"xp011","topic":"Validation","source":"Lecture 2, PDF pp.69–70; Sample Variant 1, Q2(b)","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"Three disjoint validation folds contain 30, 70, and 100 images. A fixed candidate correctly classifies 24, 42, and 90 images, respectively. What is the pooled per-image accuracy as a percentage? Weight each image equally, rather than each fold equally.","value":78,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"78%","explanation":"Pooled accuracy is (24+42+90)/(30+70+100)=156/200=0.78. The unweighted mean of fold accuracies, (0.8+0.6+0.9)/3≈0.7667, answers a different averaging question when fold sizes differ.","steps":["Total correct=24+42+90=156","Total images=30+70+100=200","100×156/200=78%"]},{"id":"xp012","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 4, 13 and 19","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A feature map is 7×7×16. Head A flattens it and maps directly to 4 class scores with one bias per class. Head B uses parameter-free global average pooling and then maps its 16 values to the same 4 scores, also with biases. Store parameters as float32 (4 bytes each). How many KiB of parameter storage does B save over A? Use 1 KiB=1024 bytes; ignore all earlier layers.","value":12,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"12 KiB","explanation":"A has 784×4+4=3140 parameters. B has 16×4+4=68. The difference is 3072 parameters, or 12,288 bytes=12 KiB. Global averaging also changes the representation, so this storage saving alone does not prove equal classification performance.","steps":["A: (7×7×16)×4+4=3140 parameters","B: 16×4+4=68 parameters","(3140−68)×4/1024=12 KiB"]},{"id":"xp013","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Sample Variant 1, Q1(c); Szeliski §3.1.2; OpenCV color-conversion formula: https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/de/d25/imgproc_color_conversions.html","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A BGR pixel is [20,80,200]. Use floating arithmetic and Y=0.299R+0.587G+0.114B, without integer rounding. A correct conversion respects BGR order; a bug treats the array as RGB. What is Y_correct−Y_bug in intensity units?","value":33.3,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"33.3 intensity units","explanation":"Correctly, R=200,G=80,B=20, giving 59.8+46.96+2.28=109.04. The bug uses R=20,G=80,B=200, giving 5.98+46.96+22.8=75.74. Their difference is 33.30. Both outputs have one channel, so tensor shape alone cannot detect this color-order error.","steps":["Correct Y=0.299×200+0.587×80+0.114×20=109.04","Bug Y=0.299×20+0.587×80+0.114×200=75.74","109.04−75.74=33.30"]},{"id":"xp014","topic":"CNN operations","source":"Lecture 5, slides 9–11, 18 and 21","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"An input tensor is 26×30×3 in H×W×C order. Apply a dense-channel convolution with 8 output filters, K=3, P=1, S=2 in both spatial directions, then max pooling with K=2, P=0, S=2. Use O=floor((W−K+2P)/S)+1 for each dimension. How many values remain per image after flattening the pooled output?","value":336,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"336 values","explanation":"Convolution gives H=floor(25/2)+1=13 and W=floor(29/2)+1=15, with 8 channels. Pooling gives H=floor(11/2)+1=6 and W=floor(13/2)+1=7, retaining 8 channels. Flattening therefore produces 6×7×8=336 values.","steps":["Convolution output: 13×15×8","Pooling output: 6×7×8","Flattened size: 6×7×8=336"]},{"id":"xp015","topic":"Losses and regularization","source":"Lecture 3, PDF pp.69–79; Lecture 5, slide 23","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Two label-preserving views of one image give true-class probabilities 0.7 and 0.1. Pipeline A averages the probabilities, then computes cross-entropy; pipeline B averages the two individual cross-entropies. Using natural logs, what is L_B−L_A? Give at least three decimals.","value":0.41333928659223396,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"Approximately 0.413339","explanation":"A gives −ln((0.7+0.1)/2)=−ln(0.4)≈0.916291. B gives −(ln(0.7)+ln(0.1))/2≈1.329630. Thus B−A≈0.413339. The logarithm is nonlinear, so averaging probabilities and averaging losses are different operations.","steps":["L_A=−ln(0.4)≈0.916291","L_B=−[ln(0.7)+ln(0.1)]/2≈1.329630","L_B−L_A≈0.413339"]},{"id":"xp016","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Sample Variant 1, Q2(b) and Q5(d); Lecture 2, PDF p.2","kind":"numeric","difficulty":"stretch","prompt":"Two binary classifiers A and B are evaluated on the same 100 images. A is correct on 84; B is correct on 86; both are correct on 78. Every image has exactly one of two labels. A selective system accepts an image only when A and B agree, and returns their shared prediction. What is accuracy among ACCEPTED images, as a percentage? Give at least three decimals.","value":90.69767441860465,"tolerance":0.001,"answer":"Approximately 90.697674%","explanation":"A-only correct=84−78=6 and B-only correct=86−78=8. Both wrong=100−78−6−8=8. In binary classification, two wrong predictions must share the other label. Thus 78+8=86 images are accepted, and 78 are correct: 100×78/86≈90.697674%. Coverage is only 86%, so accepted-image accuracy should be reported with the rejection rate.","steps":["A only correct=6; B only correct=8","Both wrong=8; binary predictions then agree","Accepted=78+8=86","Accepted-image accuracy=100×78/86≈90.697674%"]},{"id":"xp017","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Sample Variant 1, Q1(d); Lecture 2, PDF pp.68–69; Szeliski §§3.1.2 and 3.2","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"On the same validation images, a classifier gives 90% accuracy with neither grayscale conversion nor blur, 89% with grayscale only, 91% with blur only, and 85% with both. Explain what this ablation suggests about choosing preprocessing combinations, and what evidence is still needed before making a general claim.","answer":"Blur alone is best among these measured candidates. Grayscale alone costs 1 percentage point, blur alone gains 1, but their combination costs 5; the combined effect differs from simply adding their separate effects. Choose combinations on validation data, then confirm the selected pipeline on a reserved test set. Repeats or suitable uncertainty analysis are needed before claiming the small differences are reliable or the finding is universal.","explanation":"If the two measured effects added independently, the combined result would be 90−1+1=90%, not 85%. Their interaction can arise from information loss and the classifier's response to the resulting representation. These observations concern this dataset and trained setup; preprocessing benefits depend on the task."},{"id":"xp018","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","source":"Szeliski §3.1.1; Lecture 2, PDF p.8; Sample Variant 1, Q1(a,d)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A pipeline independently normalizes each grayscale image as (I−min(I))/(max(I)−min(I)). The dataset includes constant images and two classes partly distinguished by absolute brightness. Identify two problems and propose a defensible repair strategy.","answer":"A constant image has max=min, causing division by zero. Even nonconstant images can lose class-relevant absolute brightness because separate offsets and scales are removed. Define an explicit finite behavior for zero-range images, preserve brightness information or test an alternative normalization, and choose the procedure on validation data. Apply the selected rule consistently at inference.","explanation":"For nonconstant images, I and aI+b with a>0 receive identical min-max-normalized representations, even when brightness differences matter to the label. An epsilon or explicit constant-image branch addresses the denominator, but does not by itself restore the discarded brightness information."},{"id":"xp019","topic":"Evaluation and system design","source":"Lecture 2, PDF p.2; Sample Variant 1, Q5(a)","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"A trained waste classifier's output indices mean [Glass,Paper,Plastic,Other]. Inference discovers folders in alphabetical order [Glass,Other,Paper,Plastic] and uses that list to name argmax outputs. For an image whose argmax is index 2, explain the bug and how the model package should prevent it.","answer":"Training index 2 means Plastic, but the inference list prints Paper. Persist the exact class-to-index and index-to-class mappings with the trained model, validate them when loading, and decode scores with that saved mapping. A new alphabetical folder order must not redefine the trained output units.","explanation":"A score vector has meaning only in its agreed class order. This bug can silently produce plausible but wrong labels despite unchanged tensor dimensions and unchanged numerical predictions. Changing displayed names requires an intentional consistent remapping, not guessing from directory order."},{"id":"xp020","topic":"CNN training","source":"Lecture 5, slides 22–23","kind":"short","difficulty":"exam","prompt":"At inference, a CNN still uses current-batch statistics in batch normalization and still applies dropout randomly. With identical weights, an image's output changes when other images in its batch change, and repeated identical batches also give different outputs. Explain the two mechanisms and the normal evaluation configuration.","answer":"Batch normalization in training mode uses the current batch and spatial values, so companions change its mean and variance and can change this image's normalized features. Active dropout draws random masks, making repeated forwards vary. Normal evaluation disables dropout and uses the learned running batch-normalization statistics; it keeps the trained weights and learned BN scale/shift.","explanation":"Current-batch dependence and random masking are separate causes. Merely freezing weight updates or disabling gradient recording does not itself change these forward-pass behaviors. With fixed preprocessing, inputs, and deterministic computation, the usual evaluation configuration removes these two sources of variation."}],"units":[{"id":"su01","title":"OpenCV pipelines: shapes and information","topic":"OpenCV and pipelines","priority":"sample","sources":["Original sample Questions 1 and 4","OpenCV image smoothing and Canny documentation"],"summary":"Trace the shape, channel order, numeric range and retained evidence after each operation.","body":"<h3>Track what each operation changes</h3><p>Default <code>cv2.imread</code> returns a color image in BGR order. A normal image array has shape (height, width, 3). <code>cv2.resize(img, (width, height))</code> uses the reverse order for its size argument. A grayscale conversion usually returns an array of shape (height, width), representing one intensity channel.</p><pre>img = cv2.imread(\"leaf.jpg\")\nif img is None:\n    raise FileNotFoundError(\"leaf.jpg\")\nimg = cv2.resize(img, (224, 224))\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)\nedges = cv2.Canny(blur, 100, 200)</pre><p>The image after resize has shape (224,224,3). Grayscale and edges usually have shape (224,224). Canny thresholds concern gradient evidence. They are neither class probabilities nor a selected interval of pixel brightness.</p><h3>Explain the mechanism and the tradeoff</h3><p>Resize standardizes geometry but can remove small lesions or distort shape when aspect ratio is changed. Grayscale simplifies the input and can reduce dependence on color variation, but loses useful discoloration cues. Gaussian blur reduces rapid noise fluctuations before derivatives, but can erase narrow real boundaries. Edge maps can help with contour information while discarding color and much texture.</p><p>Choose transformations because they address a data problem. Compare them using validation data. More preprocessing is not a guarantee of higher classification accuracy.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Resize a 640×480 image to a 256×256 input without stretching or cropping.","steps":["Use scale=min(256/640,256/480)=0.4.","The resized image is 256×192.","The remaining 64 vertical pixels form32 pixels of padding above and below.","Use the same deterministic policy at inference."],"answer":"Resize to(width,height)=(256,192), then pad top/bottom by 32 pixels."}],"traps":["RGB2GRAY is inappropriate for an image still in default BGR order.","An explicitly supplied positive Gaussian kernel size must be odd.","A clean-looking or edge-only image may discard the signal needed for classification.","Validate image loading before passing the result to cvtColor."],"check":["For resize(img,(320,240)), what NumPy shape do you expect for a BGR image?","Which information disappears after grayscale conversion and edge extraction?"]},{"id":"su02","title":"System design and believable evaluation","topic":"Evaluation and system design","priority":"sample","sources":["Original sample Questions 2 and 5","Lecture 2 validation/cross-validation","Lecture 5 training and augmentation"],"summary":"Connect dataset organization, preprocessing, model choice and evaluation to the deployment setting.","body":"<h3>Define what counts as a new example</h3><p>For waste classification, labels must distinguish Plastic, Paper, Glass and Other consistently. Check class counts, label errors and near-duplicate captures. Several photographs of the same item or the same capture session may be dependent. Keep these together when splitting, so test performance measures the intended generalization.</p><h3>Split before making data-dependent choices</h3><p>A 70/15/15 split of 2,000 images gives 1,400 training,300 validation and300 test examples. Stratify where possible and preserve dependency groups. An 80/20 split can also work if validation is drawn from training or training-only cross-validation handles tuning.</p><p>Fit learned normalization statistics using training data. Generate augmentation after splitting and apply it to training examples. Choose transformations, hyperparameters and models using validation. Keep the final test labels out of these choices.</p><h3>Choose metrics that reveal failure</h3><p>Accuracy is useful when the class distribution and costs make it meaningful. For imbalance, add per-class precision, recall, macro-F1 and a confusion matrix. Precision concerns the reliability of positive predictions; recall concerns how many real positives you found. Macro-F1 averages each class's F1 equally.</p><h3>Recognize two different failure patterns</h3><p>A training/test accuracy gap suggests overfitting, while good in-domain scores and poor new-phone scores suggest domain shift. Inspect both data and evaluation before changing architecture. Background shortcuts, camera response, lighting and capture conditions can all cause failures.</p>","worked":[{"question":"A defect detector hasTP=30,FP=10,FN=20. Calculate precision, recall and F1.","steps":["Precision=30/(30+10)=0.75.","Recall=30/(30+20)=0.60.","F1=2×0.75×0.60/(0.75+0.60)=2/3≈0.6667."],"answer":"Precision75%, recall60%, F1≈66.7%. State defective as the positive class."}],"traps":["Do not tune repeatedly against the final test set.","A random split does not fix dependency leakage or shared background shortcuts.","High accuracy can hide failure on a rare but important class.","Neither raw scores nor normalized softmax values establish calibration."],"check":["What specific deployment change would you test for a model trained with one phone?","Why can augmentation before splitting inflate test accuracy?"]},{"id":"nu01","title":"What the learning signal changes","topic":"Learning concepts","priority":"reading","sources":["Trask Ch. 1, printed pp. 3–8","Trask Ch. 2, printed pp. 10–19","Lecture 4 slides 17–21"],"summary":"Separate the learning objective from how the model stores what it learns.","body":"<p>Chapters 1–2 supply the framework for the later calculations: understand the operations that make a prediction, compare that prediction with a learning signal, and change the stored model. Merely running prediction repeatedly does not train a network.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Distinction</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where does the signal come from?</td><td>Supervised learning uses target information. Unsupervised learning seeks structure without those category targets.</td><td>An image can be used in either setting; input format does not decide supervision.</td></tr><tr><td>How is the model represented?</td><td>A fixed architecture has a fixed parameter count. A nonparametric method can grow its representation with the data.</td><td>A basic kNN stores examples; an MLP stores a fixed collection of coefficients.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These axes are independent. An unsupervised model can be parametric. Nonparametric does not mean that nothing is stored or that there are literally no numerical quantities.</p>\n<p>A feedforward prediction uses current input and learned parameters. Previous inference inputs are not automatically recurrent state. Separately, a low training loss establishes fit to the training examples; held-out evidence is needed for generalization.</p>","worked":[{"question":"A fixed 3072→100→10 MLP is trained on twice as many images. Which model quantities necessarily double?","steps":["The number of examples doubles.","The layer dimensions and connections are unchanged.","Therefore the parameter count does not double; data storage and work per epoch can increase."],"answer":"Dataset size doubles; the fixed model parameter count stays unchanged."}],"traps":["Supervised/unsupervised and parametric/nonparametric are different distinctions.","A cache used for backward is not a recurrent memory of prior inference inputs.","Zero training error is not proof of held-out accuracy."],"check":["Can you explain what changes in predict–compare–learn?","Can you predict whether model storage grows when the dataset grows?"]},{"id":"nu02","title":"Forward prediction, dot products and matrix orientation","topic":"Forward prediction","priority":"reading","sources":["Trask Ch. 3, printed pp. 24–46"],"summary":"Track which coefficients belong to each input and output before performing the arithmetic.","body":"<p>With one output, the model adds the contributions of its features. With several outputs, each output owns a separate weight vector. Store one vector per row under the column-vector convention:</p>\n<pre>p = Wx                 W: C×D, x: D×1, p: C×1\np_j = Σ_k W_jk x_k\nP = XWᵀ                X: N×D, P: N×C</pre>\n<p>Do not mix the row-batch convention with the column-vector convention. A transpose changes orientation, not the feature–weight pairing. Gradients later must use the same convention.</p>\n<p>The dot product is a useful template response, but its magnitude depends on vector lengths as well as alignment. It is not automatically a normalized similarity or a probability. A large feature can dominate even if its coefficient is smaller than another coefficient. Permuting features preserves the response only when the coefficients are permuted identically.</p>\n<p>Chapter 3 also composes predictions. If there is no nonlinear activation between the maps, the composition remains linear: W₂(W₁x)=(W₂W₁)x. This prepares the reason Lecture 4 inserts an activation.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Compute the book’s three-input prediction with x=[8.5,0.65,1.2] and w=[0.1,0.2,0].","steps":["Pair the feature with its own coefficient.","Contributions: 8.5·0.1=0.85; 0.65·0.2=0.13; 1.2·0=0.","Sum the three contributions."],"answer":"0.98."}],"traps":["A zero coefficient ignores that feature in the present forward pass but can still receive a gradient.","A linear output need not lie between 0 and 1.","A compatible shape is necessary, but feature semantics must match as well."],"check":["Can you derive output shape for a row-major batch?","Can you explain how input scale changes a template response?"]},{"id":"nu03","title":"Linear classifiers: shapes, bias and geometry","topic":"Linear classifiers","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 3 slides 8–32","Lecture 5 slides 3–4,13–14"],"summary":"Read the same classifier as a matrix, a bank of templates and a collection of hyperplanes.","body":"<pre>s = Wx + b             W: C×D, b: C\npredicted class = argmax_j s_j\naugmented x = [x; 1]    augmented W = [W b]</pre>\n<p>Algebraically, each row of W computes one class score. Visually, reshape that row into an image-sized template. Geometrically, equality of two class scores defines (w_a−w_b)·x+(b_a−b_b)=0. The normal comes from the difference of class weights; a class’s own zero-score plane need not be a decision boundary.</p>\n<p>The bias translates the boundary. The bias trick absorbs it into a coefficient of a constant feature. It does not make an affine map nonlinear. Scaling obeys f(cx)=cf(x)+(1−c)b; the lecture’s simpler identity assumes no bias.</p>\n<p>A single binary boundary cannot isolate XOR, an annulus or several separated modes. Extra depth without nonlinearities still collapses to an affine classifier.</p>\n<p><strong>Clarification:</strong> flattening keeps the values and a recoverable ordering. A fully connected layer lacks enforced locality and weight sharing; flattening itself does not delete pixel indices. Its parameter count grows with image size: C(D+1) with one bias per class.</p>","worked":[{"question":"For x=[56,231,24,2], compute the dog response to [1.5,1.3,2.1,0] with bias 3.2.","steps":["The four products are 84,300.3,50.4,0.","Add the products to obtain 434.7.","Add the class bias 3.2."],"answer":"Dog score = 437.9."}],"traps":["Argmax uses scores; softmax preserves their ordering.","One class template cannot flexibly represent every orientation or mode.","Without scaling the bias too, scaling W alone need not scale every affine score."],"check":["Can you count weights and biases separately?","Can you write the boundary between two classes?","Can you explain XOR’s geometric obstruction?"]},{"id":"nu04","title":"Summed multiclass hinge loss and margins","topic":"Losses and regularization","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 3 slides 40–58"],"summary":"Correct class is highest is weaker than correct class beats every rival by the margin.","body":"<pre>L_i = Σ_(j≠y_i) max(0, s_j − s_yi + Δ)\nL_data = (1/N) Σ_i L_i</pre>\n<p>Compute one term for each incorrect class. Negative margin expressions contribute zero. A positive expression contributes its entire value. A correctly classified example can still have positive loss if the score gap is too small.</p>\n<p>Minimum data loss is zero, while the loss is unbounded above. With all C scores equal and Δ=1, the summed loss is C−1. Including the correct class adds the constant Δ, rather than a useful constraint. Averaging class terms instead of summing rescales the data-loss term and changes its balance with an unchanged penalty.</p>\n<p>Once all margins are satisfied, small score changes may leave the data loss exactly zero. The zero-loss weights need not be unique. If every bias-free score gap already meets Δ, scaling all weights upward preserves zero loss. With affine scores, that argument scales the full affine parameterization, including biases. Regularization can express a preference between such solutions.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Use the lecture’s first score column [3.2,5.1,−1.7]; the correct class is the first entry (Cat), and the margin is 1.","steps":["Second entry (Car): 5.1−3.2+1=2.9.","Third entry (Frog): −1.7−3.2+1=−3.9.","Apply max(0,·) to each expression and add."],"answer":"2.9. The mean of the lecture’s three example losses is (2.9+0+12.9)/3=5.266666… ."}],"traps":["Do not include the correct class in the sum.","Do not confuse a correct argmax with a satisfied margin.","A sum over classes and a mean over examples are separate operations."],"check":["Can you compute every rival’s hinge term?","Can you identify the flat zero-loss region?","Can you explain why regularization is useful even when training data loss is zero?"]},{"id":"nu05","title":"Softmax, cross-entropy and confidence","topic":"Losses and regularization","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 3 slides 70–90","Lecture 4 slides 22,29"],"summary":"Normalize scores, choose the target’s probability, and retain the correct logarithm convention.","body":"<pre>p_j = exp(s_j) / Σ_k exp(s_k)\nL_i = −ln(p_yi) = ln(Σ_k exp(s_k)) − s_yi\nstable softmax: replace every s_j by s_j − max_k s_k</pre>\n<p>The denominator makes the class probabilities sum to one. It also means every rival affects the loss even though the one-hot expression selects only the target’s log probability. Increasing the target score relative to rivals reduces the loss.</p>\n<p>Adding the same constant to all scores changes nothing. Multiplying them by a positive factor preserves argmax but changes confidence. A high probability can still be poorly calibrated; normalization supplies a probability model, not a calibration certificate.</p>\n<p>For C equal logits, p_y=1/C and L=ln C. <strong>Lecture 3 slide 84 has a sign typo:</strong> its −log(C) should be log(C); the stated log(10)≈2.3 numerical example is right. For more than one class and finite logits, cross-entropy is strictly positive; its infimum is zero, approached as p_y→1. Unlike hinge loss, it has no finite zero-loss plateau.</p>\n<p>For sigmoid with binary cross-entropy, the combined derivative with respect to the output logit is p−y. The sigmoid derivative must not be multiplied in a second time.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Logits are [0,ln 2,ln 3] and the third class is correct.","steps":["Exponentiate to obtain [1,2,3].","Normalize by 6: the correct-class probability is 0.5.","Use natural logarithms: −ln 0.5."],"answer":"L=0.693147… ."}],"traps":["For one-hot targets, evaluate −ln p for the correct class, not for the predicted class.","Use ln C, not −ln C, for uniform initialization.","Subtracting a common constant and scaling logits have different effects."],"check":["Can you compute probabilities without large exponentials?","Can you compare cross-entropy’s behavior with hinge loss?","Can you explain why logits are not calibrated probabilities?"]},{"id":"nu06","title":"Regularization as a preference between fitted models","topic":"Losses and regularization","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 3 slides 59–69","Lecture 5 slide 23"],"summary":"Read λ relative to the data-loss scale and distinguish different regularization mechanisms.","body":"<pre>L_total = L_data + λR(W)\nR_L2(W) = Σ_j W_j²     ∂(λR_L2)/∂W_j = 2λW_j\nR_L1(W) = Σ_j |W_j|</pre>\n<p>Data loss asks the model to fit the observed targets. A penalty expresses additional preferences, such as limiting coefficient magnitude or favouring simpler fitted behavior. Its purpose is to reduce unwanted fitting of noise and improve generalization; making training performance worse is not an end in itself.</p>\n<p>L2 penalizes concentrated large coefficients more strongly. For inputs [1,1], [1,0] and [0.5,0.5] give the same score, but squared L2 norms are 1 and 0.5. Their L1 norms are both 1. L1 can encourage sparsity; its derivative at zero requires a subgradient convention.</p>\n<p>Check whether a question uses λ‖W‖² or (λ/2)‖W‖². The derivative differs by two. Also check whether the data loss is a sum or mean; the same numerical λ has a different relative effect if only the data term is rescaled.</p>\n<p>Dropout and augmentation do not simply add the same scalar penalty. They change training behavior or examples. Batch normalization can have a regularizing effect but also directly changes the parameterization and optimization.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Use R=0.1(1²+(−2)²). Compute the penalty and its gradient vector.","steps":["Squared norm is 1+4=5.","Penalty is 0.1·5=0.5.","Differentiate without a 1/2 factor: 2·0.1·[1,−2]."],"answer":"Penalty 0.5; gradient [0.2,−0.4]."}],"traps":["Changing a sum to a mean can change the effective regularization strength.","Weight count alone does not determine generalization.","The convention for penalizing biases must be stated; many problems count them but do not regularize them."],"check":["Can you differentiate the exact penalty written in the question?","Can you choose between two equally fitted weight vectors?"]},{"id":"nu07","title":"Scalar gradient descent: residual, derivative and update","topic":"Gradient descent","priority":"reading","sources":["Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 48–76"],"summary":"Keep the error value, signed residual and parameter gradient distinct.","body":"<pre>p = wx\nδ = p − y\nE = δ²                 dE/dw = 2δx\nL = δ²/2               dL/dw = δx\nw_new = w − η(dL/dw)</pre>\n<p>The book displays E=δ² but uses weight_delta=δx. This corresponds to the derivative of half-squared loss; equivalently, the missing factor two can be absorbed into the learning-rate convention. In an exam, use the explicit loss and update given, rather than silently switching conventions.</p>\n<p>Multiplication by x performs the book’s three effects. A zero input stops that weight’s update because it cannot affect the prediction. A negative input reverses the required direction. A larger input changes sensitivity and can cause much larger updates.</p>\n<p>The derivative gives local sensitivity. Subtracting it is a local descent direction, but a large finite step can overshoot. A learning rate less than one is not a universal safety rule. For a repeated scalar example with half-squared loss, weight error evolves by a factor 1−ηx²; convergence requires 0&lt;η&lt;2/x² when x≠0.</p>\n<p>Hot-and-cold testing moves by a fixed increment after comparing nearby errors. Its step cannot automatically shrink with error and can oscillate near the optimum.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Use x=0.5,w=0.5,y=0.8 and the book update with η=0.1.","steps":["p=0.25; δ=−0.55; displayed squared error E=0.3025.","weight_delta=δx=−0.275.","w_new=0.5−0.1(−0.275)."],"answer":"w_new=0.5275. For full squared-error derivative with η=0.1 instead, w_new=0.555."}],"traps":["A positive squared error says nothing about the required sign of the weight change.","The full square has derivative 2δx; the half-square has derivative δx.","Training is not guaranteed to reach zero error for arbitrary data, architecture or step size."],"check":["Can you explain negative reversal using a negative x?","Can you state exactly which loss your derivative uses?","Can you distinguish local descent from a guarantee about a whole finite step?"]},{"id":"nu08","title":"Learning several weights and outputs together","topic":"Gradient descent","priority":"reading","sources":["Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 80–98","Lecture 4 slide 30"],"summary":"Each weight gets its destination’s residual times its source’s activation.","body":"<pre>p = Wx                 δ = p − y\nL = (1/2)Σ_j δ_j²\n∂L/∂W_jk = δ_j x_k     ∂L/∂W = δxᵀ\nW_new = W − ηδxᵀ</pre>\n<p>Multiple inputs share one residual if they feed the same output. Multiple outputs sharing one input use different residuals but the same input value. The outer product combines the two cases. Its orientation must match the weight matrix: rows are outputs and columns are inputs here.</p>\n<p>A zero input blocks its coefficient’s data gradient; a zero coefficient does not. Freezing a coefficient deliberately sets its update to zero, even if the true gradient is nonzero. The other coefficients may compensate, but the model’s adjustable directions are reduced.</p>\n<p><strong>Printed-code corrections:</strong> pp. 91–93 contain inconsistent snippets. For multiple outputs use input times delta, not input times weights. Preserve delta[i] rather than overwriting the residual vector with a scalar. With output rows, use outer(delta,input), rather than an input-row outer product. The equations and destination/source indexing resolve these issues without copying flawed snippets.</p>\n<p>Chapter 5’s digit templates help interpret learned positive and negative contributions. A large dot product is a class response; plain weighted sums are not constrained probabilities.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Take x=[8.5,0.65,1.2], w=[0.1,0.2,−0.1], y=1 and η=0.01 for one output.","steps":["p=0.85+0.13−0.12=0.86; δ=−0.14.","Gradient vector is [−1.19,−0.091,−0.168].","Subtract η times each corresponding gradient."],"answer":"New weights: [0.1119,0.20091,−0.09832]."}],"traps":["An outer product’s orientation depends on how W is stored.","The output’s target must appear through its residual.","Do not recompute later gradients using weights already updated in the same nominal simultaneous step."],"check":["Can you write the gradient for any indexed connection?","Can you repair a snippet by checking vector shape and target dependence?","Can you predict which gradients are blocked when one feature is zero?"]},{"id":"nu09","title":"Activation functions and the path of the gradient","topic":"Neural networks","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 4 slides 9,12–14,28","Lecture 5 slide 20"],"summary":"Nonlinearity changes representational power; local slopes change trainability.","body":"<table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Value</th><th>Local derivative</th><th>Main concern</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sigmoid</td><td>1/(1+e^(−z))</td><td>p(1−p)</td><td>Small slope at extreme z; output not zero-centred</td></tr><tr><td>tanh</td><td>tanh(z)</td><td>1−a²</td><td>Zero-centred but still saturates</td></tr><tr><td>ReLU</td><td>max(0,z)</td><td>1 for z&gt;0, 0 for z&lt;0</td><td>Negative branch can block learning</td></tr><tr><td>Leaky ReLU</td><td>max(0.01z,z)</td><td>1 or 0.01 away from zero</td><td>Allows a small negative-side slope</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A ReLU’s nonlinearity comes from selecting different branches. A stack of affine layers without an intervening nonlinear function remains affine, even if there are biases. Sigmoid or tanh saturation gives very small local derivatives, which multiply along a chain. ReLU avoids that saturation on its positive branch, but its negative branch is flat.</p>\n<p>A dead unit stays inactive across the relevant data, rather than merely producing zero on one example. Leaky ReLU addresses the flat negative side. The lecture also names GELU, g(z)=zΦ(z), for smooth activation gating used in newer architectures.</p>\n<p>At z=0, ReLU is nondifferentiable: the implementation selects a convention. The formulas above are for points strictly on one branch.</p>","worked":[{"question":"A sigmoid output is p=0.8 and upstream gradient is −2. Find the input gradient.","steps":["Local derivative is 0.8·0.2=0.16.","Apply the chain rule: local derivative times upstream.","Keep the negative sign of the upstream gradient."],"answer":"−0.32. Five consecutive local derivatives of 0.1 would instead attenuate a unit upstream signal to 10^−5."}],"traps":["Do not call ReLU non-saturating everywhere.","The sign of a single weight does not determine whether a neuron is active.","A probability-producing output activation and a hidden activation serve different purposes."],"check":["Can you derive the slope from a sigmoid or tanh output value?","Can you distinguish zero activation from a permanently dead unit?","Can you explain why repeated small derivatives attenuate gradients?"]},{"id":"nu10","title":"Hidden features, forward passes and network capacity","topic":"Neural networks","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 4 slides 4–22"],"summary":"A hidden layer learns several responses that the output layer can combine.","body":"<pre>h = ReLU(W₁x+b₁)\ns = W₂h+b₂\nparameter count for D→H→C = DH + H + HC + C</pre>\n<p>The hidden layer is a learned feature map. Different responses can support different appearances of one class, while the output combines them. The template interpretation is useful, but hidden units need not be literal named parts.</p>\n<p>A constructive XOR example on binary inputs uses h₁=ReLU(x₁+x₂), h₂=ReLU(x₁+x₂−1), and output h₁−2h₂. Its four outputs are 0,1,1,0. Removing the nonlinear operations would collapse this into an affine map and lose that behavior.</p>\n<p>For the lecture’s 3072→100→10 network, affine-layer parameter counts are 307,300 and 1,010, totaling 308,310. The input and ReLU layers add no learned coefficients.</p>\n<p>Architecture follows the structure and objective: MLP for fixed vectors, CNN for grids, recurrent state for sequences, Transformer attention for tokens or patches, autoencoder for compression/reconstruction, GAN for adversarial generation, and Siamese shared encoders for pairwise comparison.</p>\n<p>For the survey terms in slide 20, distinguish their mechanisms: an RBF network uses radial-basis responses for function approximation; a self-organizing map forms a neighborhood-preserving unsupervised map; a deep belief network stacks stochastic layers; capsule networks represent part–whole relationships; and spiking networks use event-like activations for neuromorphic settings. These are architecture concepts, not interchangeable synonyms for a standard MLP.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Run the spam example x=[1,0,1] with H₁ weights [0.5,−0.2,0.3], H₂ [0.4,0.1,−0.5], output weights [0.7,0.2], and all biases zero.","steps":["Hidden preactivations are 0.8 and −0.1.","ReLU gives [0.8,0].","Output logit is 0.7·0.8+0.2·0=0.56.","Sigmoid gives the model’s spam probability."],"answer":"p=0.6364525…; threshold 0.5 predicts spam."}],"traps":["Count both biases, not just matrix weights.","A larger network can overfit; smaller size is not the only possible regularization choice.","The input layer is usually not counted as a learned affine layer."],"check":["Can you run the hidden and output stages without changing weights?","Can you explain the constructive XOR feature map?","Can you match a learning objective to an architecture?"]},{"id":"nu11","title":"Backpropagation through gates and branches","topic":"Backpropagation","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 4 slides 23–29,31,33"],"summary":"Cache forward values, multiply along each path and add at shared inputs.","body":"<pre>upstream = ∂L/∂output\ninput gradient = local derivative × upstream</pre>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Backward behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a+b</td><td>Send the upstream gradient unchanged to both inputs.</td></tr><tr><td>ab</td><td>Send g·b to a and g·a to b.</td></tr><tr><td>max(a,b)</td><td>Route the gradient to the unique winning branch; specify a tie convention.</td></tr><tr><td>One value reused</td><td>Add every branch’s contribution at that value.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Backpropagation applies the chain rule in reverse graph order. The local derivative must use the cached forward values. A variable reused in several places has several routes to the loss, so overwriting rather than summing its gradient loses information.</p>\n<p>Backpropagation computes parameter gradients. The optimizer then uses those gradients to change the parameters. These are distinct operations.</p>\n<p>In the spam example, sigmoid plus BCE supplies p−y=−0.363547… at the output logit. The first output connection gets this times 0.8. The inactive hidden unit blocks its upstream signal at ReLU. A zero input feature blocks only its own connection gradient.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Backpropagate f=(x+y)z with x=−2,y=5,z=−4.","steps":["Forward: q=x+y=3 and f=qz=−12.","Seed ∂f/∂f=1.","Multiply backward: ∂f/∂q=z=−4 and ∂f/∂z=q=3.","Add backward: ∂f/∂x=∂f/∂y=−4."],"answer":"Forward f=−12; gradients for (x,y,z) are (−4,−4,3)."}],"traps":["The upstream gradient may be negative; max routing does not take its absolute value.","The multiply gate uses the other input.","Shared inputs require summed gradients.","A cached value from a different forward pass gives an incorrect derivative."],"check":["Can you label every intermediate value and its local derivative?","Can you explain gradient accumulation at a copied variable?","Can you distinguish gradient computation from the optimizer update?"]},{"id":"nu12","title":"Vector backpropagation and numerical checks","topic":"Backpropagation","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 4 slides 30–33"],"summary":"A gradient has its variable’s shape, and a numerical reference can expose a wrong backward pass.","body":"<pre>y = Wx+b               g = ∂L/∂y\n∂L/∂W = gxᵀ            ∂L/∂x = Wᵀg\n∂L/∂b = g              for one example\ncentral difference ≈ [L(w+h)−L(w−h)]/(2h)</pre>\n<p>For batches, contributions to a shared parameter add. If the loss is a mean, its averaging factor must be included exactly once. Shape compatibility helps diagnose transposes, but it is not a proof that the derivative represents the right computation.</p>\n<p>Numerical checks perturb one coefficient and rerun the forward function. They are too expensive for routine training but useful on a small smooth test case. Compare the numerical and analytic gradients with an absolute or relative error measure that handles near-zero values sensibly.</p>\n<p>The lecture suggests h≈10^−5 and relative error below 10^−7. These are useful heuristics, not universal correctness guarantees: precision, magnitude, finite-difference cancellation and nondifferentiable hinges/ReLUs can change the expected discrepancy. Do not check across an active-branch change without interpreting it.</p>\n<p>In a normal per-batch training loop, clear accumulated parameter gradients before the next backward pass. Intentional gradient accumulation must scale or interpret the effective update consistently.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Use x=[1,−2]ᵀ, W=[[3,4],[−1,2]], and g=[0.5,−1]ᵀ.","steps":["Form gxᵀ row by row.","The first row is [0.5,−1]; the second is [−1,2].","Compute Wᵀg for the input gradient.","Sum each input’s contributions from the two outputs."],"answer":"∂L/∂W=[[0.5,−1],[−1,2]]; ∂L/∂x=[2.5,0]ᵀ; ∂L/∂b=[0.5,−1]ᵀ."}],"traps":["Do not include the mean-loss factor twice.","An analytic derivative may disagree with a central difference at a kink.","Backward() accumulates gradients unless the code clears them."],"check":["Can you obtain every derivative shape before calculating values?","Can you explain why central difference is a debugging reference rather than a training algorithm?","Can you identify when a gradient-check mismatch is ambiguous?"]},{"id":"nu13","title":"CNN convolution: operation, shape and shared parameters","topic":"CNN operations","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 5 slides 4–16,24","Lecture 4 slides 28,30"],"summary":"A filter is local spatially, spans the input depth and is reused across output positions.","body":"<pre>O = floor((W−K+2P)/S)+1       apply separately to H and W\nordinary conv parameters = C_out(K²C_in+1)\noutput depth = number of filters = C_out</pre>\n<p>A standard CNN layer computes cross-correlation: multiply a patch with the displayed filter and sum, adding its bias. Mathematical convolution flips the kernel along both spatial axes. A fixed-kernel calculation must state which operation is intended.</p>\n<p>For a color image, one ordinary filter has depth C_in; it sums spatial and channel contributions to one output feature map. Different filters give different channels. The kernel coefficients and one bias are reused at every position, so resolution changes computation without changing this parameter count.</p>\n<p>Stride changes the spacing of sampled windows. Padding changes what windows fit at the border. For odd K and stride 1, symmetric P=(K−1)/2 preserves width. The simple formula assumes no dilation.</p>\n<p>A shared coefficient participates in many output positions, so its backward gradient sums all of those contributions: each contribution is the upstream gradient at that output times the corresponding patch value. The filter bias similarly sums upstream values. An input pixel receives contributions from every window that used it. Input-gradient expressions can involve flipped kernels, but stride and padding must also be accounted for; copying the forward operation blindly is insufficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Slide 11 clarification:</strong> its table rejects W=7,K=3,S=3,P=0 as a non-integer exact fit, but the stated floor formula gives O=2. Standard floor sizing uses only fully fitting windows and leaves a border remainder. If an exam instead requires exact tiling, state that convention explicitly.</p>","worked":[{"question":"A 32×32×3 input meets ten 5×5 filters, stride 1, padding 2, with one bias per filter.","steps":["Spatial output: floor((32−5+4)/1)+1=32.","Depth is ten, one channel per filter.","Each filter has 5·5·3+1=76 parameters.","Reuse these parameters at every output position."],"answer":"Output 32×32×10; 760 trainable parameters."}],"traps":["Do not multiply parameter count by output area.","Kernel depth equals input channels for an ordinary dense-channel convolution.","Padding is per side, so the spatial formula uses 2P.","Output depth is controlled by filter count, not input RGB depth."],"check":["Can you compute one filter response by hand?","Can you separately derive spatial shape, depth and parameter count?","Can you handle a non-exact stride fit under an explicit floor convention?"]},{"id":"nu14","title":"Pooling, receptive fields and translation behavior","topic":"CNN operations","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 5 slides 17–21,29,34"],"summary":"Track spatial coverage and downsampling separately from the number of channels.","body":"<p>Max and average pooling reduce spatial resolution independently in each channel and ordinarily add no trainable coefficients. A 2×2 stride-2 pool maps 16×16×32 to 8×8×32. Max-pool backward routes gradients to the selected maxima; ties need a convention.</p>\n<pre>r₀=1, j₀=1\nr_l = r_(l−1) + (K_l−1)j_(l−1)\nj_l = j_(l−1) S_l\nstride-1, equal kernels: r = 1 + L(K−1)</pre>\n<p>Here r is receptive-field width in original-input coordinates and j is spacing between neighboring feature positions in those coordinates. Downsampling increases j, so later kernels expand the receptive field faster. This is theoretical coverage, not a claim that every covered pixel has equal learned influence.</p>\n<p>Global average pooling averages each channel’s entire spatial plane. It maps 7×7×512 to 512 values and can greatly shrink the final fully connected head.</p>\n<p>Translation equivariance means a translated input produces a corresponding translated feature map, subject to boundary and stride effects. Classification invariance means the output does not change. Convolution alone does not guarantee that final invariance; local pooling offers limited tolerance, not perfect invariance to every shift.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Track the receptive field through conv 3×3 S1 → pool 2×2 S2 → conv 3×3 S1.","steps":["Start r=1,j=1.","First conv gives r=3,j=1.","Pool gives r=4,j=2.","Final conv gives r=4+2·2=8,j=2."],"answer":"The output unit can depend on an 8×8 input region."}],"traps":["Three 3×3 stride-1 kernels have receptive field 7, not 9.","Pool preserves channels unless another operation changes them.","Flatten counts spatial values times channels, excluding batch size.","Equivariance and invariance are different claims."],"check":["Can you update both receptive-field size and input-coordinate jump?","Can you compute the values of a pooled feature map?","Can you explain why global pooling reduces classifier size?"]},{"id":"nu15","title":"CNN training: batch norm, regularization and evaluation","topic":"CNN training","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 5 slides 20–24,39","Lecture 4 slides 16,22,33"],"summary":"Keep learned coefficients, batch statistics and evaluation choices distinct.","body":"<pre>x_hat = (x−μ_B)/sqrt(σ_B²+ε)\ny = γx_hat+β\nW_new = W−η∇_W L</pre>\n<p>Convolutional batch normalization forms mean and variance for each channel over the batch and spatial positions. Its learned scale and shift contribute two affine parameters per channel. During evaluation, the lecture’s model uses running statistics collected during training, rather than current evaluation-batch statistics. The usual course order is Conv→BN→ReLU.</p>\n<p>Weight decay or an L2 penalty limits coefficient magnitude. Dropout masks activations during training; under inverted dropout, kept values are divided by their keep probability. Augmentation produces plausible label-preserving training variants. Whether a flip, crop or color change preserves the target is task-dependent.</p>\n<p>A training step runs forward scores, loss, backward gradients and an optimizer update. Momentum combines past and present gradient information; a learning-rate schedule changes the step-size multiplier. The lecture’s OneCycle schedule warms up and then decays.</p>\n<p>For model selection, monitor training and validation performance. Repeatedly adjusting a model using the test set converts that test set into validation evidence. Keep an untouched test assessment for the final report. For a small transfer-learning dataset, the lecture suggests freezing the pretrained backbone and training a new classifier; more data can justify fine-tuning more layers with a small learning rate.</p>","worked":[{"question":"One BN channel has x=10,mean=6,variance=4,ε=0,γ=2,β=−1.","steps":["Standardize: (10−6)/sqrt(4)=2.","Scale and shift: 2·2−1.","Separate these learned affine parameters from the mean/variance statistics."],"answer":"Output is 3. With 64 channels, γ and β add 128 trainable affine parameters."}],"traps":["Do not add a softmax before the CrossEntropyLoss shown in the lecture.","Do not accidentally leave BN/dropout in training mode for ordinary evaluation.","Do not tune repeatedly on the final test split.","The meaning of p in dropout must be fixed as drop probability or keep probability."],"check":["Can you distinguish a scheduler from momentum?","Can you count BN’s trainable affine parameters?","Can you explain how train/validation behavior reveals overfitting?"]},{"id":"nu16","title":"CNN architectures: identify the mechanism, not just the name","topic":"CNN architectures","priority":"lecture","sources":["Lecture 5 slides 25–39"],"summary":"Connect each family to the problem its design addresses and interpret the course experiment cautiously.","body":"<table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Course-relevant mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>LeNet</td><td>Early learned convolution and pooling hierarchy before a classifier.</td></tr><tr><td>AlexNet</td><td>ReLU, dropout, augmentation and GPU computation helped train a larger network.</td></tr><tr><td>VGG</td><td>Stack small 3×3 kernels to grow coverage with additional nonlinear transformations.</td></tr><tr><td>GoogLeNet / Inception</td><td>Parallel branches at different scales; 1×1 bottlenecks reduce costly channel depth; concatenate outputs.</td></tr><tr><td>ResNet</td><td>Learn F(x)=H(x)−x with y=x+F(x), making identity-like solutions and gradient paths easier.</td></tr><tr><td>DenseNet</td><td>Concatenate earlier features for reuse; this is different from residual addition.</td></tr><tr><td>MobileNet</td><td>Depthwise spatial filtering followed by 1×1 channel mixing.</td></tr><tr><td>EfficientNet</td><td>Coordinate depth, width and input-resolution scaling.</td></tr><tr><td>ViT</td><td>Embed image patches and use attention over tokens with position information.</td></tr><tr><td>ConvNeXt</td><td>Modernized convolutional design with depthwise kernels, LayerNorm and GELU.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A 1×1 convolution is a learned channel map at each spatial position, not a spatial neighborhood filter. An Inception bottleneck can reduce the channels before a 3×3 or 5×5 operation. Residual addition requires compatible tensors; a projected shortcut can match spatial resolution and channel depth.</p>\n<p>Degradation refers to worse training fit as a plain network gets deeper. It differs from overfitting, where training fit may be good but held-out behavior deteriorates. Residuals help optimization; they do not guarantee the best result in every shallow, short-training experiment.</p>\n<p>The slide 38 CIFAR comparison reports a specific subset/CPU/short-training setup. SimpleCNN has fewer parameters and higher accuracy than its LeNet-style baseline. VGGMini’s 70.1% exceeds ResNetMini’s 63.7% in that setup; this does not establish a universal ranking of the architecture families.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Reduce channels before a 5×5 convolution: 28×28×192 → 1×1 with 16 outputs → 5×5 with 32 outputs. Ignore biases and preserve spatial size.","steps":["Direct 5×5→32 cost is 28²·192·25·32=120,422,400 multiplies.","The 1×1 reduction costs 28²·192·16=2,408,448.","The reduced 5×5 costs 28²·16·25·32=10,035,200.","Total bottleneck cost is 12,443,648; divide direct by bottleneck."],"answer":"About 9.677 times fewer multiplies for this specified design; both outputs have shape 28×28×32."}],"traps":["Concatenation adds channels; addition matches existing channels entry by entry.","Equal receptive field does not mean equal function.","A shortcut cannot add incompatible shapes without a matching operation.","Course experiment accuracy is evidence for that setup, not a universal architecture constant."],"check":["Can you justify a 1×1 bottleneck with arithmetic?","Can you distinguish ResNet addition from DenseNet concatenation?","Can you explain what problem residual learning targets?","Can you interpret the experimental comparison without generalizing beyond its setup?"]},{"id":"iu01","title":"Scope, inverse problems, and evidence","topic":"Vision foundations","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 1, printed pp. 1-32 (PDF pp. 27-58)","CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 1-83"],"summary":"Know what the assigned sources cover, why pixels underdetermine a scene, and how to evaluate a proposed method.","body":"<p><strong>Coverage distinction:</strong> Lecture 2 teaches image classification, datasets, nearest-neighbor methods, and validation. Szeliski Chapters 2 and 3 add image formation and processing; those are assigned-reading coverage here. No claim is made that a topic is likely to appear on the exam merely because it is in the book.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Assigned source</th><th>Substantive coverage in these units</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Szeliski Ch. 1, §§1.1-1.5</td><td>Inverse vision, applications and constraints, scientific/statistical/engineering/data approaches, history context, dependency overview, column-vector notation. The syllabus and additional-reading lists are context rather than extra examinable algorithms.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Szeliski Ch. 2, §2.1</td><td>Points, lines, planes, conics/quadrics as representations; 2D/3D transforms and rotations; orthographic/perspective projection; intrinsics/extrinsics; depth and planar mappings; lens distortion.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Szeliski Ch. 2, §§2.2-2.3</td><td>Lighting, BRDF/diffuse/specular/Phong/global illumination, optics, sensing/noise, sampling/aliasing, color, gamma, compression and PSNR.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Szeliski Ch. 3, §§3.1-3.3</td><td>Point/color operators, alpha compositing/matting, histograms/tonal adjustment, linear/separable/oriented/recursive filters, nonlinear/bilateral/guided filtering, morphology, distances, connected components.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Szeliski Ch. 3, §§3.4-3.6</td><td>Fourier/DFT/FFT/DCT and restoration, interpolation/decimation, Gaussian/Laplacian pyramids, wavelets/lifting/blending, parametric/mesh warping and morphing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lecture 2</td><td>Semantic gap and nuisance variation, datasets and top-5, L1/L2 and kNN boundaries/costs, hyperparameters, train/validation/test and cross-validation, dimensionality and learned features.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Think of vision as estimating latent causes from observations. Geometry maps a 3D scene to viewing rays; illumination and reflectance determine outgoing light; optics and the sensor map this into pixels. Several causes can produce the same observation. A dark patch may be dark material, a shaded surface, or low exposure. A single perspective image also cannot distinguish proportional changes in object size and distance.</p>\n<p>A physics model exposes ambiguity, a prior or learned representation adds information, and evaluation tells you whether the resulting assumptions work. Specify the output and the conditions first. Use synthetic inputs with a known answer, introduce controlled noise, then test representative real images. A plausible picture alone is weak evidence of correctness.</p>\n<p>Chapter 1's history describes a movement from restricted scene models toward feature/statistical methods and then large-scale learned recognition. Learn this progression as context; the practical link is that large datasets, computation, and learning methods changed what could be estimated reliably.</p>\n<pre>Column-vector convention: apply A first, then B -> B A x.\nFinite homogeneous point: (u,v,w), w != 0 -> (u/w,v/w).\nSame observations do not necessarily imply the same scene.</pre>","worked":[{"question":"Why can two differently sized objects have identical perspective images?","steps":["Model u=fX/Z and v=fY/Z.","Scale every object coordinate and depth by the same factor c.","The ratios cX/(cZ) and cY/(cZ) do not change."],"answer":"Absolute size/depth cannot be recovered from that single projection without extra information."}],"traps":["Do not infer lecturer emphasis from a reading chapter.","A method can look plausible while violating a known synthetic answer.","Historical descriptions in this 2021 draft are not statements about the latest state of the field."],"check":["Explain one ambiguity involving geometry and one involving light.","State a test that measures correctness and one that measures robustness.","Distinguish geometric inference from a semantic category output."]},{"id":"iu02","title":"Classification challenges and what an image distance measures","topic":"Image classification","priority":"lecture","sources":["CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 2-31","CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 76-82"],"summary":"Relate nuisance variation to the semantic gap, know dataset roles, and distinguish top-1 from top-5.","body":"<p>Classification assigns an image to a category, but the numeric array contains no explicit category semantics. The same object changes pixels with viewpoint, illumination, deformation, occlusion, and background. Different examples of one class vary internally; fine-grained classes may differ only subtly. A useful classifier must respond to category evidence while tolerating irrelevant variation.</p>\n<p>A raw-pixel representation is sensitive to location. Translating an image can move every edge onto different coordinates and make an otherwise identical object far away by L1/L2 distance. Occlusion changes the evidence available; invariance is useful only for transformations that preserve the target label. Learned visual features can support kNN by making their distance reflect task-relevant similarity more closely.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Lecture example</th><th>What to retain</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>MNIST</td><td>28×28 grayscale handwritten digits; ten classes. The slides use a 50k training convention and 10k test set, so keep that convention separate from other dataset splits.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CIFAR-10 / CIFAR-100</td><td>32×32 RGB; 10 versus 100 classes; both slides describe 50k training and 10k test examples. A flattened image has 3072 values.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ImageNet lecture setting</td><td>1000 categories, variable image sizes, a validation split, and top-5 evaluation. These are the lecture's challenge setting, not every version of ImageNet.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Places / Omniglot</td><td>Scene categories / character categories used to discuss few-shot learning.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Top-1 asks whether the highest-ranked predicted class is correct. Top-5 asks whether the correct class is among the five highest-ranked choices. Detection additionally localizes objects; captioning predicts a structured text sequence. Classification can act as a building block, but the required outputs differ.</p>","worked":[{"question":"The true class is ranked fourth. What do top-1 and top-5 record?","steps":["Top-1 examines rank 1 only.","Top-5 examines ranks 1 through 5."],"answer":"Top-1 failure, top-5 success."},{"question":"How many coordinates are in one flattened CIFAR RGB image?","steps":["32×32 spatial sites.","Three channel values per site."],"answer":"32×32×3=3072."}],"traps":["A small pixel distance need not mean semantic similarity.","The target label decides which transformations are relevant invariances.","Top-5 success is not the same as identifying the correct highest-ranked label."],"check":["Distinguish intraclass variation from fine-grained classes.","Explain why background correlations can fail at deployment.","Describe why learned features can improve kNN without guaranteeing success."]},{"id":"iu03","title":"Nearest neighbors, boundary behavior, and honest model selection","topic":"Nearest neighbors","priority":"lecture","sources":["CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 32-70","CV_Lecture_2.pdf, PDF pp. 71-82"],"summary":"Compute distances and votes, explain complexity and dimensionality, and select k without contaminating the test set.","body":"<p>1-NN stores training examples and returns the label of the closest example. Unweighted kNN finds the k closest and takes a class vote. L1 creates distance contours aligned with coordinate axes; L2 uses Euclidean geometry. The chosen metric can change the neighbor ordering and prediction. Features with different scales can dominate either metric; any learned preprocessing statistics belong to the training/development procedure.</p>\n<pre>L1(a,b) = sum_j |a_j-b_j|\nL2(a,b) = sqrt(sum_j (a_j-b_j)^2)\nBrute-force distance search for one query: O(ND)\nStored numeric values: O(ND)\n</pre>\n<p>The slide's O(1) training shorthand assumes retaining a reference to already-loaded arrays. Copying/loading N vectors of D features costs O(ND); no optimization of classifier weights is needed. Prediction is summarized as O(N) when D is treated as fixed. kNN boundaries may have small irregular regions caused by isolated examples. Larger k often smooths the boundary and reduces outlier sensitivity, but can wash out real small regions. An odd k does not eliminate all multiclass ties.</p>\n<p>k and distance metric are hyperparameters. Use training data to fit/store examples and validation data to choose them. Cross-validation rotates a held-out development fold and averages results; keep the final test data outside that choice. Querying training examples against a set that includes themselves is resubstitution: distinct points retrieve their own labels at zero distance, so it cannot establish generalization.</p>\n<p>Uniform coverage becomes expensive as dimension grows: with m locations along each of D independent coordinates, a grid requires m^D points. Lecture 2 uses this curse of dimensionality to explain why a formal approximation claim with infinitely many examples does not make raw-pixel kNN practical. Learned features can supply a more useful lower-complexity notion of similarity.</p>","worked":[{"question":"For q=(0,0), compare A=(3,0) and B=(2,2).","steps":["L1(q,A)=3; L1(q,B)=4.","L2(q,A)=3; L2(q,B)=√8≈2.828."],"answer":"L1 picks A; L2 picks B."},{"question":"Nearest labels are A,B,B,A,B for k=5.","steps":["Count two A votes and three B votes.","Choose the class with most votes."],"answer":"B."},{"question":"How should you compare k=1,3,5 fairly?","steps":["Fit/store using training data.","Evaluate candidate k values on validation or development cross-validation.","Freeze the choice; evaluate final performance on untouched test data."],"answer":"Select with validation; reserve test for the final estimate."}],"traps":["Squared L2 has the same ranking as L2 but a different numeric value.","A tie-breaking policy must cover distance ties and class-vote ties.","Repeated test-driven selection can produce optimistic accuracy.","Do not interpret the slide's O(1) statement as zero data-loading or storage cost."],"check":["Compute L1/L2 and a multiclass vote by hand.","Explain why higher k can increase bias.","Explain the separate roles of train, validation, and test."]},{"id":"iu04","title":"Homogeneous primitives and incidence","topic":"Geometry","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.1, printed pp. 36-39 (PDF pp. 62-65)"],"summary":"Use projective coordinates to unify finite points, lines, parallel intersections, and 3D planes.","body":"<p>A homogeneous point is an equivalence class of nonzero vectors: multiplying all coordinates by a nonzero scalar changes the representation, not the point. A finite 2D point (u,v,w) becomes (u/w,v/w); w=0 represents a direction at infinity. The all-zero vector is excluded.</p>\n<pre>2D line l=(a,b,c): a x+b y+c=0, or l^T (x,y,1)=0.\nLine through p,q: l=p×q.\nIntersection of lines l1,l2: p=l1×l2.\n3D plane m=(a,b,c,d): aX+bY+cZ+d=0.\n3D line through p,q: r=(1-lambda)p+lambda q.\n</pre>\n<p>Normalizing a line by sqrt(a²+b²) gives a unit normal; its constant is an oriented quantity, and the unsigned origin distance is |c|/sqrt(a²+b²). Parallel lines have an ideal homogeneous intersection. In 3D, a line is conveniently represented by two points or a point and a direction, although an infinite 3D line has four geometric degrees of freedom and the unrestricted two-endpoint form is redundant. Restricting lambda to [0,1] gives the segment.</p>\n<p>Conics and 3D quadrics have quadratic homogeneous equations pᵀQp=0 and provide compact curve/surface representations. This chapter introduces them as primitives; it does not require deriving their full multi-view calibration theory. Incidence constraints are the useful common thread: a point is on a line/plane when the appropriate inner product vanishes.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Normalize (12,−6,3).","steps":["Divide all finite Cartesian coordinates by w=3."],"answer":"(4,−2)."},{"question":"Where do x=1 and x=3 meet projectively?","steps":["Use l1=(1,0,−1), l2=(1,0,−3).","Their cross product is (0,2,0), proportional to (0,1,0)."],"answer":"The vertical ideal point; no finite Cartesian intersection."}],"traps":["Homogeneous coordinates are not normalized by the sum of their entries.","A zero last coordinate cannot be converted by dividing.","Avoid treating an oriented line constant as a universally positive distance."],"check":["Test point-line incidence with an inner product.","Explain why two scaled homogeneous vectors can be equivalent.","Identify the geometric meaning of a point at infinity."]},{"id":"iu05","title":"Transformation hierarchy and 3D rotation choices","topic":"Transforms","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.1.1-2.1.3, printed pp. 40-51 (PDF pp. 66-77)"],"summary":"Track what each transformation preserves, apply composition in the correct order, and understand constrained rotation representations.","body":"<table><thead><tr><th>2D transform</th><th>Form</th><th>DoF</th><th>Guaranteed geometric preservation</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Translation</td><td>x′=x+t</td><td>2</td><td>Orientation, lengths, angles, parallel lines, straight lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rigid/Euclidean</td><td>x′=Rx+t</td><td>3</td><td>Lengths and angles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Similarity</td><td>x′=sRx+t</td><td>4</td><td>Angles and common relative scale</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Affine</td><td>x′=Ax+t</td><td>6</td><td>Parallelism and straight lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Projective</td><td>p′∝Hp</td><td>8</td><td>Straight lines; parallelism need not remain</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For 2D Cartesian x-right/y-up coordinates, R(theta)=[[cosθ,−sinθ],[sinθ,cosθ]]. In an image whose y axis points down, the visible rotation direction differs; always state axes. A 3×3 homogeneous representation makes translation part of matrix multiplication. Applying A then B means BA for column vectors. For nonsingular transforms, invert that matrix to undo the mapping.</p>\n<p>In 3D the corresponding degrees of freedom are 3,6,7,12,15. A proper rotation obeys RᵀR=I and det(R)=+1. Nine matrix entries therefore do not mean nine independent orientation parameters. 2D lines and 3D planes are covectors: for p′=Hp, their equation coefficients transform by H⁻ᵀ so that incidence is preserved. A 3D line has a different representation.</p>\n<p>Euler angles are order-dependent and can have singularities. Axis-angle uses a unit axis n and angle θ, or the minimal vector omega=θn. Rodrigues' formula builds a matrix; for a small radians vector, Rv≈v+omega×v. Unit quaternions q=(v,w)=(n sin(θ/2),cos(θ/2)) have four entries with a unit-length constraint. q and −q represent the same rotation. They are convenient for smooth tracking and spherical interpolation; compose them in the same order as their corresponding matrices.</p>\n<pre>R = I + sin(theta)[n]_cross + (1-cos(theta))[n]_cross²\nQuaternion order used here: (qx,qy,qz,qw).\nRightmost matrix acts first.</pre>","worked":[{"question":"Rotate (2,1) +90° counterclockwise, then translate (3,−2).","steps":["R(2,1)=(−1,2).","Add translation:(2,0)."],"answer":"(2,0) with the stated x-right/y-up axes."},{"question":"Quaternion for +90° about z?","steps":["Half-angle=45°.","Vector part=(0,0,sin45°); scalar part=cos45°."],"answer":"(0,0,√2/2,√2/2), equivalent to its negative."}],"traps":["An affine shear preserves parallelism but can change angles.","Do not omit perspective division after a homography.","3D rotation order is generally noncommutative.","Quaternion normalization and component ordering must be explicit."],"check":["Give the DoF and preserved properties for each 2D class.","Apply two transforms in sequence.","Compare matrix, Euler, axis-angle, and quaternion representations."]},{"id":"iu06","title":"Projection, calibration, and lens distortion","topic":"Projection","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.1.4-2.1.5, printed pp. 51-66 (PDF pp. 77-92)"],"summary":"Separate geometry from calibration and identify where a homography is sufficient and where depth or nonlinear lens correction is needed.","body":"<p>Orthography drops depth: (X,Y,Z) becomes (X,Y). Scaled orthography adds a common scale, useful when an object's depth variation is small relative to its distance. Para-perspective is an affine approximation that improves the off-axis model while retaining linear projection. Perspective divides by depth and models foreshortening.</p>\n<pre>Virtual plane in front of the pinhole, positive Z:\nu = fx X/Z + skew Y/Z + cx\nv = fy Y/Z + cy\nK = [[fx,skew,cx],[0,fy,cy],[0,0,1]]\nP = K [R | t], with camera coordinates = R world_point + t.\nHorizontal field of view: FOV = 2 atan(W/(2f)).\n</pre>\n<p>Use matching units for image width W and f: pixels for a pixel calibration matrix, millimetres for a physical sensor. Doubling image resolution with the same field of view doubles focal length in pixels. A single projected point supplies a ray, not a unique distance. Inverse depth is often a convenient parameter; a full 4×4 representation can retain depth/disparity, and a chosen reference plane can provide a plane-plus-parallax coordinate.</p>\n<p>A plane induces a homography between views. Pure camera rotation about one projection center also induces a homography without scene depth. General camera translation across a nonplanar scene introduces depth-dependent parallax and cannot be represented by one global homography. Object-centered projection highlights another ambiguity: for a distant shallow object, changing focal length and camera distance can yield similar image scale.</p>\n<p>Lens distortion is separate from ideal perspective. In a common normalized-coordinate model, r²=x²+y², (x̂,ŷ)=(x,y)(1+k1r²+k2r⁴), followed by focal scaling and principal-point offset. The mapping direction and coordinates matter because other implementations parameterize the inverse. Wide-angle distortion can bend straight lines; fisheye models such as r=fθ use a different angular projection.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Project X=2,Z=8 with f=800px,cx=320px.","steps":["X/Z=0.25.","u=800×0.25+320."],"answer":"u=520px."},{"question":"A 1000px image has a 90° horizontal field of view.","steps":["f=1000/(2tan45°)."],"answer":"f=500px."},{"question":"Distort normalized x=0.5,y=0 with k1=0.2,k2=0.","steps":["r²=0.25.","x̂=0.5(1+0.2×0.25)."],"answer":"x̂=0.525."}],"traps":["The image plane may be drawn behind or in front of the pinhole; signs must match the convention.","Intrinsics do not replace extrinsics.","A homography does not generally align translated views with multiple depths.","Lens-distortion coefficients depend on coordinate normalization and mapping direction."],"check":["Compute a pinhole projection and a field-of-view conversion.","Explain depth ambiguity along a viewing ray.","Recognize when orthography or a homography is a justified approximation."]},{"id":"iu07","title":"Lighting, reflectance, and why intensity is ambiguous","topic":"Photometry","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.2.1-2.2.2, printed pp. 66-74 (PDF pp. 92-100)"],"summary":"Use illumination and material models to explain brightness changes rather than equating pixel values with surface color.","body":"<p>Point illumination decreases with squared source distance; area sources require integrating a distribution of positions/directions and can produce soft shadows. An environment map represents illumination by incoming direction, commonly assuming distant sources. These models determine what light arrives at a surface, not yet what the camera sees.</p>\n<p>A BRDF describes the reflected radiance associated with incoming and outgoing directions at a surface and wavelength. Its directional variables are four angles in the general local model. Isotropic materials have no preferred in-plane direction; anisotropic brushed materials do. Reflection also depends on the cosine foreshortening factor of incoming illumination and visibility. Reciprocity interchanges the incident and reflected roles under the stated physical assumptions.</p>\n<pre>Normalized Lambertian example:\nI = rho L max(0,n dot l), with n,l unit vectors.\nDiffuse term is view-independent in the ideal model.\nPhong: ambient + diffuse + specular.\nSpecular lobe approximately ks[max(0,view dot mirror_direction)]^p.\n</pre>\n<p>A Lambertian patch changes brightness with light direction and surface normal even if its material is unchanged. Ideal outgoing radiance is independent of viewing direction. A specular term peaks when the viewer approaches the mirror-reflection direction; increasing the Phong exponent narrows the highlight. The simple ambient term approximates illumination that the direct-source model leaves out.</p>\n<p>The dichromatic model separates interface and body-reflection spectra with geometry-dependent magnitudes. Global illumination adds occlusion, indirect bounces, and color exchange among surfaces: ray/path tracing follows light paths, while radiosity exchanges energy among surface patches. These phenomena explain why the same local material can look different in different scenes.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Compute I for rho=0.6,L=2,angle=60°.","steps":["max(0,cos60°)=0.5.","I=0.6×2×0.5."],"answer":"I=0.6 under the explicitly normalized model."},{"question":"Move a point source from distance r to 2r with angle fixed.","steps":["Compare1/(2r)² with1/r²."],"answer":"Illumination becomes one quarter."}],"traps":["Specify whether albedo/radiance conventions include a factor of π.","Clamp the direct diffuse contribution to zero when the light is behind the surface.","Diffuse and specular responses have different view dependence.","A dark pixel does not uniquely identify dark material."],"check":["Explain the roles of light, normal, material, and view.","Distinguish hard versus soft source shadows.","Explain what a constant ambient term fails to model."]},{"id":"iu08","title":"Optics: focus, exposure, and peripheral effects","topic":"Optics","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.2.3, printed pp. 74-79 (PDF pp. 100-105)"],"summary":"Solve the thin-lens relation and connect aperture diameter to exposure, geometric defocus, and vignetting.","body":"<pre>Thin lens: 1/zo + 1/zi = 1/f.\nf-number N=f/d, so aperture diameter d=f/N.\nApproximate irradiance for fixed scene: proportional to 1/N².\nNatural off-axis falloff: proportional to cos(alpha)^4.\n</pre>\n<p>For a real object beyond the focal distance (zo&gt;f), a finite object distance focuses behind the lens at zi greater than f; an object at infinity focuses at zi=f. An object inside the focal distance forms a virtual image instead. An incorrectly placed sensor plane creates a circle of confusion rather than a sharp point. Depth of field is the scene-depth range whose blur meets a chosen acceptable limit; it depends on aperture, focal length, focus distance, and that criterion.</p>\n<p>A smaller aperture diameter (larger f-number) usually decreases geometric defocus and increases depth of field, while collecting less light. One full stop changes aperture area by a factor of two: neighboring f-numbers are separated by roughly √2. A move from f/4 to f/8 is two stops, leaving one quarter of the irradiance with shutter time fixed. Longer exposure may compensate light loss but changes motion blur.</p>\n<p>Real compound lenses can suffer geometric aberrations. Chromatic aberration arises because refraction depends on wavelength: transverse effects change color magnification, and longitudinal effects change color focus. Vignetting reduces peripheral brightness; natural vignetting has a cos⁴ off-axis relation in the simplified model, while mechanical vignetting comes from occlusion by lens components.</p>","worked":[{"question":"f=50 mm and zo=1000 mm. Find zi.","steps":["1/zi=1/50−1/1000=19/1000.","Invert the reciprocal."],"answer":"zi≈52.631579 mm."},{"question":"A 50 mm lens at f/4 versus f/8.","steps":["Diameter at f/4=12.5 mm; at f/8=6.25 mm.","Area ratio=(6.25/12.5)²=1/4."],"answer":"One quarter of irradiance at f/8, with other settings fixed."}],"traps":["Use the same length units throughout the lens equation.","A higher f-number means a smaller aperture.","Exposure and focus are distinct effects.","Depth-of-field comparisons need fixed focus distance and a blur criterion."],"check":["Solve for image distance from focal length and object distance.","Compute an aperture diameter and an exposure ratio.","Identify vignetting versus chromatic aberration."]},{"id":"iu09","title":"Digital sensing, noise, sampling, and aliasing","topic":"Digital imaging","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §2.3 and §2.3.1, printed pp. 79-87 (PDF pp. 105-113)"],"summary":"Track the sensing pipeline and explain why resolution, noise, and aliasing are different constraints.","body":"<p>Light is integrated during exposure, converted to an electronic signal, amplified, and digitized. A camera then typically demosaics, denoises/sharpens, white-balances, maps tone/gamma, and compresses. CCDs transfer charge toward an output; CMOS sensors provide local pixel readout circuitry. The important reasoning is that the display-ready pixel value can differ substantially from a linear measurement of incoming light.</p>\n<p>Exposure time changes collected photons and motion blur. Pixel pitch determines spatial sampling density; sensing area and fill factor affect light collection. Gain/ISO rescales the signal and its noise; it does not by itself gather more photons. Noise may include fixed-pattern, dark-current, photon shot, amplifier, and quantization components. Photon-limited counts can have a Poisson model whose variance depends on intensity, while a constant-variance Gaussian approximation suits other regimes. Nominal ADC bit depth is not the same as the number of reliably useful bits.</p>\n<pre>Sampling frequency fs -> Nyquist limit fs/2.\nTo preserve a band-limited signal, sampling must exceed twice its highest frequency\nunder the relevant assumptions; avoid phase-degenerate equality edge cases.\nDecimation by r -> new Nyquist limit = old fs/(2r).\nPSF: spatial point response; OTF: Fourier transform(PSF); MTF=|OTF|.\n</pre>\n<p>Aliasing occurs when different continuous signals generate indistinguishable samples, often showing false stripes or moiré. Averaging over a sensor cell attenuates high frequencies but does not necessarily remove them all, even at a high fill factor. The overall PSF combines lens blur, sensor integration, and any optical antialiasing filter; the MTF shows how frequencies are transmitted. Downsampling after acquisition can create a new aliasing problem unless the image is filtered for the new grid first.</p>","worked":[{"question":"fs=100 samples/unit. What is the frequency limit?","steps":["Nyquist limit=fs/2."],"answer":"50 cycles/unit."},{"question":"Decimate an input sampled at one sample/unit by 4.","steps":["New fs=1/4.","New limit=(1/4)/2."],"answer":"0.125 cycles per original input unit."}],"traps":["Photon shot noise is not the same phenomenon as random salt-and-pepper impulse corruption.","More ADC bits do not guarantee less sensor noise.","Increasing gain is not equivalent to increasing photon collection.","Filtering after aliasing cannot generally reveal which original frequency produced the samples."],"check":["Describe where gain, ADC, and digital processing occur.","Explain a resolution/noise tradeoff.","Relate spatial blur to a frequency response and an antialiasing requirement."]},{"id":"iu10","title":"Color, gamma, and compression without mixing conventions","topic":"Color and compression","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 2, §§2.3.2-2.3.3, printed pp. 87-101 (PDF pp. 113-127)"],"summary":"Treat color as channel responses to spectra, decode nonlinear encodings for physical arithmetic, and understand where compression loses information.","body":"<p>A camera channel integrates the spectrum weighted by its spectral sensitivity; it does not sample exactly one red, green, or blue wavelength. Three responses cannot uniquely specify a full spectrum. Different spectra with the same response are metamers under that condition; they may separate under another illuminant or sensor. RGB additive mixtures differ from subtractive pigment mixing.</p>\n<p>XYZ provides a colorimetric tristimulus representation; Y relates to luminance. Dividing X,Y,Z by their sum gives chromaticity x,y,z, which sum to one and discard overall intensity. Lab applies a nonlinear reference-white-dependent mapping aimed at greater perceptual uniformity. HSV separates a hue/saturation/value description useful for manipulation but is not a physically linear light space. Channel ratios also separate relative color from common intensity scaling.</p>\n<p>A Bayer CFA senses two green samples, one red, and one blue per typical 2×2 block. Demosaicing estimates missing channel values at every pixel. White balance can be modeled as a diagonal channel-gain transform, with more general color calibration using a 3×3 matrix.</p>\n<pre>Stated gamma convention: encoded e = I^(1/gamma).\nDecode linear light: I = e^gamma.\nArithmetic for radiance/exposure blending should use linear light.\nChromaticity x=X/(X+Y+Z), y=Y/(X+Y+Z).\nPSNR = 10 log10(peak²/MSE), with matching image range and peak.\n</pre>\n<p>Conventional JPEG commonly separates luma from chroma, may subsample chroma, transforms blocks with the DCT, quantizes coefficients, then entropy-codes them. Quantization loses precision; independent block coding can create block boundaries and ringing. The constant DCT basis gives DC, related to mean block brightness; other bases give AC detail. Lower numeric error (higher PSNR) need not imply the most pleasing perceptual result.</p>\n<p><strong>Source convention caution:</strong> the supplied draft's gamma wording around Eq. 3.7 conflicts with its clear Chapter 2 encoding model. All examples here state the function and invert it algebraically. The displayed Lab lightness equation also appears to omit its offset; these notes teach the role of Lab without reproducing that defective formula.</p>","worked":[{"question":"XYZ=(2,3,5): find chromaticity.","steps":["Sum=10.","Divide each coordinate by 10."],"answer":"(x,y,z)=(0.2,0.3,0.5)."},{"question":"Encode as e=√I, observe e=0.5.","steps":["Invert: I=e²."],"answer":"I=0.25."},{"question":"Peak=1, MSE=0.01.","steps":["Compute 10 log10(1/0.01)."],"answer":"PSNR=20 dB."}],"traps":["Encoded RGB is not automatically linear radiance.","Equal channel offsets generally change color ratios.","Chromaticity is undefined for zero total signal.","Use peak 1 for normalized images and peak 255 for 8-bit images.","PSNR measures squared-error fidelity, not every perceptual artifact."],"check":["Explain metamerism and demosaicing.","Decode and re-encode a stated gamma function.","Identify quantization as a lossy step and explain blocking."]},{"id":"iu11","title":"Point operations, alpha compositing, and tone distributions","topic":"Point operators","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.1, printed pp. 109-119 (PDF pp. 135-145)"],"summary":"Separate linear gain from affine offset/clipping, use alpha correctly, and compute CDF-based tone mappings with an explicit convention.","body":"<p>A point operation changes a pixel using the corresponding input pixel, possibly with globally estimated parameters. g=af+b uses gain and bias. Multiplication by fixed a is linear; nonzero b makes the mapping affine; clipping makes the full bounded operation nonlinear. A shared RGB scale preserves normalized channel ratios before clipping, whereas equal offsets or separate gains can change perceived color.</p>\n<pre>Straight foreground F over opaque B: C=alpha F+(1-alpha)B.\nPremultiplied foreground P=alpha F: C=P+(1-alpha)B.\nHistogram h(i)=number of pixels at level i.\nCDF(i)=sum_(j<=i) h(j)/N.\nExample equalization convention: s(i)=(L-1)CDF(i), before rounding.\n</pre>\n<p>Matting estimates foreground color, background color, and alpha from observations; compositing uses those quantities to make a new image. Alpha is coverage/opacity: one is fully foreground and zero is fully background. Fractional alpha supports fine boundaries. Premultiplied storage makes filtering and resampling RGBA convenient, but its color must not be multiplied by alpha twice. Physical light mixtures should be computed in linear light.</p>\n<p>A histogram discards spatial arrangement but describes intensity occupancy. Equalization remaps by a CDF to spread values; discrete finite images need not become exactly flat. Implementation conventions differ in whether they subtract the first nonzero CDF and how they round. Global equalization can overemphasize noise or produce an unwanted tone distribution, so partial mappings blend with the identity. Independent local blocks can make seams; adaptive histogram equalization interpolates local transfer functions, and contrast limits reduce excessive amplification.</p>","worked":[{"question":"f=180,g=clip(1.5f+20,0,255).","steps":["Unclipped value=290.","Clamp to8-bit maximum."],"answer":"255."},{"question":"Straight F=0.8,B=0.2,alpha=0.25.","steps":["Foreground contribution=0.20.","Background contribution=0.15."],"answer":"C=0.35."},{"question":"Histogram[1,2,1,0], L=4; find s(1) under s=3CDF.","steps":["N=4; CDF(1)=3/4.","s(1)=3×3/4."],"answer":"2.25 before rounding; no CDFmin subtraction is used."}],"traps":["A histogram contains no object position information.","Premultiplied and straight color values are different representations.","Do not assume every library uses the same equalization convention.","An operation can be pointwise while its parameters were estimated globally."],"check":["Compute gain/bias with and without clipping.","Explain matting versus compositing.","Compute a CDF and describe a local-equalization seam."]},{"id":"iu12","title":"Linear, separable, directional, and recursive filtering","topic":"Filtering","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.2, printed pp. 119-131 (PDF pp. 145-157)"],"summary":"Read a kernel with explicit offsets, reason about frequency and border effects, and recognize efficient implementations.","body":"<pre>Correlation: g(i,j)=sum_(k,l) f(i+k,j+l) h(k,l).\nConvolution: g(i,j)=sum_(k,l) f(i-k,j-l) h(k,l).\nSeparable kernel: K=v h^T; K×K direct work≈K², two passes≈2K.\nUnsharp mask: sharpened=f+lambda(f-blurred).\nDirectional derivative: G_u=cos(theta)G_x+sin(theta)G_y.\n</pre>\n<p>Convolution reverses the kernel relative to correlation; symmetric kernels produce the same result, but an antisymmetric derivative changes sign. Fixed neighborhood filtering is linear, and it is shift invariant on an unbounded grid or where boundary rules do not interfere. Kernels summing to one preserve a constant signal under suitable boundaries; derivative/band-pass kernels have zero response to a constant.</p>\n<p>Zero padding can darken borders, replicate padding repeats edge values, wrapping assumes periodicity, and reflection depends on the exact endpoint convention. State the border rule before calculating. A separable rank-one kernel is an outer product: Gaussian approximations, box kernels, and Sobel constructions often exploit this. A nonseparable kernel may be approximated by a sum of separable SVD terms.</p>\n<p>Smoothing suppresses high frequencies and can reduce noise, but also softens edges. Unsharp masking adds a residual and may amplify noise or produce halos. Sobel x combines differentiation across x with smoothing across y and therefore responds to vertical edges. Gaussian derivative families provide smoother band-pass analysis; LoG combines smoothing with the Laplacian, and steerable bases make many directions cheap to evaluate.</p>\n<p>An integral image stores running sums so that a rectangle sum needs four lookups with inclusion-exclusion. Accumulator range must be large enough for many pixels. FIR filters use a finite input footprint. Recursive/IIR filters use previous outputs and can have an indefinitely extended impulse response. These are implementation/response distinctions, not a guarantee that a filter is linear or nonlinear in every design.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Neighborhood[2,4,8], kernel[−1,0,1].","steps":["Correlation=(-1)2+0(4)+1(8)=6.","Convolution=(-1)8+0(4)+1(2)=−6."],"answer":"Correlation6; convolution−6 under centered offsets."},{"question":"Rectangle integral lookups40,12,9,3.","steps":["40−12−9+3."],"answer":"22."},{"question":"7×7 separable filtering cost.","steps":["Two7-tap passes use14 terms instead of49."],"answer":"14 terms per output pixel, ignoring overhead."}],"traps":["Kernel symmetry can hide a correlation/convolution mistake.","Derivative direction and edge orientation are perpendicular.","Borders can violate a casual claim of perfect shift invariance.","Clipping after a linear filter changes the full operator."],"check":["Compute correlation and convolution with an asymmetric mask.","Explain separability and rank one.","Compute an integral-image rectangle and an unsharp result."]},{"id":"iu13","title":"Nonlinear denoising, morphology, and regions","topic":"Nonlinear and binary processing","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.3, printed pp. 131-142 (PDF pp. 157-168)"],"summary":"Choose an operator by its assumptions about outliers, edges, binary shape, and adjacency.","body":"<p>A median chooses the middle neighborhood value and is useful against isolated impulse outliers. A mean averages all samples and can be better for independent small zero-mean noise when edges are absent. A trimmed mean removes extreme values before averaging. Weighted absolute-deviation minimization gives a weighted median; squared-deviation minimization gives a weighted mean. These assumptions explain the different behavior, rather than one filter being universally best.</p>\n<pre>Bilateral weight(i,j)=spatial_weight(i,j)×range_weight(i,j).\nspatial_weight=exp(−distance²/(2 sigma_d²)).\nrange_weight=exp(−intensity_difference²/(2 sigma_r²)).\nOutput=sum(weights×input_values)/sum(weights).\n</pre>\n<p>Large intensity differences reduce bilateral mixing across edges; the range weights depend on the image, making the usual filter nonlinear. A very large sigma_r approaches ordinary spatial Gaussian smoothing. Joint/cross-bilateral filtering uses another image for the range weights while averaging the input values. Guided filtering models a local affine relationship to a guide. Image alignment and guide relevance matter. Repeating edge-dependent local averaging relates to anisotropic diffusion, but excessive iteration can destroy detail; the process needs an appropriate stopping rule or data fidelity.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Binary operation (foreground=1)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Dilation</td><td>Output1 if any foreground appears under the structuring element; grows foreground.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Erosion</td><td>Output1 if all required samples are foreground; shrinks foreground.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Opening</td><td>Erosion then dilation; removes structures too small to survive the element.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Closing</td><td>Dilation then erosion; fills sufficiently small gaps/holes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Majority</td><td>Threshold the foreground count near half the element size; define an even-size tie policy if necessary.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A distance transform gives the distance to the nearest specified background/target set. State whether zeros or ones form the target and which metric is used. Manhattan distance sums absolute coordinate differences; Euclidean uses their squared sum and root. Signed distances add an inside/outside sign convention. Connected components depend on N4 versus N8 adjacency; compute region area, centroid, and moments after labeling. A diagonal contact can join components under N8 and leave them separate under N4.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Median of[10,11,250,12,9].","steps":["Sort[9,10,11,12,250]."],"answer":"11."},{"question":"Values10,20 with bilateral weights1,0.5.","steps":["Weighted sum=20; normalization=1.5."],"answer":"13.333333."},{"question":"Nearest zero is at(0,2), query(3,4), Manhattan metric.","steps":["3+2."],"answer":"5."},{"question":"Pixels(0,0),(1,1) only.","steps":["No horizontal/vertical path exists.","A diagonal connection exists."],"answer":"Two N4 components; one N8 component."}],"traps":["Photon-count shot noise and impulse corruption are different models despite loose terminology in the draft.","Normalized bilateral weights must sum to one after division.","Foreground convention and the structuring-element shape change morphology results.","Distance to background is not distance to the origin unless the origin is the nearest target."],"check":["Explain why median/bilateral filters are not ordinary fixed linear kernels.","Apply erosion, dilation, opening, and closing in order.","Specify adjacency and compute a simple centroid or distance."]},{"id":"iu14","title":"Fourier reasoning for filters and restoration","topic":"Fourier","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.4, printed pp. 142-149 (PDF pp. 168-175)"],"summary":"Use frequency response to explain blur, sharpening, transform computation, and what lost information cannot be restored.","body":"<p>An LSI filter maps a sinusoid into a sinusoid of the same frequency with a changed amplitude and phase. The Fourier transform tabulates this response. Low frequencies describe slow variations; high frequencies describe rapid spatial changes. The complex phase matters for where structures occur, so magnitude alone is generally insufficient to reconstruct an image.</p>\n<pre>Forward convention used in the textbook:\nF(k)=(1/N) sum_n f(n) exp(−j2pi kn/N).\nThen DC F(0)=mean(f).\nMany libraries instead use an unnormalized forward transform and a 1/N inverse.\nDFT direct: O(N²); FFT: O(N log N), the same mathematical transform.\n</pre>\n<p>Convolution corresponds to multiplication in frequency, with the scale factor depending on normalization. Under an unnormalized forward DFT and normalized inverse, circular convolution gives G=FH. A finite FFT assumes periodic extension; pad to at least N+M−1 for the full linear convolution of 1D arrays of lengths N and M. For 2D images the idea applies along both axes.</p>\n<p>The DCT uses real cosine bases and is useful for blocks because natural patches often concentrate energy in low-frequency coefficients. Its DC component relates to average brightness. Filtering can be studied in either spatial or frequency terms: smoothing attenuates rapid variation; derivative filters suppress constant values and emphasize certain variation; a band-pass filter suppresses both very low and very high frequencies.</p>\n<p>Restoration must consider signal and noise together. Dividing by a tiny blur response amplifies noise; no stable division exists where the response is zero. The reading introduces the statistical idea of Wiener restoration but removes its derivation in this edition; it is background here, not an added derivation requirement. PSNR compares output with a known clean reference using MSE; perceptual metrics assess other aspects of similarity. Apparent sharpening does not prove the original physical detail was recovered.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Normalized DFT DC for[2,4,6,8].","steps":["At k=0 the exponential is1.","Divide sum20 byN=4."],"answer":"5."},{"question":"What FFT padding is sufficient for lengths4 and3?","steps":["Full linear support length=4+3−1."],"answer":"At least6 samples."},{"question":"Unsharp pixel100, blur80, gain0.5.","steps":["Residual20.","100+0.5×20."],"answer":"110 before clipping."}],"traps":["State transform normalization before a numeric DFT calculation.","An FFT is an algorithm, not a different representation from the DFT.","DFT multiplication is circular until padding is handled.","Amplitude boost can amplify noise and cannot uniquely recover erased information."],"check":["Compute DC under a stated normalization.","Explain why sinusoid frequency remains fixed through an LSI filter.","Use Fourier reasoning to explain sharpening and unstable deblurring."]},{"id":"iu15","title":"Interpolation, pyramids, wavelets, and multiband blending","topic":"Pyramids and wavelets","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.5, printed pp. 149-167 (PDF pp. 175-193)"],"summary":"Keep sampling and reconstruction operators explicit and use scale-dependent representations for analysis and blending.","body":"<p>Interpolation estimates values between available samples; it increases output grid density without creating new measured information. Linear interpolation is simple but can soften or create derivative creases. Cubic and windowed-sinc choices trade sharpness, smoothness, cost, overshoot, and ringing. An interpolating kernel passes through source samples; an approximating B-spline reconstruction need not. Decimation first filters for the smaller Nyquist range and then reduces the sample rate.</p>\n<pre>Gaussian octave pyramid: G_(l+1)=downsample2(lowpass(G_l)).\nLaplacian residual: L_l=G_l−expand(G_(l+1)).\nReconstruction: G_l=L_l+expand(G_(l+1)).\nKeep the coarsest G and every residual L for exact reconstruction.\nEach octave has1/4 as many pixels as its parent.\n</pre>\n<p>A Gaussian pyramid supports multi-scale detection and coarse-to-fine search. A Laplacian pyramid adds residual bands so the exact fine image can be recovered before quantization when the same expansion operator is used. Its name does not make each residual exactly a mathematical LoG: a difference of smoothed scales produces a DoG-like band-pass effect. Half-octave/quincunx representations provide intermediate scales with different grids.</p>\n<p>Wavelets localize structure in space, scale, and often orientation. A critically sampled separable 2D stage produces LL,LH,HL,HH, each with one quarter of the pixels. Recurse on LL and retain detail bands; the total coefficient count can equal the source count, whereas regular pyramids are overcomplete. Some wavelet/steerable families deliberately use redundancy for better shift/orientation behavior. Specify axis naming before attaching horizontal/vertical edge names to LH and HL.</p>\n<p>Lifting splits even and odd samples, predicts one sequence from the other and stores the residual, then updates the coarse sequence. Reverse the operations and signs to reconstruct; the predictor need not be perfect. Multiband blending builds Laplacian pyramids of the images and a Gaussian pyramid of the mask. Coarse color variations blend broadly, while fine texture blends locally; reconstruct the weighted bands afterward.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Linear interpolation of 10 and 30 at t=0.25.","steps":["0.75×10+0.25×30."],"answer":"15."},{"question":"256×256 input, octave level 2.","steps":["Level 1=128×128.","Level 2=64×64."],"answer":"4096 pixels."},{"question":"G=50 and expanded coarse estimate 44.","steps":["Residual=50−44.","Reconstruct with 6+44."],"answer":"Residual 6; exact original 50."},{"question":"Infinite octave pyramid sample count.","steps":["N(1+1/4+1/16+…).","Geometric sum=N/(1−1/4)."],"answer":"4N/3, ignoring integer rounding and finite termination."}],"traps":["Upsampling does not add new measured scene information.","An antialiasing filter must match the decimation factor.","DoG and LoG are related but not identical at arbitrary scales.","Drop the coarsest Gaussian level and exact Laplacian reconstruction is lost.","Some wavelet families are redundant, so do not universalize the critical-sampling count."],"check":["Compute pyramid dimensions and a residual/reconstruction step.","Explain the four wavelet subbands and lifting reversibility.","Explain why mask smoothing varies with scale during blending."]},{"id":"iu16","title":"Warping, antialiasing footprints, and morphing","topic":"Warping","priority":"reading","sources":["Szeliski, 2nd-edition draft, Ch. 3, §3.6, printed pp. 168-178 (PDF pp. 194-204)"],"summary":"Map destination coordinates back to source samples, handle minification, and distinguish geometric alignment from appearance blending.","body":"<p>A point operator changes an image's range; a geometric warp changes its domain. If the forward coordinate map is x′=h(x), a destination-driven implementation samples g(x′)=f(h⁻¹(x′)). Forward mapping pushes source samples onto target locations; rounding can create overlaps, holes, and flicker. Splatting distributes samples but needs weighting/normalization and can blur. Inverse warping queries every destination pixel where its source map is defined, then handles noninteger coordinates by interpolation and out-of-range coordinates by a stated border rule.</p>\n<pre>Bilinear weights at relative(x,y)in[0,1]²:\nw00=(1-x)(1-y), w10=x(1-y), w01=(1-x)y, w11=xy.\nMIP octave level for source-footprint scale r: l=log2(r).\nFractional l can blend adjacent levels after within-level sampling.\n</pre>\n<p>Inverse warping solves sampling coverage, not every aliasing problem. One destination pixel can cover a large or elongated source footprint under minification. The local Jacobian gives an affine approximation to the transform and the directional sampling rates. MIP-maps use prefiltered resolutions for efficient scalar scale selection. Trilinear MIP sampling blends two bilinearly sampled levels. Elliptical/anisotropic filtering can better follow a footprint that compresses one axis more than another. Multi-pass 1D resampling/shearing can improve efficiency but still needs careful antialiasing.</p>\n<p>Global transformations have a few parameters; a face-expression edit often needs local deformation. A triangular mesh interpolates displacement by a per-triangle affine map from three non-collinear correspondences. Quadrilateral grids, smooth spline fields, sparse control points, or line correspondences provide other warp models. Folded or degenerate meshes can make the mapping invalid, so correspondence geometry matters.</p>\n<p>Cross-dissolving only mixes corresponding pixel intensities and can duplicate unaligned features. A feature-based morph first defines intermediate landmarks, warps both images into that common geometry, and then blends appearance. At time t the usual position interpolation and appearance weights are1−t and t. Geometry and color interpolation are separate calculations.</p>","worked":[{"question":"Forward x′=2x+3, destination 11.","steps":["Solve x=(11−3)/2."],"answer":"Source x=4."},{"question":"Corners 0,10,20,30 at (0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1); sample (0.25,0.5).","steps":["Weights=0.375,0.125,0.375,0.125.","Weighted sum=1.25+7.5+3.75."],"answer":"12.5."},{"question":"A destination footprint spans 8 source widths.","steps":["l=log2(8)."],"answer":"MIP level 3."},{"question":"Morph at t=0.5.","steps":["Interpolate common midpoint landmarks.","Warp both inputs to that geometry.","Mix aligned intensities equally."],"answer":"Aligned midpoint shape with a 50/50 appearance blend."}],"traps":["Use the inverse coordinate transform when pulling destination samples.","Interpolation and antialiasing are related but serve different needs under minification.","Bilinear corner order and axes must be stated.","One global homography cannot represent every local deformation.","Blending alone does not align features."],"check":["Compute an inverse mapping and bilinear sample.","Explain why an elongated footprint calls for directional filtering.","Describe the steps of a warp-plus-blend morph."]}],"exams":[{"id":"sample","title":"Original sample — Midterm Variant 1","time":120,"total":100,"description":"The supplied sample midterm: its question wording, numbers, code, tables, and marks are preserved. Worked answers and marking suggestions were added for preparation; they are not an official answer key.","instructions":"Instructions: This is an individual, handwritten, closed-book exam. Show calculations where required. Answers without explanation may receive partial credit. Phones, laptops, smart watches, notes, and AI tools are not permitted.","groups":[{"title":"Question 1 - Image Processing & OpenCV","points":20,"scenario":"A student is building a system to classify healthy and diseased plant leaves. The collected images have different resolutions, lighting conditions, and some contain image noise.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Propose three preprocessing operations that could be useful. For each operation, explain why you would use it.","answer":"Resize to a common input size, preferably preserving the aspect ratio with padding, so batches have consistent dimensions. Apply a suitable illumination or intensity adjustment when lighting differs, while preserving disease-related color. Use mild Gaussian or median denoising when noise is present, choosing the filter for the noise type. Evaluate the choices on validation data.","steps":["Identify the stated problems: resolution, lighting, and noise.","Map each proposed operation to one problem.","Check that preprocessing preserves lesion texture and useful color."],"rubric":["2 points per valid operation with a relevant reason: 1 for the operation and 1 for the explanation.","Accept alternatives such as crop/segmentation, normalization, or a justified contrast adjustment; no single trio is required."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"The student converts every image to grayscale. Give one advantage and one disadvantage of doing this for plant-disease classification.","answer":"Advantage: one channel reduces storage and computation and can reduce irrelevant color variation. Disadvantage: yellowing, discoloration, or colored lesions may distinguish disease classes; grayscale removes that color information.","steps":["Compare three color channels with one intensity channel.","Connect lost color information to this specific task."],"rubric":["2 points for a valid advantage with explanation.","2 points for a task-related disadvantage. Accept any equivalent reason; grayscale is not automatically beneficial."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Consider the following code. Identify two problems and provide the corrected code.","answer":"cv2.imread loads a color image in BGR order by default, so use COLOR_BGR2GRAY. A specified Gaussian kernel must have positive odd dimensions, so replace (4, 4) with (5, 5), for example. Corrected code:\nimg = cv2.imread(\"leaf.jpg\")\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)\nAn optional check `if img is None: raise FileNotFoundError(\"leaf.jpg\")` can be placed after imread.","steps":["Match the conversion constant to the actual BGR input order.","Use an odd kernel such as (3, 3) or (5, 5).","A file-load check is useful but is additional to the two intended corrections."],"rubric":["2 points for explaining BGR versus RGB and correcting the conversion.","2 points for identifying the invalid even kernel and replacing it with a positive odd kernel.","1 point for coherent corrected code. Accept another valid odd kernel; optional missing-file handling is not required."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"A student says: \"Applying more preprocessing always improves classification accuracy.\" Do you agree? Explain using one example.","answer":"No. Excessive smoothing can erase small lesions or vein texture needed to identify disease. A heavy blur may therefore reduce accuracy even though it removes noise. Preprocessing choices should be tested on validation data using the same evaluation protocol.","steps":["Reject the word \"always\".","Give an operation that can remove task-relevant information.","Explain the resulting classification error."],"rubric":["1 point for disagreeing with the universal claim.","3 points for a concrete operation and correct explanation of the harm.","1 point for connecting the decision to task evidence or validation. Accept color removal, distorted resizing, or other well-explained examples."]}],"code":"img = cv2.imread(\"leaf.jpg\")\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (4, 4), 0)"},{"title":"Question 2 - Image Classification","points":20,"scenario":"A classifier produces the following scores:","parts":[{"label":"a","points":4,"prompt":"Write the predicted class for A, B, C, and D.","answer":"A: Cat; B: Bird; C: Bird; D: Dog. Choose the class with the largest score in each row.","steps":["A: max(2.4, 0.7, -0.2) = 2.4, Cat.","B: max(0.3, 1.2, 1.8) = 1.8, Bird.","C: max(-0.5, 0.4, 2.1) = 2.1, Bird.","D: max(1.4, 1.6, 0.2) = 1.6, Dog."],"rubric":["1 point per correct predicted class."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Calculate classification accuracy. Show your calculation.","answer":"Accuracy = number correct / number evaluated = 3/4 = 0.75 = 75%. A, C, and D are correct.","steps":["Compare each predicted class with its true class.","Count 3 correct predictions out of 4.","Compute 3 ÷ 4 × 100 = 75%."],"rubric":["2 points for the correct count and denominator; 1 point for the formula; 1 point for 75% (or 0.75). Exact fraction 3/4 is accepted."]},{"label":"c","points":4,"prompt":"Which image was incorrectly classified?","answer":"Image B: its true class is Dog, but Bird has the highest score.","steps":["Compare B’s largest score, 1.8 for Bird, with its Dog label."],"rubric":["2 points for B and 2 for correctly identifying Bird versus Dog."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"Explain the difference between a class score and a class probability.","answer":"A class score is a raw model output used to compare classes; it can be negative or greater than 1 and need not sum to 1. A probability distribution has values in [0,1] that sum to 1. Softmax transforms scores into a probability distribution, but those values are not guaranteed to match real frequencies without calibration.","steps":["State that raw scores have no probability-range or normalization requirement.","State the range and sum requirements for mutually exclusive class probabilities.","A score can choose the predicted class without being a probability."],"rubric":["2 points for the raw-score explanation; 2 for the probability explanation.","Accept a correct softmax example. Calibration detail is useful but not required for full marks."]},{"label":"e","points":4,"prompt":"The model achieves 95% training accuracy but 62% test accuracy. What is the likely problem? Give one possible solution.","answer":"The gap suggests overfitting: the model fits training-specific patterns but generalizes poorly. One remedy is stronger regularization or appropriate training augmentation, selected using a validation set. Other possibilities, such as different train/test distributions, should be investigated if evidence supports them.","steps":["Compare training accuracy with accuracy on unseen examples.","Name a plausible cause of the generalization gap.","Connect a remedy to the cause."],"rubric":["2 points for overfitting or another well-justified cause, and 2 for a suitable remedy.","Accept more representative training data, early stopping on validation, weight decay, valid augmentation, or an appropriately simpler model. The gap alone does not prove overfitting."]}],"table":{"headers":["Image","Cat","Dog","Bird","True class"],"rows":[["A","2.4","0.7","-0.2","Cat"],["B","0.3","1.2","1.8","Dog"],["C","-0.5","0.4","2.1","Bird"],["D","1.4","1.6","0.2","Dog"]]}},{"title":"Question 3 - Linear Classifier","points":25,"scenario":"Consider a three-class linear image classifier: s = Wx + b\nx = [2, -1]T\nW = [[1, 2], [-1, 1], [2, -1]]\nb = [0, 1, -1]T\nClasses: 0 = Cat,   1 = Dog,   2 = Bird","parts":[{"label":"a","points":12,"prompt":"Calculate s = Wx + b. Show every step.","answer":"s = [0, -2, 4]T. Cat: 1·2 + 2·(-1) + 0 = 0. Dog: (-1)·2 + 1·(-1) + 1 = -2. Bird: 2·2 + (-1)·(-1) - 1 = 4.","steps":["Wx = [2 - 2, -2 - 1, 4 + 1]T = [0, -3, 5]T.","Add b = [0, 1, -1]T.","s = [0, -2, 4]T."],"rubric":["4 points per class: 2 for the dot product, 1 for the correct bias, and 1 for the final score.","Require exact values 0, -2, 4; accept row-vector notation when order is clear. Give method credit for one arithmetic error carried forward consistently."]},{"label":"b","points":3,"prompt":"What class does the classifier predict?","answer":"Class 2, Bird, because 4 is the largest score.","steps":["argmax([0, -2, 4]) = 2.","Map index 2 to Bird."],"rubric":["1 point for choosing the largest score; 2 for class 2/Bird. Accept consistent follow-through from part (a)."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Explain in your own words what W and b represent.","answer":"Each row of W contains the weights or learned template for one class. It determines how each input feature contributes to that class’s score. b contains one learned offset per class, shifting its score and the locations of decision boundaries without depending on the current input.","steps":["W has one row per class and one column per input feature.","Each b value is added to the corresponding class score."],"rubric":["3 points for W as learned feature weights/class templates, and 2 for b as a learned class offset.","Accept algebraic, visual, or geometric explanations that are consistent with s = Wx + b."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Why can't a single linear classifier correctly separate every possible dataset? You may include a small drawing.","answer":"Its pairwise decision boundaries are hyperplanes: equality between two class scores gives a linear equation. Some data, such as XOR, require a nonlinear boundary. For XOR, (0,0) and (1,1) have one label while (0,1) and (1,0) have the other; no single straight line separates these two binary classes.","steps":["Set two scores equal to obtain (w_i - w_j)·x + (b_i - b_j) = 0.","Explain a dataset whose classes cannot be separated by that type of boundary.","A nonlinear feature transform or a network with nonlinear activations can expand the model family."],"rubric":["2 points for linear/hyperplane limitations; 3 for a correct nonlinear example with explanation.","Accept XOR, concentric rings, or an equivalent valid example. A drawing is optional; do not penalize its absence."]}]},{"title":"Question 4 - Understand the CV Pipeline","points":20,"scenario":"A student wrote the following image-processing pipeline:","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Write the dimensions of img after resizing.","answer":"Under the default successful color read, img has NumPy shape (224, 224, 3): height 224, width 224, and 3 BGR channels. OpenCV’s resize argument is (width, height); the square size makes the two spatial numbers equal.","steps":["cv2.imread uses color mode by default.","cv2.resize(img, (224, 224)) sets width and height to 224.","The operation does not remove color channels."],"rubric":["2 points for height 224, 2 for width 224, and 1 for 3 channels.","Accept 224×224×3 with the order clearly stated. The question assumes that imread succeeds."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"What happens to the number of channels after BGR2GRAY?","answer":"The 3 BGR channels are combined into 1 grayscale intensity channel. The usual NumPy shape of gray is (224, 224), with no explicit singleton channel axis.","steps":["Conversion changes channel count, not spatial size.","A grayscale array commonly stores only height and width."],"rubric":["3 points for 3 → 1 channel; 2 for explaining intensity conversion or retaining spatial dimensions.","Accept a conceptual 224×224×1 representation if it is distinguished from the actual 2D OpenCV array shape."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Explain why Gaussian blur might be applied before Canny edge detection.","answer":"Noise creates rapid intensity changes that can produce false edges. Gaussian smoothing reduces those high-frequency fluctuations before Canny computes gradients and selects edges. Too much blur can also suppress real fine edges.","steps":["Connect noise to unstable or false gradient responses.","Explain how smoothing makes edge detection less sensitive to noise."],"rubric":["2 points for identifying the noise/false-edge issue; 3 for the smoothing explanation.","A correct practical noise-reduction explanation earns full marks; the blur trade-off is optional."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Suppose the classification task depends strongly on object color. Would you feed edges alone to the classifier? Explain your decision.","answer":"No. Edges mainly describe boundaries and structure and do not retain the object’s color. Preserve color images, or combine color-derived features with edge features, and compare alternatives on validation data.","steps":["Identify color as task-relevant information.","Show that grayscale edge extraction discards that information.","Retain color in the classifier input or feature set."],"rubric":["1 point for the decision, 3 for a correct task-based reason, and 1 for a viable color-preserving input.","Accept RGB/BGR images, suitable color-space features, or combined color and edge features."]}],"code":"img = cv2.imread(\"cat.jpg\")\nimg = cv2.resize(img, (224, 224))\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)\nedges = cv2.Canny(blur, 100, 200)"},{"title":"Question 5 - Design Your Own CV System","points":15,"scenario":"You need to develop a system that classifies waste into Plastic | Paper | Glass | Other. You have collected 2,000 images.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":3,"prompt":"How would you organize the dataset?","answer":"Assign one checked label to each image using the four classes. Store images with a label manifest, or class folders within each split. Remove duplicates and keep images of the same physical item together when creating independent train/validation/test sets.","steps":["Define a consistent four-class labeling scheme.","Use a reproducible image-to-label structure.","Prevent related images from crossing evaluation splits."],"rubric":["1 point for the four labels, 1 for a usable image/label organization, and 1 for a defensible organization or quality-control detail.","Accept class folders or a manifest; there is no required directory format."]},{"label":"b","points":3,"prompt":"Propose a train/test split and calculate the number of images in each part.","answer":"One valid choice is 80% training and 20% testing: 0.8·2000 = 1600 training images and 0.2·2000 = 400 test images. Keep the test set untouched; use a validation subset or cross-validation within the training portion for model selection.","steps":["Choose a defensible split ratio.","Multiply each fraction by 2000.","Check that the counts add to 2000."],"rubric":["1 point for a valid split, 1 for correct training count, and 1 for correct testing count.","Accept other justified ratios whose integer counts sum to 2000. A clearly explained three-way split is also defensible, although the prompt asks for train/test."]},{"label":"c","points":3,"prompt":"Give two preprocessing operations you would consider.","answer":"Resize with aspect-ratio-preserving padding to a common input size, and normalize intensities using the model’s expected convention. Mild denoising or a justified background crop are other possibilities.","steps":["Choose operations that standardize input or reduce irrelevant variation without erasing waste material cues."],"rubric":["1.5 points per relevant operation; accept any two justified task-appropriate choices.","Augmentation may also be accepted if clearly used only on training images and label-preserving."]},{"label":"d","points":3,"prompt":"What metric would you use to evaluate your classifier? Explain why.","answer":"Accuracy is a reasonable overall metric if the four classes are reasonably balanced, because it measures the fraction assigned the correct label. With imbalance, macro F1 or per-class precision and recall better expose poor minority-class performance; a confusion matrix shows which materials are confused.","steps":["Choose a metric appropriate to the stated or explicitly assumed class distribution.","Explain what it measures and why that matters."],"rubric":["1 point for a valid metric and 2 for a correct reason.","Accept accuracy with a balanced-class assumption, macro F1, balanced accuracy, or relevant per-class metrics. No class proportions are given, so accuracy is not the only correct answer."]},{"label":"e","points":3,"prompt":"Your model performs very well on your dataset but poorly when tested using photos taken by another student's phone. Give one possible reason.","answer":"Domain shift: the other phone may produce different lighting, color processing, sharpness, resolutions, viewpoints, or backgrounds from the training images. The classifier may have relied on those camera-specific patterns.","steps":["Identify a concrete difference in the input distribution.","Explain why the learned model may not generalize across it."],"rubric":["1 point for a plausible cause and 2 for its connection to worse performance.","Accept camera/lighting/background shift, inconsistent preprocessing, overfitting, or leakage in the original dataset when explained."]}]}]},{"id":"mock2","title":"Mock 2 — Sample-style: traffic signs and fruit sorting","time":120,"total":100,"description":"New practice questions closely matching the sample structure and mark distribution. They are original preparation material, not a prediction of the real exam. For arrays, state (height, width, channels); classify by the largest score.","groups":[{"title":"Question 1 - Image Processing & OpenCV","points":20,"scenario":"A traffic-sign classifier receives photographs of different sizes, with shadows and mild sensor noise. Sign color helps distinguish several categories.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Suggest three preprocessing operations. Explain the purpose of each in this scenario.","answer":"Resize with padding to a standard input size so batches align; apply a suitable intensity/illumination adjustment or model-specific normalization to reduce acquisition differences; use mild denoising to suppress sensor noise. Preserve sign color and small symbols.","steps":["Match resolution, illumination, and noise to suitable operations.","Explain the information each operation should preserve."],"rubric":["2 points per appropriate operation and reason.","Accept other justified choices such as a reliable sign crop; require three distinct operations."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Give one advantage and one risk of using grayscale traffic-sign images.","answer":"One channel reduces memory and computation. However, grayscale can remove color distinctions such as a red border versus a blue background that are useful for recognizing sign categories.","steps":["Compare channel count.","Connect the lost information to the sign classification task."],"rubric":["2 points for a valid advantage; 2 for a task-specific disadvantage. Accept equivalent explanations."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Identify two problems in the code and write a corrected version.","answer":"Default imread returns BGR, so replace COLOR_RGB2GRAY with COLOR_BGR2GRAY. GaussianBlur needs positive odd specified kernel sizes, so replace (6, 6) with (5, 5).\nimg = cv2.imread(\"sign.jpg\")\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (5, 5), 0)","steps":["Correct the color-order mismatch.","Choose a valid odd Gaussian kernel.","Optionally check img is not None before conversion."],"rubric":["2 points for the color correction and reason; 2 for the kernel correction and reason; 1 for usable code.","Accept another valid positive odd kernel."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Would very strong smoothing necessarily improve this classifier? Use a specific sign feature to explain.","answer":"No. Strong smoothing can erase a thin digit stroke, an arrow, or a small boundary detail, making distinct signs look alike. Use only as much denoising as validation results justify.","steps":["Name a discriminative sign feature.","Explain how smoothing can remove it and cause an error."],"rubric":["1 point for rejecting the universal claim; 3 for a specific feature and mechanism; 1 for a justified evaluation approach.","Accept any concrete sign feature and correct explanation."]}],"code":"img = cv2.imread(\"sign.jpg\")\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (6, 6), 0)"},{"title":"Question 2 - Image Classification","points":20,"scenario":"Use the largest score to predict the class for each sign image.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":4,"prompt":"Write the predictions for A, B, C, and D.","answer":"A: Stop; B: Yield; C: Yield; D: Speed limit.","steps":["A: 1.2 is highest.","B: 2.1 is highest.","C: 1.9 is highest.","D: 1.0 is highest."],"rubric":["1 point per correct class."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Calculate accuracy and show the fraction used.","answer":"A, B, and D are correct. Accuracy = 3/4 = 75%.","steps":["Count 3 correct labels.","Divide by 4 evaluated images."],"rubric":["1 point for the formula, 2 for the correct numerator/denominator, and 1 for 75% or 0.75."]},{"label":"c","points":4,"prompt":"Identify every incorrectly classified image and give its predicted and true class.","answer":"C is the only error: predicted Yield, true Speed limit.","steps":["Compare the largest score in C with the label in the last column."],"rubric":["2 points for C; 1 each for predicted Yield and true Speed limit."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"Can a raw score of 2.1 be read as a 210% probability? Explain.","answer":"No. A raw class score is not constrained to [0,1] or normalized across classes. Scores such as 2.1 can rank classes; an explicit transformation such as softmax produces a probability distribution.","steps":["Identify 2.1 as a raw score.","State probability bounds and normalization."],"rubric":["1 point for no; 3 for the correct score/probability explanation. Softmax need not be calculated."]},{"label":"e","points":4,"prompt":"Training accuracy is 98%, while test accuracy is 66%. Name a likely problem and one remedy.","answer":"Overfitting is likely. Use a remedy such as valid training augmentation, weight decay, or validation-based early stopping. Check distribution differences if the test images were collected differently.","steps":["Relate the large gap to generalization.","Tie a specific remedy to the proposed cause."],"rubric":["2 points for a justified cause; 2 for a relevant remedy. Accept a supported domain-shift diagnosis with a matched remedy."]}],"table":{"headers":["Image","Stop","Yield","Speed limit","True class"],"rows":[["A","1.2","0.1","-0.8","Stop"],["B","-0.4","2.1","0.7","Yield"],["C","0.8","1.9","1.5","Speed limit"],["D","-1.1","0.2","1.0","Speed limit"]]}},{"title":"Question 3 - Linear Classifier","points":25,"scenario":"s = Wx + b; x = [3, 2]T; W = [[1, -1], [-2, 2], [0.5, 1]]; b = [1, -1, 0]T. Classes: 0 = Stop, 1 = Yield, 2 = Speed limit.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":12,"prompt":"Calculate all three scores. Show the dot products and bias additions.","answer":"s = [2, -3, 3.5]T. Stop: 1·3 + (-1)·2 + 1 = 2. Yield: (-2)·3 + 2·2 - 1 = -3. Speed limit: 0.5·3 + 1·2 + 0 = 3.5.","steps":["Wx = [1, -2, 3.5]T.","Add b to get [2, -3, 3.5]T."],"rubric":["4 points per class: 2 for the dot product, 1 for bias, 1 for the score.","Require 2, -3, and 3.5 exactly; accept a consistent arithmetic carry-through for method marks."]},{"label":"b","points":3,"prompt":"Which class is predicted, and why?","answer":"Class 2, Speed limit, because 3.5 is highest.","steps":["Select argmax and map the index to its name."],"rubric":["1 point for selecting the maximum; 2 for class 2/Speed limit. Accept follow-through from part (a)."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Describe the shapes of W and b and what each row of W means.","answer":"W is 3×2: three classes, two input features. b is a length-3 vector. Each row of W gives the feature weights/template used to score one class; the corresponding bias is its input-independent offset.","steps":["Use one output per class and one weight per feature per class.","Explain the row-to-class correspondence."],"rubric":["2 points for W shape, 1 for b shape, and 2 for a correct semantic explanation."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Can a two-class linear classifier perfectly classify the XOR pattern in the original two input coordinates? Explain.","answer":"No. Opposite corners share a label, so no straight line places both of one label on one side and both of the other label on the other. Nonlinear features or a network with a nonlinear hidden layer can represent such a boundary.","steps":["List XOR labels: (0,0),(1,1) in one class; (0,1),(1,0) in the other.","Explain the straight-line limitation."],"rubric":["1 point for no; 3 for the valid geometric reason; 1 for identifying a nonlinear extension. A drawing is optional."]}]},{"title":"Question 4 - Understand the CV Pipeline","points":20,"scenario":"Assume sign.jpg loads successfully as a color image. OpenCV resize uses (width, height).","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Give the NumPy shape of img after the resize, using (height, width, channels).","answer":"(96, 160, 3). The size argument sets width to 160 and height to 96; the default color read has 3 channels.","steps":["Read resize arguments as (width, height).","Write array shape as (height, width, channels)."],"rubric":["2 points for height 96, 2 for width 160, and 1 for 3 channels."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"Give the channel counts and spatial array shapes of gray and edges.","answer":"Each has one channel and a 2D array shape (96,160). Grayscale conversion and Canny retain these spatial dimensions.","steps":["Grayscale removes the color channel axis.","Canny produces a single-channel edge map."],"rubric":["2 points for gray, 2 for edges, and 1 for retained spatial dimensions."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Why place Gaussian blur before Canny rather than rely on an unsmoothed noisy image?","answer":"It suppresses noise-induced intensity fluctuations, reducing spurious gradients and false edges. Mild smoothing can give more stable sign boundaries; excessive blur can suppress real details.","steps":["Connect sensor noise with unstable gradients.","Explain the effect of smoothing."],"rubric":["2 points for noise/false edges and 3 for the relevant smoothing explanation."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"If red and blue signs have similar silhouettes, is an edge map alone a sufficient representation? Explain one improvement.","answer":"It may be insufficient because the silhouettes can produce similar edges while the class distinction lies in color. Keep color images or combine color-space features with edges.","steps":["Identify the information removed by the pipeline.","Propose an input that preserves it."],"rubric":["1 point for the decision; 3 for the color-information reason; 1 for an appropriate improvement. Accept valid color-preserving alternatives."]}],"code":"img = cv2.imread(\"sign.jpg\")\nimg = cv2.resize(img, (160, 96))\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.GaussianBlur(gray, (3, 3), 0)\nedges = cv2.Canny(blur, 50, 150)"},{"title":"Question 5 - Design a Fruit-Sorting System","points":15,"scenario":"You have 3,200 labeled photos for Apple | Banana | Orange | Other. Some fruits were photographed several times.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":3,"prompt":"How would you organize images and labels while preventing an unrealistically easy test?","answer":"Use consistent four-class labels in folders or a manifest; remove duplicates; keep all photographs of a physical fruit in one split so the model cannot see that fruit in training and testing.","steps":["Record class and physical-item identity.","Assign related photos as a group."],"rubric":["1 point for image/label organization, 1 for the four-class scheme, and 1 for keeping related photos together. Accept equivalent leakage prevention."]},{"label":"b","points":3,"prompt":"Use an 80%/20% train/test split. Calculate the image counts.","answer":"Training: 0.8·3200 = 2560. Testing: 0.2·3200 = 640. Total = 3200.","steps":["Multiply each given fraction by 3200.","Check the sum."],"rubric":["1 point for the calculation method; 1 each for 2560 and 640. Exact integer counts are required."]},{"label":"c","points":3,"prompt":"Give two preprocessing choices suited to fruit classification.","answer":"Resize with padding to standardize shape, and apply the required intensity normalization. Preserve color because ripeness or fruit type may depend on it. A reliable background crop is another option.","steps":["Choose two distinct operations.","Retain class-relevant appearance."],"rubric":["1.5 points for each valid operation. Accept other justified choices; no specific pair is required."]},{"label":"d","points":3,"prompt":"If Other has far fewer examples, what evaluation metric would you report and why?","answer":"Macro F1 gives each class equal weight and combines precision with recall, making weak Other performance more visible than overall accuracy. Report a confusion matrix or per-class metrics to locate errors.","steps":["Identify how a majority can dominate overall accuracy.","Choose a metric that exposes minority-class performance."],"rubric":["1 point for a suitable metric; 2 for an imbalance-related reason. Accept macro F1, balanced accuracy, or relevant per-class precision/recall."]},{"label":"e","points":3,"prompt":"Performance falls on images taken in a supermarket rather than your studio. Give one plausible reason.","answer":"Domain shift in backgrounds, lighting, occlusion, or camera settings can change the appearance distribution. Studio-only training may encourage reliance on background cues.","steps":["Name a concrete environmental change.","Connect it to a learned shortcut or unseen variation."],"rubric":["1 point for a plausible change; 2 for its correct connection to poor generalization. Accept any well-explained cause."]}]}]},{"id":"mock3","title":"Mock 3 — Sample-style: industrial inspection","time":120,"total":100,"description":"A second new practice variant matching the sample’s five sections and marks. It changes the numbers and code issues. Assume image reads succeed unless a question asks about file handling.","groups":[{"title":"Question 1 - Image Processing & OpenCV","points":20,"scenario":"A factory classifies metal bolts as Normal | Crack | Rust. Images have different sizes, lighting varies, and some contain salt-and-pepper noise.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Suggest three preprocessing operations and explain each choice.","answer":"Resize or pad to a consistent input size; apply a justified illumination/intensity adjustment so lighting is less variable; use a median filter to remove isolated salt-and-pepper outliers while preserving edges reasonably well. Avoid destroying thin cracks or rust color.","steps":["Map each operation to a stated acquisition problem.","Connect median filtering to isolated extreme pixel values."],"rubric":["2 points per valid operation with a suitable reason.","Accept other justified operations. A Gaussian filter is less targeted to salt-and-pepper noise but can earn credit if its trade-off is explained."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Give one benefit and one limitation of grayscale images for this task.","answer":"Grayscale can reduce computation and retain crack shape/contrast. It can also remove orange-brown color evidence that helps distinguish rust from normal metal.","steps":["Identify an efficiency or structural benefit.","Identify a color cue that would be lost."],"rubric":["2 points for a valid benefit and 2 for a relevant limitation. Accept equivalent task-based answers."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Find the two issues in the code and provide a corrected version.","answer":"IMREAD_GRAYSCALE already returns a single-channel image, so BGR2GRAY is inappropriate and unnecessary. medianBlur also requires an odd kernel size greater than 1; change 4 to 5.\ngray = cv2.imread(\"bolt.png\", cv2.IMREAD_GRAYSCALE)\nblur = cv2.medianBlur(gray, 5)","steps":["Use the already grayscale image directly.","Replace the even median kernel size with a valid odd size.","Alternatively read in color first, then use BGR2GRAY and medianBlur with an odd kernel."],"rubric":["2 points for the channel/conversion correction; 2 for the kernel correction; 1 for coherent code.","Accept a color read followed by a correct conversion; accept a valid odd median kernel appropriate to the image type."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Explain why removing all small dark regions could damage crack detection.","answer":"A real crack can be a small dark region. An operation that removes every such region may erase positive-class evidence along with noise, making defective bolts appear normal. Distinguish noise from useful structure and verify recall on validation images.","steps":["Identify overlap between the target and the proposed removal rule.","Explain the resulting false negatives."],"rubric":["1 point for the decision; 3 for the correct crack/false-negative explanation; 1 for a task-relevant validation suggestion. Accept equivalent reasoning."]}],"code":"img = cv2.imread(\"bolt.png\", cv2.IMREAD_GRAYSCALE)\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\nblur = cv2.medianBlur(gray, 4)"},{"title":"Question 2 - Image Classification","points":20,"scenario":"The model outputs raw scores. Use argmax to classify each image.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":4,"prompt":"Give the predictions for A, B, C, and D.","answer":"A: Normal; B: Rust; C: Normal; D: Rust.","steps":["Largest scores are respectively 1.4, 1.9, 1.8, and 1.1."],"rubric":["1 point per correct predicted label."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Calculate accuracy with your working.","answer":"Only A and B are correct. Accuracy = 2/4 = 50%.","steps":["Compare predictions with true labels.","Divide 2 correct predictions by 4 images."],"rubric":["1 point for the accuracy formula, 2 for the correct numerator/denominator, and 1 for 50% or 0.5."]},{"label":"c","points":4,"prompt":"Which images are incorrect? Give their true and predicted labels.","answer":"C: true Crack, predicted Normal. D: true Crack, predicted Rust.","steps":["Check C and D against the last column."],"rubric":["2 points per error correctly identified with both labels."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"Why is a negative class score allowed, while a negative class probability is not?","answer":"A score is an unconstrained numerical preference from the model; only relative score order is needed for argmax. Probabilities represent a normalized distribution and must lie in [0,1].","steps":["Distinguish model score scale from probability constraints."],"rubric":["2 points for correctly describing raw scores; 2 for the probability constraint."]},{"label":"e","points":4,"prompt":"Training accuracy is 96% and test accuracy is 63%. Give one plausible diagnosis and a matched improvement.","answer":"Overfitting is plausible; use training augmentation, regularization, or validation-based early stopping. If lighting/camera conditions differ across splits, collect representative training images or standardize the preprocessing to address domain shift.","steps":["State one cause.","Choose a remedy that targets that cause."],"rubric":["2 points for a justified diagnosis; 2 for a relevant improvement. The gap alone does not establish the cause."]}],"table":{"headers":["Image","Normal","Crack","Rust","True class"],"rows":[["A","1.4","0.2","-0.1","Normal"],["B","0.7","0.5","1.9","Rust"],["C","1.8","1.5","0.2","Crack"],["D","-0.3","0.9","1.1","Crack"]]}},{"title":"Question 3 - Linear Classifier","points":25,"scenario":"s = Wx + b; x = [-2, 3]T; W = [[2, 1], [-1, 2], [1, -2]]; b = [0, -2, 1]T. Classes: 0 = Normal, 1 = Crack, 2 = Rust.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":12,"prompt":"Calculate all scores, showing the row-by-row products and biases.","answer":"Normal: 2·(-2) + 1·3 + 0 = -1. Crack: (-1)·(-2) + 2·3 - 2 = 6. Rust: 1·(-2) + (-2)·3 + 1 = -7. Thus s = [-1, 6, -7]T.","steps":["Wx = [-1, 8, -8]T.","Add [0, -2, 1]T to get [-1, 6, -7]T."],"rubric":["4 points per score: 2 for the dot product, 1 for adding bias, 1 for the final value.","Require exact integer scores; allow consistent follow-through for arithmetic errors."]},{"label":"b","points":3,"prompt":"State the predicted class and decision rule.","answer":"Class 1, Crack; choose the highest of the three scores, 6.","steps":["argmax([-1,6,-7]) = 1."],"rubric":["1 point for the rule; 2 for class 1/Crack. Accept consistent follow-through."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Explain how changing one row of W differs from changing one entry of b.","answer":"Changing a W row changes how input features influence one class score, so its effect depends on x. Changing the corresponding bias adds the same offset to that class score for every input, shifting its preference and pairwise boundaries.","steps":["Identify input-dependent feature effects from W.","Identify the input-independent offset from b."],"rubric":["3 points for the weight-row interpretation and 2 for the bias interpretation. Accept correct algebraic/geometric explanations."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Why might a linear model fail if Rust examples form a ring around Normal examples in two feature dimensions?","answer":"A ring surrounds the inner class, so a straight-line binary boundary cannot separate the full ring from its center. A nonlinear feature such as distance from the center, or a nonlinear model, can represent this structure.","steps":["Connect linear scores with straight boundaries in two dimensions.","Explain the surrounding-ring geometry."],"rubric":["2 points for the linear-boundary limitation; 2 for a correct ring explanation; 1 for a suitable nonlinear extension. Accept equivalent reasoning."]}]},{"title":"Question 4 - Understand the CV Pipeline","points":20,"scenario":"Assume bolt.jpg is successfully read as a uint8 color image. THRESH_BINARY produces maxval when the input exceeds the threshold and 0 otherwise.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Give img.shape after resize, in (height, width, channels) order.","answer":"(64, 128, 3). OpenCV reads the resize size as (width, height).","steps":["Width is 128, height is 64, color channels remain 3."],"rubric":["2 points for height 64, 2 for width 128, and 1 for channels 3."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"Give the shapes and possible pixel values of gray and binary.","answer":"Both arrays have shape (64,128) and one channel. gray may have uint8 intensities from 0 to 255. binary contains only 0 and 255.","steps":["Grayscale conversion retains spatial size.","Binary threshold maps intensities to two output values."],"rubric":["2 points for the shapes/channel count; 1 for gray’s range; 2 for binary values 0 and 255."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Why can the fixed threshold 120 fail when the room lighting changes?","answer":"An illumination change shifts measured intensities, so the same material may fall on opposite sides of 120. Illumination normalization or a suitable adaptive threshold can help; validate the result because material and defect cues must remain distinguishable.","steps":["Connect lighting with changed pixel intensities.","Explain incorrect foreground/background decisions.","Propose a relevant adjustment."],"rubric":["3 points for the correct failure mechanism; 2 for a justified remedy. Accept controlled lighting, normalization, or suitable threshold selection."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Would binary shape information alone be enough to distinguish orange rust from gray metal? Explain one alternative.","answer":"It can be insufficient because thresholding destroys color and most intensity detail. Keep the color image or color-space features, possibly alongside structural features.","steps":["Identify the relevant color distinction.","Explain which information binary thresholding discards."],"rubric":["1 point for the decision, 3 for a correct explanation, and 1 for a color-preserving alternative."]}],"code":"img = cv2.imread(\"bolt.jpg\")\nimg = cv2.resize(img, (128, 64))\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n_, binary = cv2.threshold(gray, 120, 255, cv2.THRESH_BINARY)"},{"title":"Question 5 - Design an Inspection System","points":15,"scenario":"You have 1,500 metal-part images for Normal | Cracked | Rusted | Other. Photos from the same physical part may be very similar.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":3,"prompt":"Describe a suitable label organization and one rule for independent evaluation.","answer":"Maintain four consistent class labels in folders or a manifest and include part IDs. Keep all images of each part within one split, rather than putting near-identical part photos in both training and evaluation.","steps":["Organize image-to-label mapping.","Split by physical-part identity."],"rubric":["1 point for a usable organization, 1 for class consistency, and 1 for avoiding related-image leakage."]},{"label":"b","points":3,"prompt":"Use 70% train, 15% validation, and 15% test. Calculate counts.","answer":"Training: 1050; validation: 225; test: 225. Counts sum to 1500.","steps":["0.70·1500 = 1050.","0.15·1500 = 225 for each evaluation subset."],"rubric":["1 point per exact count."]},{"label":"c","points":3,"prompt":"Name two useful preprocessing operations for this inspection task.","answer":"Resize with padding, and mild median denoising when impulse noise is present. Model-specific intensity normalization or a reliable part crop are also reasonable. Preserve fine cracks and rust color.","steps":["Choose operations relevant to the task’s image variation."],"rubric":["1.5 points for each valid choice. Accept any two distinct task-appropriate operations."]},{"label":"d","points":3,"prompt":"If missing a crack is expensive, what per-class metric matters and why?","answer":"Crack recall = TP/(TP+FN) matters because it measures the fraction of genuinely cracked parts detected. It directly exposes missed cracks; precision and inspection costs should also be monitored.","steps":["Identify false negatives as missed cracks.","Relate recall to detecting the true positive class."],"rubric":["1 point for crack recall, 1 for the correct interpretation/formula, and 1 for the cost-based explanation.","Accept an appropriate cost-sensitive metric if the rationale is explicit."]},{"label":"e","points":3,"prompt":"The system fails on a new conveyor belt although the parts are similar. Give one plausible cause.","answer":"Background domain shift: the model may have associated the original conveyor background with a label and now sees different texture/color. Lighting, camera angle, or motion blur could also differ.","steps":["Identify one concrete deployment difference.","Explain how the classifier’s reliance on it can hurt generalization."],"rubric":["1 point for the cause; 2 for its relevant explanation. Accept any well-supported acquisition or distribution shift."]}]}]},{"id":"mock4","title":"Mock 4 — Expanded lecture coverage: losses, learning, CNNs","time":120,"total":100,"description":"Expanded preparation beyond the supplied sample: Lecture 2 kNN, Lecture 3 losses, Lecture 4 derivatives and updates, and Lecture 5 CNN shapes. All logarithms are natural; include one bias per convolutional output channel. These sections broaden coverage and are not a forecast of the exam.","groups":[{"title":"Question 1 - kNN and Evaluation","points":20,"scenario":"A query has features x = [1,2]. Use L1 distance d(x,z)=|x1-z1|+|x2-z2|. The training table follows. In a distance tie, use alphabetical ID order; for a vote tie, choose the label of the closest selected neighbor.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Calculate the L1 distance to every training point.","answer":"A: 3; B: 1; C: 2; D: 4; E: 1.","steps":["A: |1-0|+|2-0|=3.","B: |1-2|+|2-2|=1.","C: |1-1|+|2-4|=2.","D: |1-4|+|2-1|=4.","E: |1-0|+|2-2|=1."],"rubric":["1 point for each exact distance and 1 for showing a correct distance calculation or formula."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Predict using k=1 and k=3. Identify the selected neighbors.","answer":"k=1: B (tie with E resolved by ID), so Paper. k=3: B, E, C; all are Paper, so Paper.","steps":["Sort by distance, then ID: B, E, C, A, D.","Use the nearest label for k=1 and majority vote for k=3."],"rubric":["2 points for each correct neighbor selection and label. Follow the stated tie convention."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Where should you choose k and the distance metric? Why should the final test not be used for that choice?","answer":"Choose hyperparameters using a validation set or cross-validation on the training portion. Reserve the independent final test for evaluation after choices are fixed; selecting the best test result makes the reported performance optimistically biased.","steps":["Separate fitting, model selection, and final evaluation.","Explain how repeated test-based choices leak information into model selection."],"rubric":["2 points for validation/cross-validation, 2 for test-set selection bias, and 1 for a correct final-evaluation protocol. Accept equivalent explanations."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Explain one difficulty of raw-pixel nearest neighbors on high-resolution images and one possible improvement.","answer":"Raw pixel distances can be dominated by lighting, shifts, or backgrounds, and high-dimensional space requires many examples for useful coverage. A learned feature representation can make distances more semantically meaningful; reducing dimensionality or controlling acquisition may also help.","steps":["Identify one concrete raw-pixel/high-dimensional difficulty.","Connect an improvement to that difficulty."],"rubric":["3 points for a correct difficulty with explanation; 2 for a matched improvement.","Accept curse-of-dimensionality, shift sensitivity, nuisance variation, or brute-force distance cost with an appropriate remedy."]}],"table":{"headers":["ID","Features","Label"],"rows":[["A","[0,0]","Plastic"],["B","[2,2]","Paper"],["C","[1,4]","Paper"],["D","[4,1]","Glass"],["E","[0,2]","Paper"]]}},{"title":"Question 2 - Scores and Losses","points":20,"scenario":"For one example, scores in class-index order are s=[2,1,0] and the true class index is y=1. Softmax pj=exp(sj)/sum_k exp(sk); cross-entropy LCE=-ln(py). Multiclass hinge loss is LH=sum_{j≠y} max(0,sj-sy+1). No regularization is included.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":4,"prompt":"Give the predicted class and say whether it is correct.","answer":"Class 0 is predicted because its score 2 is largest. It is incorrect because the true class is 1.","steps":["Take argmax over [2,1,0].","Compare index 0 with index 1."],"rubric":["2 points for predicted index 0 and 2 for the correctness comparison."]},{"label":"b","points":6,"prompt":"Calculate all softmax probabilities and the cross-entropy loss. Round to three decimals.","answer":"p ≈ [0.665,0.245,0.090]. LCE = -ln(0.244728471) ≈ 1.408.","steps":["For stability, subtract 2: exp scores [1,e^-1,e^-2].","Divide by 1+e^-1+e^-2 ≈ 1.503214724.","Use the true-class probability p1 ≈ 0.244728471, not the largest probability."],"rubric":["1 point per probability (3 total), 1 for normalization, 1 for using p1, and 1 for LCE≈1.408.","Accept probabilities within 0.001 and loss within 0.002; accept exact exponential/log expressions. Avoid computing loss from over-rounded probabilities when exact values are available."]},{"label":"c","points":6,"prompt":"Calculate the multiclass hinge loss, showing each incorrect-class term.","answer":"For j=0: max(0,2-1+1)=2. For j=2: max(0,0-1+1)=0. LH=2+0=2.","steps":["Exclude the true-class term.","Use margin 1 for each other class.","Sum both nonnegative terms."],"rubric":["2 points per correct term, 1 for excluding j=y, and 1 for exact total 2."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"If the true class has the highest score, must either loss equal zero? Explain the two conditions.","answer":"Highest score alone does not imply zero hinge loss: the correct score must exceed each other score by at least the margin 1. Softmax cross-entropy is greater than zero for finite scores with more than one class, because the correct probability is strictly less than 1; it approaches zero as that probability approaches 1.","steps":["Distinguish argmax correctness from satisfying a margin.","Distinguish high probability from probability exactly 1."],"rubric":["2 points for the hinge margin condition; 2 for the finite-score cross-entropy condition. Accept equivalent mathematically correct explanations."]}]},{"title":"Question 3 - Gradient Descent and Branching","points":25,"scenario":"One training example has x=2, target t=1, prediction yhat=wx+b, and L=0.5(yhat-t)^2. Initially w=0.25, b=0. Use learning rate η=0.1 and simultaneous gradient-descent updates of w and b using the old values. There is no regularization.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Calculate the initial prediction and loss.","answer":"yhat=0.25·2+0=0.5. L=0.5(0.5-1)^2=0.125.","steps":["Compute the linear prediction.","Compute the residual -0.5, square it, and multiply by 0.5."],"rubric":["2 points for exact prediction 0.5; 3 for exact loss 0.125 with the stated one-half factor."]},{"label":"b","points":8,"prompt":"Find ∂L/∂w and ∂L/∂b, showing the chain rule.","answer":"∂L/∂yhat=yhat-t=-0.5. ∂yhat/∂w=x=2 and ∂yhat/∂b=1. Therefore ∂L/∂w=-1 and ∂L/∂b=-0.5.","steps":["Differentiate 0.5(yhat-t)^2.","Multiply the upstream residual by each local derivative."],"rubric":["2 points for the residual derivative; 2 for local derivatives; 2 each for the exact parameter gradients."]},{"label":"c","points":6,"prompt":"Perform one update, then calculate the new prediction and loss on the same example.","answer":"wnew=0.25-0.1(-1)=0.35. bnew=0-0.1(-0.5)=0.05. yhatnew=0.35·2+0.05=0.75. Lnew=0.5(0.75-1)^2=0.03125.","steps":["Subtract η times each old gradient.","Evaluate using both new parameters.","The loss decreases from 0.125 to 0.03125 for this step."],"rubric":["2 points for the parameter updates, 2 for prediction 0.75, and 2 for loss 0.03125.","Accept decimal results within 1e-6 or exact fractions. Do not recompute the second gradient after updating the first parameter."]},{"label":"d","points":6,"prompt":"For the separate graph f(u)=u·u+3·u at u=2, find df/du. Explain why gradients from both branches must be added.","answer":"The u·u branch contributes u+u=4 and the 3·u branch contributes 3. Since the same u influences both terms, df/du=4+3=7.","steps":["Treat the two uses of u in multiplication as separate paths; each contributes u=2.","Add the third contribution, 3.","Sum all paths that reach the shared input."],"rubric":["2 points for derivative 4 of u·u, 1 for derivative 3 of 3·u, 2 for summing branches, and 1 for exact total 7."]}]},{"title":"Question 4 - CNN Shapes and Parameters","points":20,"scenario":"Input shape is 32×32×3 (H×W×C). Layer 1: convolution with eight 3×3 filters, stride 1, padding 1, one bias/filter, followed by ReLU. Then max pool 2×2, stride 2, no padding. Layer 2: sixteen 3×3 filters, stride 1, no padding, one bias/filter. Use O=floor((N-K+2P)/S)+1 for each spatial axis.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Find the shape after layer 1/ReLU and after max pooling.","answer":"After layer 1/ReLU: 32×32×8. After pooling: 16×16×8.","steps":["Conv: (32-3+2·1)/1+1=32; output channels=8.","ReLU changes values but not shape.","Pool: (32-2)/2+1=16; channels stay 8."],"rubric":["3 points for each correct shape with supporting reasoning. Require exact integer dimensions."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"Find the output shape of layer 2.","answer":"14×14×16, because (16-3)/1+1=14 and there are sixteen filters.","steps":["Use pooled spatial size 16 as the input.","Output depth equals filter count."],"rubric":["3 points for spatial calculation and 2 for correct channels 16."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Count trainable parameters in each convolutional layer, including biases.","answer":"Layer 1: 8·(3·3·3+1)=224. Layer 2: 16·(3·3·8+1)=1168. ReLU and max pooling contribute no trainable parameters.","steps":["Each filter spans all input channels.","Include one bias per output filter.","Count weights once, because they are shared across positions."],"rubric":["2 points for 224, 2 for 1168, and 1 for a correct parameter-count method including biases. Exact counts are required."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"Explain how local connectivity and weight sharing help compared with a fully connected layer on flattened pixels.","answer":"Local connectivity uses spatially neighboring pixels to detect patterns. Weight sharing reuses one filter at every position, reducing parameter count and making a shifted feature produce a shifted response, subject to borders/stride. A fully connected layer uses separate weights for every input-to-unit connection.","steps":["Link locality to spatial structure.","Link sharing to reuse, parameter economy, and shifted responses."],"rubric":["2 points for locality and 2 for weight sharing. Accept equivalent explanations; do not require a claim of perfect translation invariance."]}]},{"title":"Question 5 - Build and Evaluate a Leaf Classifier","points":15,"scenario":"A leaf dataset contains 7,500 images, including several views from each plant. A mobile app will classify Healthy | Disease A | Disease B.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Use a nominal 70%/15%/15% train/validation/test allocation. Calculate image counts and explain how repeated views should be handled.","answer":"Nominal counts: 5250 training, 1125 validation, 1125 test. Keep all views of a plant in one split; grouped splitting may slightly change exact image counts to maintain independence. Use validation for model selection and test for final evaluation.","steps":["Multiply fractions by 7500.","Treat plant identity as a grouping unit.","Assign distinct roles to validation and test."],"rubric":["1 point for each nominal count, 1 for plant-group separation, and 1 for correct validation/test roles."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"The CNN reaches 99% training accuracy but 68% validation accuracy. Suggest a cause and two actions, with reasons.","answer":"Overfitting or distribution mismatch is plausible. Use valid augmentation that represents realistic camera/light variation, and stronger regularization or validation-based early stopping. Also inspect labels and split independence rather than assuming that the gap has only one cause.","steps":["Connect the accuracy gap with weak generalization.","Choose two interventions and state how each could help."],"rubric":["1 point for a plausible cause; 2 points per action with a correct reason.","Accept improved representative data, appropriate model-capacity changes, label auditing, or other matched remedies."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Name one suitable architecture family for a phone and explain how you would compare it with a larger model.","answer":"MobileNet is a reasonable candidate because depthwise separable convolutions reduce computation; a small EfficientNet is another option. Compare validation accuracy/per-class metrics and measured latency, memory, and energy on the target phone, then evaluate the selected system on the untouched test.","steps":["Identify an efficiency-oriented architecture.","Measure the trade-off on the deployment hardware.","Avoid choosing the architecture by repeatedly using the final test."],"rubric":["2 points for a suitable family and relevant reason; 2 for accuracy/efficiency comparison; 1 for an independent final test.","Accept another justified small architecture; no architecture is automatically best for every device."]}]}]},{"id":"mock5","title":"Mock 5 — Expanded lecture coverage: filtering, backprop, architecture","time":120,"total":100,"description":"Expanded practice using Szeliski Chapter 3 image processing and Lectures 3–5. Numerical conventions are explicit. CNN calculations use cross-correlation as in deep-learning libraries, one bias per output channel, and exact integer shapes.","groups":[{"title":"Question 1 - Image Processing Decisions","points":20,"scenario":"Work with scalar grayscale intensities. For the first two subparts, calculate only the center output from the stated 3×3 patch; no border rule is needed.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"For patch [[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]], apply a 3×3 averaging filter with every coefficient 1/9. Calculate the center output.","answer":"(1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8+9)/9 = 45/9 = 5.","steps":["Multiply each patch value by 1/9.","Sum the nine products."],"rubric":["2 points for the averaging method, 2 for sum 45, and 1 for exact output 5."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"For patch [[10,10,11],[9,255,10],[11,9,10]], calculate the 3×3 median-filter center output. Why is this useful for the bright outlier?","answer":"Sorted values: [9,9,10,10,10,10,11,11,255]. The fifth value is 10. The median ignores the magnitude of the isolated 255 outlier rather than allowing it to dominate the local estimate.","steps":["Sort the nine values.","Choose the fifth value, not their average.","Relate robustness to impulse noise."],"rubric":["2 points for sorting/selecting the median, 1 for exact output 10, and 2 for the outlier explanation."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Transform intensities [0,64,128,255] using J=clip(1.5I+20,0,255). Give outputs and explain why clipping should occur before casting to uint8.","answer":"The unbounded results are [20,116,212,402]; clipping gives [20,116,212,255]. Compute in a sufficiently wide or floating type and clip before casting so out-of-range values do not wrap or overflow.","steps":["Multiply and add in a wide numeric type.","Clamp the final value to the valid range.","Cast to uint8 after clamping."],"rubric":["3 points for the four correct outputs, 1 for clipping 402 to 255, and 1 for the overflow/wrap explanation. Exact values are required."]},{"label":"d","points":5,"prompt":"Why can naive downsampling create aliasing, and what processing step can reduce it?","answer":"Downsampling lowers the sampling rate. High-frequency patterns can then appear as false lower-frequency structures, such as moiré. Apply an appropriate low-pass/anti-alias filter before decimation so frequencies above the new sampling limit are suppressed.","steps":["Connect lower sample density to unresolved high frequencies.","Explain the purpose of prefiltering."],"rubric":["3 points for the correct aliasing mechanism; 2 for an appropriate low-pass step. Accept a correctly described anti-aliased resize/interpolation method."]}]},{"title":"Question 2 - A Two-Layer Network","points":20,"scenario":"x=[1,-2]T. W1=[[1,1],[-1,-2]], b1=[0.5,-0.5]T; z=W1x+b1; h=ReLU(z). W2=[[2,-1],[1,2]], b2=[0.5,-0.5]T; s=W2h+b2. Classes are 0 and 1, predicted by argmax.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Calculate z and h, showing the effect of ReLU.","answer":"z1=1·1+1·(-2)+0.5=-0.5; z2=(-1)·1+(-2)·(-2)-0.5=2.5. Therefore z=[-0.5,2.5]T and h=[0,2.5]T.","steps":["Compute each affine hidden value.","ReLU replaces the negative entry with zero and keeps the positive entry."],"rubric":["2 points for each z entry and 2 for the exact ReLU output."]},{"label":"b","points":4,"prompt":"Calculate s and the predicted class.","answer":"s0=2·0+(-1)·2.5+0.5=-2. s1=1·0+2·2.5-0.5=4.5. Class 1 is predicted.","steps":["s=[-2,4.5]T.","The maximum is at index 1."],"rubric":["1.5 points per exact score and 1 for class 1. Accept consistent follow-through from (a)."]},{"label":"c","points":6,"prompt":"If ReLU were removed, derive the effective single-layer Wprime and bprime. Explain the implication.","answer":"s=W2(W1x+b1)+b2=(W2W1)x+(W2b1+b2). Wprime=[[3,4],[-1,-3]] and bprime=[2,-1]T. Stacking these affine layers without a nonlinear activation still gives a single affine classifier, so depth alone does not create nonlinear boundaries.","steps":["Multiply W2W1: first row [3,4], second row [-1,-3].","Compute W2b1=[1.5,-0.5]T, then add b2.","Identify the unchanged affine model family."],"rubric":["2 points for the derivation, 2 for exact Wprime/bprime, and 2 for the implication. Accept equivalent algebraic explanations."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"The first hidden preactivation is negative for this example. What happens to its gradient through ReLU? Does that alone prove the unit is permanently dead?","answer":"Its ReLU local derivative is 0, so this example sends zero gradient through that branch. It is not necessarily permanently dead: other inputs may make its preactivation positive. A dead unit stays inactive across the relevant inputs and receives no useful activation gradient.","steps":["Apply the derivative at -0.5.","Distinguish one inactive example from persistent inactivity."],"rubric":["2 points for zero gradient and 2 for the correct distinction. No convention at exactly zero is needed here."]}]},{"title":"Question 3 - Matrix Backpropagation","points":25,"scenario":"s=Wx+b, x=[2,-1]T, W=[[1,0],[-1,2]], b=[0.5,-0.5]T. For this example the upstream gradient is g=∂L/∂s=[3,-2]T. For subpart (d), use the scalar loss L=3s0-2s1 and vary only w=W[0,0].","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Calculate s and give the shapes of W, x, and g.","answer":"s=[2.5,-4.5]T. W has shape 2×2; x and g are 2×1 column vectors.","steps":["s0=1·2+0·(-1)+0.5=2.5.","s1=(-1)·2+2·(-1)-0.5=-4.5."],"rubric":["2 points for scores, 1 for W shape, and 1 each for x and g shapes. Equivalent length-2 array notation is accepted if column-vector operations are clear."]},{"label":"b","points":8,"prompt":"Calculate ∂L/∂W and ∂L/∂b. Show the outer product.","answer":"∂L/∂W=gxT=[[3],[-2]]·[[2,-1]]=[[6,-3],[-4,2]]. ∂L/∂b=g=[3,-2]T.","steps":["Gradient entry (i,j) is gi·xj.","Adding b to s gives identity local derivatives.","Gradient shapes match W and b."],"rubric":["2 points for gxT and its shape, 1 point per correct matrix entry (4 total), and 2 for db. Exact values are required."]},{"label":"c","points":6,"prompt":"Calculate ∂L/∂x and explain the transpose.","answer":"∂L/∂x=WTg=[[1,-1],[0,2]]·[3,-2]T=[5,-4]T. Each input contributes to multiple output scores, so its gradient sums the corresponding W column weighted by g.","steps":["dx1=1·3+(-1)·(-2)=5.","dx2=0·3+2·(-2)=-4.","WT maps the output-gradient column back to the input-gradient column."],"rubric":["2 points for WTg, 1.5 points per exact gradient entry, and 1 for a correct shape/contribution explanation."]},{"label":"d","points":6,"prompt":"At w=1, use h=0.00001, L(w+h)=16.500060, and L(w-h)=16.499940. Compute the central-difference gradient and compare with the analytic result for w.","answer":"[16.500060-16.499940]/(2·0.00001)=0.000120/0.000020=6. This agrees with ∂L/∂W[0,0]=6.","steps":["Use the two-sided central-difference formula.","Calculate numerator and denominator separately.","Compare with the matching weight entry, not dx or db."],"rubric":["2 points for the formula, 2 for numerical result 6, and 2 for the correct analytic comparison.","Accept within 1e-6 given the stated rounded loss values. This validates this coordinate for this example; it does not prove every gradient implementation correct."]}]},{"title":"Question 4 - CNN Architecture","points":20,"scenario":"Input: 28×28×1. Conv A: six 5×5 filters, S=1, P=0. Max pool: K=2, S=2, P=0. Conv B: twelve 3×3 filters, S=1, P=1. Then global average pooling and a fully connected 3-class head. Every convolution filter and every FC output has one bias. Shapes are H×W×C.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":6,"prompt":"Give shapes after Conv A, max pool, Conv B, and global average pooling.","answer":"Conv A: 24×24×6. Pool: 12×12×6. Conv B: 12×12×12. Global average pooling: 1×1×12, or a vector of 12 features.","steps":["Conv A: 28-5+1=24.","Pool: (24-2)/2+1=12.","Conv B: 12-3+2·1+1=12.","Average each feature map over its spatial positions."],"rubric":["1.5 points per correct stage. Accept the GAP vector representation when its 12 features are stated."]},{"label":"b","points":6,"prompt":"Count parameters in Conv A, Conv B, and the FC head. Give the total.","answer":"Conv A: 6·(5·5·1+1)=156. Conv B: 12·(3·3·6+1)=660. FC: 12·3+3=39. Total=156+660+39=855. Pooling and global average pooling add no learned parameters.","steps":["Include full input-channel depth in each filter.","Include one bias per output.","GAP gives the FC head exactly 12 inputs."],"rubric":["1.5 points per exact layer count and 1.5 for exact total 855. Give method credit for correct follow-through."]},{"label":"c","points":4,"prompt":"With stride 1, compare the receptive field of two 3×3 convolutions with one 5×5 convolution. Ignore biases and assume every layer has C input/output channels. Compare weight counts.","answer":"Both have a 5×5 receptive field. Two 3×3 layers use 2·9C²=18C² weights versus 25C² for one 5×5 layer, and can include an additional intermediate nonlinearity.","steps":["Two 3-wide layers see 1+2·(3-1)=5 input positions per axis.","Count both sets of 3×3 weights under the stated equal-channel assumption."],"rubric":["2 points for receptive field 5×5; 2 for correct comparison 18C² versus 25C². The extra nonlinearity explanation is useful but optional."]},{"label":"d","points":4,"prompt":"State the residual-block relation and explain when its skip connection needs a projection.","answer":"A residual block adds y=F(x)+x before any final activation. Elementwise addition requires matching spatial and channel shapes. If the main path changes resolution or channels, a suitable projection, often a stride/channel-adjusting 1×1 convolution, can match the skip shape.","steps":["State the identity addition.","Check shape compatibility before addition."],"rubric":["2 points for the relation, 1 for matching-shape requirement, and 1 for a suitable projection. Accept equivalent valid shape-matching methods."]}]},{"title":"Question 5 - Evaluation Under Imbalance and Shift","points":15,"scenario":"An inspection test set contains 1000 images: 20 faulty and 980 normal. Treat Faulty as positive. Counts: true positives TP=12, false negatives FN=8, false positives FP=28, true negatives TN=952. All model choices were fixed before this final test.","parts":[{"label":"a","points":5,"prompt":"Calculate Faulty precision and recall.","answer":"Precision=TP/(TP+FP)=12/40=0.30=30%. Recall=TP/(TP+FN)=12/20=0.60=60%.","steps":["Use predicted-positive count 40 for precision.","Use actual-positive count 20 for recall."],"rubric":["2.5 points per metric: formula, denominator, and correct value. Accept exact fractions; decimals within 0.001."]},{"label":"b","points":5,"prompt":"Calculate accuracy and compare with always predicting Normal. Why can accuracy alone mislead here?","answer":"Model accuracy=(TP+TN)/1000=964/1000=96.4%. Always Normal gives 980/1000=98%. The baseline detects no faulty items despite higher accuracy; minority-class recall and precision, or an explicit cost measure, reveal this weakness.","steps":["Count all correct predictions for accuracy.","Compute the majority-class baseline.","Compare the positive-class behavior, not only overall correctness."],"rubric":["2 points for 96.4%, 1 for 98%, and 2 for the correct imbalance explanation. Accept equivalent cost/per-class reasoning."]},{"label":"c","points":5,"prompt":"Recall worsens on a second phone. Give one possible cause and one remedy or evaluation check. Should you keep adjusting hyperparameters against this final test set?","answer":"A camera or lighting domain shift, or inconsistent input preprocessing, may change the image distribution. Check the exact color order/normalization and collect representative images for a separate development/validation process. Do not keep tuning on the final test; if it informs changes, use a fresh independent test to evaluate the revised system.","steps":["Name a specific distribution or preprocessing difference.","Match a data/processing remedy to it.","Keep final evaluation independent of subsequent selection."],"rubric":["1.5 points for a plausible cause, 1.5 for a matched remedy/check, and 2 for the correct test-set protocol.","Accept domain shift, preprocessing mismatch, or a supported alternate explanation; multiple remedies are possible."]}]}]}],"corrections":[{"source":"Lecture 3 slide 84","issue":"Uniform-score cross-entropy formula has an incorrect minus sign.","correction":"L=−ln(1/C)=ln(C), consistent with its positive log(10) numerical example."},{"source":"Lecture 3 slides 17–18","issue":"Score-scaling identity assumes bias is ignored.","correction":"For f(x)=Wx+b, f(cx)=cf(x)+(1−c)b; the simple identity is valid with b=0."},{"source":"Lecture 3 slides 81–82","issue":"Minimum-zero wording needs the finite-logit qualification.","correction":"With more than one class and finite logits, cross-entropy is positive and has infimum zero as p_y approaches one."},{"source":"Lecture 5 slide 11","issue":"Floor formula is inconsistent with the exact-fit table entry for W=7,K=3,S=3,P=0.","correction":"Standard floor sizing gives two fully fitting windows. An exact-tiling convention can reject the remainder, but that is a different convention."},{"source":"Lecture 5 slides 4,14","issue":"Flattening wording suggests literal information loss.","correction":"Known flattening preserves values and ordering; an ordinary FC layer lacks enforced locality and sharing."},{"source":"Lecture 5 slides 5,18","issue":"Translation equivariance and small-shift pooling tolerance can be mistaken for perfect classifier invariance.","correction":"Equivariance shifts feature maps; invariance fixes outputs. Padding, stride, boundaries and the classifier limit exact guarantees."},{"source":"Trask Ch. 4, printed pp. 50,56–59,70–71","issue":"The displayed squared error and the named derivative use different factor conventions.","correction":"For E=(p−y)^2 the gradient is 2(p−y)x. The book uses (p−y)x, matching half-squared loss or a rescaled learning rate."},{"source":"Trask Ch. 5, printed pp. 91–93","issue":"Several printed multi-output snippets use weights rather than residuals, overwrite delta, or orient the outer product inconsistently.","correction":"For column-vector p=Wx with output rows, maintain delta[i]=p[i]−y[i] and use outer(delta,input), then update W simultaneously."},{"source":"Lecture 4 slide 32","issue":"Fixed finite-difference step and relative-error thresholds are presented as universal rules.","correction":"They are heuristics. Account for dtype, magnitude, near-zero derivatives, cancellation and nonsmooth branch changes."},{"source":"Lecture 5 slide 24","issue":"Monitoring test accuracy can be misread as authorization to tune repeatedly on the final test set.","correction":"Use validation performance for selection; reserve test assessment after choices are fixed."},{"source":"Lecture 5 slide 38","issue":"The small course experiment can be misread as a universal architecture ranking.","correction":"Treat its accuracy figures as specific to the reported subset, CPU budget and short training procedure."}],"conventions":["Column-vector Wx unless a row-major batch is explicitly stated.","Natural logarithms for cross-entropy.","Summed multiclass hinge loss over incorrect classes, then mean over examples, unless stated otherwise.","Numeric gradient questions state full-squared versus half-squared loss.","CNN operation is cross-correlation unless kernel flipping is explicitly stated.","CNN size questions use floor sizing unless exact tiling is explicitly stated.","Parameter counts include biases only when specified."]};
